<h4 id=3D"human-dignity-in-the-new-industrial-revolution">Human dignity in =
the new Industrial Revolution</h4>
<p>The Holy Father formerly known as <strong>Richard Prevost</strong> took =
the name =E2=80=9CLeo=E2=80=9D for a reason. In 1891, the previous Pope Leo=
, <strong>Leo XIII</strong>, wrote <em><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/email=
s/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/ey=
JzdWJqZWN0IjoiQWZ0ZXIg4oCYQWZ0ZXIgQXV0b21hdGlvbuKAmSIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyNzYsI=
nBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3d3dy52YXRpY2FuLnZhL2NvbnRlbnQv=
bGVvLXhpaWkvZW4vZW5jeWNsaWNhbHMvZG9jdW1lbnRzL2hmX2wteGlpaV9lbmNfMTUwNTE4OTF=
fcmVydW0tbm92YXJ1bS5odG1sIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjMyfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener nore=
ferrer" target=3D"_blank">Rerum Novarum</a></u></em>, the letter where the =
Church took the side of workers against industrial capital. His indictment:=
 The wealth made by the many had pooled in the hands of a few, leaving work=
ers with =E2=80=9Ca yoke little better than that of slavery itself.=E2=80=
=9D The indictment came with a policy agenda: a living wage, humane hours, =
rest, limits on child and exhausting labor, the right of workers to form un=
ions and mutual-aid societies, and a state willing to step in when the poor=
 were crushed by market power.</p>
<p>Our present Leo signed <em>Magnifica Humanitas</em> on the 135th anniver=
sary of the previous Leo=E2=80=99s letter. Translation: AI is the new facto=
ry, and the Church means to do for the large language model what it once tr=
ied to do for the assembly line. The present policy agenda: Regulate data a=
s a shared good; make algorithmic decisions transparent, contestable, and a=
ccountable; design workplace systems around human dignity rather than machi=
ne-speed productivity; invest in retraining and access; use taxation, socia=
l protection, and industrial policy to spread the gains; protect children f=
rom extractive platforms; and keep lethal decisions out of automated hands.=
 </p>
<p>A key part of the argument in <em>Magnifica Humanitas </em>is built on a=
 philosophical principle older than capitalism: the <em>universal destinati=
on of goods</em>. It=E2=80=99s the idea, developed in Catholic teaching fro=
m Aquinas forward, that the world=E2=80=99s resources are intended for ever=
yone, and private ownership is a stewardship arrangement rather than carte =
blanche. Bible readers will recognize the spirit of it in Acts: The first f=
ollowers of Jesus =E2=80=9Chad all things in common,=E2=80=9D selling what =
they owned and giving =E2=80=9Cto each as any had need=E2=80=9D (Acts 2:44=
=E2=80=9345 NRSVUE)=E2=80=94a line that would echo, centuries later, throug=
h everyone=E2=80=99s favorite, non-divisive German philosopher <strong>Karl=
 Marx</strong>. Leo XIV updates it for the era of the data center. He exten=
ds =E2=80=9Cgoods=E2=80=9D to include =E2=80=9Cpatents, algorithms, digital=
 platforms, technological infrastructure and data,=E2=80=9D and warns that =
when those stay =E2=80=9Cconcentrated in the hands of a few,=E2=80=9D the r=
esult is =E2=80=9Ca new imbalance=E2=80=9D (=C2=B667).</p>
<p>The models you hand your work to were trained on the collective writing =
of everyone who ever put words down=E2=80=94yours and mine included. We=E2=
=80=99ve built the material underlying this technology collectively. But ac=
cording to Leo XIV, the value is being disproportionately captured by =E2=
=80=9Cprivate, often transnational, parties=E2=80=9D whose resources =E2=80=
=9Csurpass those of many Governments=E2=80=9D (=C2=B65). A pope is describi=
ng the means of production=E2=80=94and the fact that the people whose livel=
ihoods now run on them don=E2=80=99t own a share.</p>
<h4 id=3D"a-pope-and-a-ceo-walk-into-a-discourse">A Pope and a CEO walk int=
o a discourse</h4>
<p>Dan=E2=80=99s focus in <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f9=
5455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0Ijo=
iQWZ0ZXIg4oCYQWZ0ZXIgQXV0b21hdGlvbuKAmSIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyNzYsInBvc3RfdHlwZS=
I6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3AvYWZ0ZXItYXV0b21hdGlvbiIsInBvc=
2l0aW9uIjozM30=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">=E2=80=9CA=
fter Automation=E2=80=9D</a></u> is mostly on the individual. What can <em>=
I</em> do to stay ahead and make the most of AI progress? Answer: Become th=
e framer=E2=80=94the person in charge of deciding what=E2=80=99s worth doin=
g, and why. His Holiness takes the collective view, and reading their persp=
ectives together is what makes Dan=E2=80=99s piece feel both right and inco=
mplete at once.</p>
<p>Becoming the framer is the correct individual strategy. It=E2=80=99s als=
o a move that only pays off if you=E2=80=99re positioned to make it=E2=80=
=94with savings to play with, time to learn to use the tool well, and somew=
here soft to land if you leap. I had all three when I was first experimenti=
ng with AI. The same model, handed to a single mother working two jobs to p=
ay for childcare, won=E2=80=99t have the same effect. Access to AI multipli=
es what you already have, and the machine doing the multiplying still belon=
gs to someone else.</p>
<h4 id=3D"what-you-can-do">What you can do</h4>
<p>Leo=E2=80=99s question doesn=E2=80=99t resolve into action items, but th=
ere are a few moves available to anyone who works in or around AI.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Know what (and who) you=E2=80=99re depending on. </strong>Start wit=
h your own tools. List the models, agents, APIs, and platforms that sit bet=
ween you and your work. Ask what happens if the price changes, access disap=
pears, terms shift, or your data gets locked in. Keep the parts of your wor=
k that create lasting value=E2=80=94notes, prompts, workflows, client conte=
xt, and taste=E2=80=94in places you control.</li>
<li>
<strong>Bring ownership and governance into decisions you already touch</st=
rong>. When a team pilots a tool, ask about more than time saved. Ask who b=
enefits from that saved time, whose work changes, what needs human review, =
and what should not be automated. Put those questions into kickoff docs, ve=
ndor decisions, retros, and performance reviews.</li>
<li>
<strong>Use your position to set the standard. </strong>If you are reading =
this, you are on the first wave of AI adoption, whether it feels that way o=
r not. You are testing tools, designing workflows, advising clients, and mo=
deling what =E2=80=9Cgood AI use=E2=80=9D looks like. Take that responsibil=
ity seriously. The standard we set now is the baseline for everyone else wh=
o comes after. </li>
</ul>
<p>AI has given me a working life I love, on loan from a commons everyone b=
uilt and a few companies own. Dan=E2=80=99s question I can answer by myself=
, which is what makes it comfortable. Leo=E2=80=99s I can=E2=80=99t answer =
alone, and neither can you. What we can do is stop seeing our own good luck=
 as proof the system is fair, and keep the big question on the table: Who o=
wns the machine that makes my work valuable, and at what cost? </p>
