PLATFORM STRUCTURE: LOCK-IN =
=E2=86=94 COMMODITIZED

The next axis concerns the tools and infrastructure=
 on which you are building your business.

Ask yourself: Are you locked =
into one AI provider or can you swap them without breaking things?

If you=E2=80=99re building on one provider=E2=80=99s ecosystem, know that =
you=E2=80=99re making this bet. You might be optimizing for their APIs, =
using their fine-tuning infrastructure, or depending on capabilities unique=
 to their models=E2=80=94like OpenAI=E2=80=99s interface for apps to call =
their models in code, Anthropic=E2=80=99s ability to control a computer =
like a human would, or Gemini=E2=80=99s web search integration. The more =
you do that, the harder it becomes to switch providers without rebuilding =
significant parts of your product. That=E2=80=99s not inherently wrong. =
Amazon Web Services (AWS) lock-in has been fine for most companies. Have a =
view on whether that analogy can apply in your case.

If what you=E2=80=99re building doesn=E2=80=99t depend on one =
provider=E2=80=99s ecosystem, you=E2=80=99re counting on model choice =
mattering less over time. You might be building multi-model routing, or =
tools that work across providers=E2=80=94like OpenRouter, LiteLLM, or =
Vercel=E2=80=99s AI Gateway. You=E2=80=99re betting that switching costs =
will stay low and that what sets you apart lives in your product, not the =
underlying model. The analogy here is Postgres=E2=80=94open-source =
relational database technology that lets users store and query structured =
data. There are now dozens of managed Postgres providers competing on =
execution rather than the core technology.

Both can be right. The answer =
might differ by use case=E2=80=94if you need the best possible model for a =
high-stakes task, you=E2=80=99ll probably anchor to one frontier provider. =
If you need something good enough to summarize a document, you=E2=80=99ll =
pick the cheapest tool that week. Concentration in AI infrastructure =
suggests one or two providers will eventually dominate, but that =
consolidation is years away, not months. You don=E2=80=99t have to lock =
into OpenAI or Anthropic today if you can stay flexible for the next few =
years while open-weight models like Llama, DeepSeek, and Qwen are still =
viable alternatives.

Betting on one provider isn=E2=80=99t wrong right now=
. But we like founders who have thought about how they=E2=80=99d diversify =
if necessary, even if they=E2=80=99re not planning to today.

TRUST AND GOVERNANCE: PERMISSIVE =E2=86=94 CONSTRAINED

This axis concerns the rules and compliance norms under which you operate. =
Unlike the others, it depends less on technology itself, and more on =
regulators, enterprise buyers, and public reaction.

Ask yourself: Do you move fast now and deal with governance later, betting =
the rules stay permissive? Or do you invest in audit trails and =
certifications upfront, betting compliance only gets stricter?

If you=E2=80=99re moving fast and treating governance as a future problem, =
you=E2=80=99re betting regulation stays light. That=E2=80=99s been a =
reasonable bet in the U.S. market so far. It may continue to be reasonable.

If you=E2=80=99re investing early in audit trails, explainability =
infrastructure, and standards like NIST AI RMF and ISO =
42001=E2=80=94frameworks for managing AI risk=E2=80=94along with model =
cards that document a model=E2=80=99s training data, limitations, and =
intended use, you=E2=80=99re betting the environment tightens. =
You=E2=80=99re accepting slower movement now to be ready when compliance =
stops being optional.

Both have opportunity costs. The fast-mover gives up=
 positioning if governance requirements suddenly become important. The =
early-compliance investor gives up speed if the permissive environment =
continues.

This axis is harder to predict than the others. A major AI =
failure with undeniable public harm=E2=80=94medical misdiagnosis at scale, =
infrastructure disruption, something with casualties=E2=80=94would trigger =
legislative response faster than a roadmap can adapt. Labor displacement =
could bring unexpected regulation if it catches enough political fire. Or =
you could have something like what happened https://every.=
