PUTTING THE FRAMEWORK INTO PRACTICE

For each axis, ask yourself four =
questions.

 * What is our implicit bet? Most of us haven=E2=80=99t =
articulated this clearly, even to ourselves. Writing it down forces =
precision. Think of it as an extension of the classic =E2=80=9CWhy =
now=E2=80=9D slide in a pitch deck=E2=80=94explaining why the timing is =
right and which version of the future you=E2=80=99re building toward.
 * What would have to be true for that bet to pay off? If you=E2=80=99re =
betting on token abundance improving your margins, what has to be true =
about the cost curve, about your usage patterns, about competitive dynamics=
? If you=E2=80=99re betting on scaffolding remaining valuable, what has to =
be true about the pace of model capability improvement?
 * What signals would tell us we=E2=80=99re wrong? This is where most =
strategic planning fails. If you can=E2=80=99t name the signals that would =
cause you to think you were making the wrong bet, you=E2=80=99re not really=
 stress-testing your strategy.
 * How quickly could we adapt if we are =
wrong? Some companies can pivot. Others have made commitments=E2=80=94techn=
ical, organizational, contractual=E2=80=94that lock them into a fixed =
position. Neither is inherently better, but you should know which kind of =
company you are.

As business strategist Hamilton Helmer argued in 7 =
Powers: The Foundations of Business Strategy, stronger moats survive shifts=
 in the market. If your competitive advantage depends on one specific cost =
or regulatory assumption holding, you=E2=80=99re exposed. But if your =
advantage works across multiple possible futures=E2=80=94if you=E2=80=99re =
defensible whether tokens get cheaper or stay expensive, whether regulation=
 stays light or tightens=E2=80=94you=E2=80=99re in a durable position.

That same logic applies to how you size your bets: High-conviction founders=
 often win precisely because they make concentrated bets while others hedge=
, but they are aware of this choice.

