<h4 id=3D"token-economics-scarce-abundant">Token economics: Scarce =E2=86=
=94 abundant</h4>
<p>If you=E2=80=99re a token consumer=E2=80=94which most AI startups are=E2=
=80=94abundance is good news, and right now we=E2=80=99re in an era of frag=
ile token abundance. Your margins improve. You can do more, experiment more=
 freely, and build features that would have been prohibitively expensive a =
year ago. Cursor and its ilk exist as a category because the cost of runnin=
g AI got cheap enough for products to run constantly in the background and =
still make money.</p>
<p>But abundance is a trap: If everyone can build what you=E2=80=99re build=
ing for pennies, what=E2=80=99s your advantage? As a founder, you need to k=
now whether you=E2=80=99re betting on costs rising enough to be a barrier=
=E2=80=94or whether you have a defensible advantage that holds even if they=
 don=E2=80=99t.  </p>
<p><strong>Ask yourself: Does the cost of running AI become high enough to =
constrain you, or fall toward zero?</strong></p>
<p><em>If you=E2=80=99re betting on scarcity,</em> <em>you need to articula=
te why falling token costs won=E2=80=99t destroy your advantage. </em>One e=
xample: applications where agents run constantly, not just on demand=E2=80=
=94at that volume, even cheap tokens add up, so squeezing cost out of every=
 call becomes difficult. Another is latency-sensitive work, like voice agen=
ts built on <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Fe=
mails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiWW91ciBBSSBTdHJhdGVneSBJcyBNYWtpbmcgQmV0cy4gRG8gWW9=
1IEtub3cgV2hpY2ggT25lcz8gIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDMyNCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsIn=
VybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vdmFwaS5haS8iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTB9/1/0100019f197f77fc-7e38a=
e12-3145-4476-aa59-878b0c685637-000000/P6OKuNnOXXf_IbuQMU8zGs95QzrwbGWvI-mJ=
mD8VgcM=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Vapi</a></u>, =
where any delay makes the conversation feel broken. There, you=E2=80=99re s=
tuck paying for the fastest models even as other models get cheaper.</p>
<p><em>If you=E2=80=99re betting on abundance continuing, </em>y<em>ou need=
 to build an advantage with one of the following TK: proprietary data, doma=
in expertise, distribution, or knowing what to build. </em>We believe that =
tokens will keep getting cheaper. But we see value in companies that reduce=
 cost variance=E2=80=94making spend predictable and insulating customers fr=
om volatility. A startup that helps customers cap their maximum bills is mo=
re defensible than one competing on average price. We=E2=80=99ve seen it in=
 healthcare: Companies processing huge volumes of data still need to manage=
 their worst-case costs and avoid expensive reprocessing of documents, tran=
scripts, and session logs. Even as per-token prices fall, being able to tel=
l a client =E2=80=9CYour bill won=E2=80=99t exceed X=E2=80=9D is valuable. =
</p>
<h4 id=3D"model-self-sufficiency-needs-scaffolding-handles-natively">Model =
self-sufficiency: Needs scaffolding =E2=86=94 handles natively</h4>
<p>This axis determines the fate of what we=E2=80=99d broadly call =E2=80=
=9Cmodel wrapper=E2=80=9D companies. If you=E2=80=99re augmenting what mode=
ls can do=E2=80=94adding memory, improving retrieval, orchestrating multi-s=
tep workflows=E2=80=94you=E2=80=99re implicitly wagering that the model won=
=E2=80=99t one day be able to do that itself. That=E2=80=99s a bet with an =
expiration date. Maybe a good bet, maybe not, but you should know you=E2=80=
=99re making it. Harvey, an AI platform for law firms, and Sierra, a custom=
er service AI, are both counting on a general model not being able to do wh=
at they do in their niches.</p>
<p><strong>Ask yourself: Would my product still be needed if the model had =
unlimited capabilities?</strong> </p>
<p><em>If the answer is yes, you=E2=80=99re building integration. </em>Comp=
anies whose primary value is integration=E2=80=94connecting models to messy=
 external systems=E2=80=94are competitive regardless of how capable the mod=
els become. A company that brings AI into healthcare workflows, say, sits o=
n a more defensible part of the axis. Any model can reason over medical inf=
ormation; what=E2=80=99s defensible is that the product integrates with the=
 systems of record, document flows, and operational constraints that health=
care already runs on. </p>
<p><em>If the answer is no, you=E2=80=99re building scaffolding. </em>Compa=
nies whose primary value is making models smarter face absorption risk=E2=
=80=94what they offer today is augmenting what current models can do. A com=
pany doing AI-assisted code review may be building something that customers=
 need right now, but as models get better at catching errors and understand=
ing larger repositories, the company has to be clear about what remains dif=
ferentiated outside the model. </p>
<p>Some capabilities have already been absorbed by models=E2=80=94context w=
indows have expanded dramatically, reducing the need to break documents int=
o small chunks for retrieval in many use cases, and we think models will ke=
ep getting more capable. </p>
<p>Other capabilities look stickier for now: routing between providers with=
 different strengths or costs, and compliance in regulated industries. A ca=
pable model can learn the regulations, but it can=E2=80=99t be trusted to c=
heck its own work. Financial model risk management takes this tension into =
account: The Federal Reserve has told banking organizations that model risk=
 management should include =E2=80=9C<u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/=
https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619ea=
fce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiWW91ciBBSSBTdHJhdGVneSBJcyB=
NYWtpbmcgQmV0cy4gRG8gWW91IEtub3cgV2hpY2ggT25lcz8gIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDMyNCwicG=
9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3LmZlZGVyYWxyZXNlcnZlLmdvdi9zd=
XBlcnZpc2lvbnJlZy9zcmxldHRlcnMvU1IyNjAyLnBkZiIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMX0=3D/1/010=
0019f197f77fc-7e38ae12-3145-4476-aa59-878b0c685637-000000/J-A5ISy_96RMSXF0_=
BER3QX7SwfIltI4brIodVFm1UU=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_bl=
ank">effective challenge</a></u>,=E2=80=9D review by parties independent of=
 the model=E2=80=99s builders. Regulated healthcare has the same structure.=
 When something goes wrong, a regulator or enterprise needs an accountable =
party to point to, and =E2=80=9Cthe model judged itself compliant=E2=80=9D =
isn=E2=80=99t one. </p>
<h4 id=3D"platform-structure-lock-in-commoditized">Platform structure: Lock=
-in =E2=86=94 commoditized</h4>
<p>The next axis concerns the tools and infrastructure on which you are bui=
lding your business. </p>
<p><strong>Ask yourself: Are you locked into one AI provider or can you swa=
p them without breaking things? </strong></p>
<p><em>If you=E2=80=99re building on one provider=E2=80=99s ecosystem, know=
 that you=E2=80=99re making this bet. </em>You might be optimizing for thei=
r APIs, using their fine-tuning infrastructure, or depending on capabilitie=
s unique to their models=E2=80=94like OpenAI=E2=80=99s interface for apps t=
o call their models in code, Anthropic=E2=80=99s ability to control a compu=
ter like a human would, or Gemini=E2=80=99s web search integration. The mor=
e you do that, the harder it becomes to switch providers without rebuilding=
 significant parts of your product. That=E2=80=99s not inherently wrong. Am=
azon Web Services (AWS) lock-in has been fine for most companies. Have a vi=
ew on whether that analogy can apply in your case.</p>
<p><em>If what you=E2=80=99re building doesn=E2=80=99t depend on one provid=
er=E2=80=99s ecosystem,</em> <em>you=E2=80=99re counting on model choice ma=
ttering less over time.</em> You might be building multi-model routing, or =
tools that work across providers=E2=80=94like  OpenRouter, LiteLLM, or Verc=
el=E2=80=99s AI Gateway. You=E2=80=99re betting that switching costs will s=
tay low and that what sets you apart lives in your product, not the underly=
ing model. The analogy here is Postgres=E2=80=94open-source relational data=
base technology that lets users store and query structured data. There are =
now dozens of managed Postgres providers competing on execution rather than=
 the core technology. </p>
<p>Both can be right. The answer might differ by use case=E2=80=94if you ne=
ed the best possible model for a high-stakes task, you=E2=80=99ll probably =
anchor to one frontier provider. If you need something good enough to summa=
rize a document, you=E2=80=99ll pick the cheapest tool that week. Concentra=
tion in AI infrastructure suggests one or two providers will eventually dom=
inate,  but that consolidation is years away, not months. You don=E2=80=99t=
 have to lock into OpenAI or Anthropic today if you can stay flexible for t=
he next few years while open-weight models like Llama, DeepSeek, and Qwen a=
re still viable alternatives. </p>
<p>Betting on one provider isn=E2=80=99t wrong right now. But we like found=
ers who have thought about how they=E2=80=99d diversify if necessary, even =
if they=E2=80=99re not planning to today. </p>
<h4 id=3D"trust-and-governance-permissive-constrained">Trust and governance=
: Permissive =E2=86=94 constrained</h4>
<p>This axis concerns the rules and compliance norms under which you operat=
e. Unlike the others, it depends less on technology itself, and more on reg=
ulators, enterprise buyers, and public reaction. </p>
<p><strong>Ask yourself: Do you move fast now and deal with governance late=
r, betting the rules stay permissive? Or do you invest in audit trails and =
certifications upfront, betting compliance only gets stricter? </strong></p=
>
<p><em>If you=E2=80=99re moving fast and treating governance as a future pr=
oblem</em>, <em>you=E2=80=99re betting regulation stays light. </em>That=E2=
=80=99s been a reasonable bet in the U.S. market so far. It may continue to=
 be reasonable. </p>
<p><em>If you=E2=80=99re investing early in audit trails, explainability in=
frastructure, and standards like NIST AI RMF and ISO 42001=E2=80=94framewor=
ks for managing AI risk=E2=80=94along with model cards that document a mode=
l=E2=80=99s training data, limitations, and intended use, you=E2=80=99re be=
tting the environment tightens. </em>You=E2=80=99re accepting slower moveme=
nt now to be ready when compliance stops being optional.</p>
<p>Both have opportunity costs. The fast-mover gives up positioning if gove=
rnance requirements suddenly become important. The early-compliance investo=
r gives up speed if the permissive environment continues. </p>
<p>This axis is harder to predict than the others. A major AI failure with =
undeniable public harm=E2=80=94medical misdiagnosis at scale, infrastructur=
e disruption, something with casualties=E2=80=94would trigger legislative r=
esponse faster than a roadmap can adapt. Labor displacement could bring une=
xpected regulation if it catches enough political fire. Or you could have s=
omething like <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2=
Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18=
