referrer" target=3D"_blank">model cards</a></u>, every vendor that wants to=
 sell to them has to comply, and then those vendors=E2=80=99 other customer=
s start expecting the same thing. This creates de facto regulation without =
legislation. It moves faster than the government and is harder to predict.<=
/p>
<p>The trajectory of the SOC2 security compliance standard is one precedent=
. Once large enterprises started requiring SOC2 from their vendors, it casc=
aded through the procurement chain until any B2B SaaS company needed it to =
win enterprise deals=E2=80=94even early-stage startups. Automated complianc=
e company Vanta built a business on that wave. Whether AI compliance follow=
s the same path is an open question.</p>
<h2 id=3D"putting-the-framework-into-practice"><strong>Putting the framewor=
k into practice </strong></h2>
<p>For each axis, ask yourself four questions.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>What is our implicit bet?</strong> Most of us haven=E2=80=99t artic=
ulated this clearly, even to ourselves. Writing it down forces precision. T=
hink of it as an extension of the classic =E2=80=9CWhy now=E2=80=9D slide i=
n a pitch deck=E2=80=94explaining why the timing is right and which version=
 of the future you=E2=80=99re building toward.</li>
<li>
<strong>What would have to be true for that bet to pay off?</strong> If you=
=E2=80=99re betting on token abundance improving your margins, what has to =
be true about the cost curve, about your usage patterns, about competitive =
dynamics? If you=E2=80=99re betting on scaffolding remaining valuable, what=
 has to be true about the pace of model capability improvement?</li>
<li>
<strong>What signals would tell us we=E2=80=99re wrong?</strong> This is wh=
ere most strategic planning fails. If you can=E2=80=99t name the signals th=
at would cause you to think you were making the wrong bet, you=E2=80=99re n=
ot really stress-testing your strategy.</li>
<li>
<strong>How quickly could we adapt if we are wrong?</strong> Some companies=
 can pivot. Others have made commitments=E2=80=94technical, organizational,=
 contractual=E2=80=94that lock them into a fixed position. Neither is inher=
ently better, but you should know which kind of company you are.</li>
</ul>
<p>As business strategist <strong>Hamilton Helmer </strong>argued in <em>7 =
Powers</em>: <em>The Foundations of Business Strategy</em>, stronger moats =
survive shifts in the market. If your competitive advantage depends on one =
specific cost or regulatory assumption holding, you=E2=80=99re exposed. But=
 if your advantage works across multiple possible futures=E2=80=94if you=E2=
=80=99re defensible whether tokens get cheaper or stay expensive, whether r=
egulation stays light or tightens=E2=80=94you=E2=80=99re in a durable posit=
ion.</p>
<p>That same logic applies to how you size your bets: High-conviction found=
ers often win precisely because they make concentrated bets while others he=
dge, but they are aware of this choice. </p>
<p>Some bets can flip in a day. Intel=E2=80=99s former CEO <strong>Andy Gro=
ve</strong> called these <a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fev=
