<p>Here are the highlights:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>The =E2=80=9CSaaSpocalypse=E2=80=9D narrative has it backwards. </s=
trong>AI agents turn anyone into a vibe coder, kicking off investor panic t=
hat traditional software-as-a-service (SaaS) companies like Figma would cea=
se to justify their cost. Colyer isn=E2=80=99t worried: AI has exponentiall=
y expanded the developer base, while underscoring how difficult it is to cr=
eate a vibe coded version of Figma that works as well or as reliably as the=
 real thing. He=E2=80=99s vibe coded multiple agents to do stuff like handl=
e his emails, but the maintenance costs piled up quickly and never seemed w=
orth it. =E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99m buying more software these days than I ever d=
id before,=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9C=E2=80=98I=E2=80=99m just go=
ing to pay somebody else to run my agent for me.=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>
<strong>Figma is embracing agents. </strong>The company has launched an MCP=
 server=E2=80=94a standardized interface any AI tool can plug into=E2=80=94=
that allows you to approach design work from two directions. =E2=80=9CCode =
to design=E2=80=9D takes a live web page and reconstructs it on the Figma c=
anvas, so you can manipulate the elements directly; meanwhile, =E2=80=9Cdes=
ign to code=E2=80=9D flips the process by packaging a Figma design and givi=
ng it to an agent, which makes changes for you via pull request. </li>
<li>
<strong>There=E2=80=99s a ceiling to chat-based generative design. </strong=
>Great design hinges on a diamond-shaped process: First you diverge, or gen=
erate lots of ideas, and only then do you converge around the most promisin=
g options. Text-based chats are inherently linear and therefore bad at dive=
rgence; the setup forces you to <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/http=
s:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce6=
87adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiT3B1cyA0LjggSXMgU21hcnQgRW5vdWd=
oIHRvIEdldCBpbiBZb3VyIFdheSIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyODUsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLC=
J1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL2NvbnRleHQtd2luZG93L21pbmktdmliZS1jaGVjay1jb=
GF1ZGUtZGVzaWduIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE1fQ=3D=3D/1/0100019e8ed92d1e-0f223a78-807=
c-471b-884f-fe82ba4b3bbc-000000/Z8vQb54iN2BXHocndlwn8hFhwwqTsVyfhy5W0e0KayA=
=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">select an option</a><=
/u> and iterate on it. Agents are already good at the task-completion workf=
lows Figma supports today, but the divergent, exploratory part of design re=
mains unsolved across the industry. Colyer is interested in dividing the pr=
ocess so specialized agents handle the divergence by pushing you to expand =
your thinking, while another set filters through the options to identify a =
single path forward. =E2=80=9CEven the best agents, the command-line agents=
, don=E2=80=99t have the ability to do those workflows,=E2=80=9D he says. =
=E2=80=9CThat=E2=80=99s where I see the future of design and product thinki=
ng.=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>
<strong>Agents can produce so much so quickly. </strong>They=E2=80=99re les=
s good at determining whether any of it meets a company=E2=80=99s values or=
 design standards. Colyer isn=E2=80=99t sure the best way to close this gap=
=E2=80=94maybe it=E2=80=99s a video walkthrough, a screenshot, or a trusted=
 review agent=E2=80=94but for good design to scale, AI needs to play a larg=
er role in evaluations.</li>
</ul>
