Mike: Like the model router in GitHub Copilot?

Kyle Daigle: Exactly. GitHub has an automatic model router with task intent=
, and Microsoft Foundry has a model router at the API level. The more we =
can let people tell us where their bars are, such as =E2=80=9CThis is an =
incredibly hard problem and I am willing to go all the way to the top,=
=E2=80=9D or, =E2=80=9CI want to stay here,=E2=80=9D the more we can help =
choose the model.

Tokens are often expensive because people choose the =
model of the day, week, or hour, and those models can be expensive. But a =
train of thought moves between hard problems and simple ones. I might ask =
an agent to do an enormous amount of work, then have a final step that is =
just changing names. I probably will not manually switch from an expensive =
model down to a smaller one just to save tokens for that step, but the =
tools could. That will help enterprises, individual developers, and people =
building automations with the Copilot SDK.

Simulation note: The AI got the=
 gist of the problem correct, but it couldn=E2=80=99t solve it. It gave a =
framing for the problem, noting that a model doing more work =
doesn=E2=80=99t automatically mean a cheaper bill, while the real Kyle =
talked about a thing to solve the problem: the automatic model router


12. UNUSUAL PERSONAL USES FOR AGENTS

Mike: I made an AI version of you to =
practice this interview and found it immensely useful. What other unusual =
things are you seeing people do with agents internally or externally?

Kyle Daigle: I do something similar. I have one setup through the app and =
another Claude instance that cannot talk to work systems, so there is =
separation of state. I spend a lot of time having it read what I write. =
This interview will ultimately get fed into it. Every day I receive a =
communications report that says things like, =E2=80=9CKyle, you keep saying=
 this,=E2=80=9D or, =E2=80=9CThis is not super clear.=E2=80=9D Because I =
write and speak in a particular way and like to use metaphors, it gives me =
examples of metaphors that are clear.

The self-improvement loop for a =
human is incredibly powerful. We used to talk about this with Hubot and =
ChatOps at GitHub: Humans are much more willing to take critical feedback =
from robots than from other humans. When my Claude instance tells me how =
poorly I did at something, I feel better asking it to explain why and using=
 that feedback when I write emails, write a script, or review details.

A lot of my agent loop is about me rather than software. It looks backward:=
 read Kyle=E2=80=99s emails and Slack messages from the last seven days, =
give feedback, then look back at the advice and check whether Kyle acted on=
 it. That loop is extremely powerful. It is the kind of personal consumer =
AI experience I want.

Simulation note: Funny that the question about =
simulated interviews was also a miss. Asked to name unusual agent uses, the=
 AI Kyle admitted it didn=E2=80=99t have much to offer and fell back on =
vague talk about removing toil. The real Kyle=E2=80=99s answer revealed a =
concrete, personal workflow that=E2=80=99s truly interesting and helpful.


THE BEST INTERVIEW PREPARATION IS KNOWING WHERE TO DIG DEEPER

Every managing editor Eleanor Warnock https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiSSBJbnRlcnZpZXdlZCBhbiBBSSBWZXJzaW9uIG9mIEdpdEh1YuKAm=
XMgQ09P4oCUVGhlbiBTcG9rZSB0byB0aGUgUmVhbCBPbmUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzAyLCJwb3N0=
X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9AZWxlYW5vcl9iMDM0NzRfMSI=
sInBvc2l0aW9uIjo4fQ=3D=3D wrote recently about what she calls =
Socrates-as-a-service https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e6=
54f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiSSBJbnRlcnZpZXdlZ=
CBhbiBBSSBWZXJzaW9uIG9mIEdpdEh1YuKAmXMgQ09P4oCUVGhlbiBTcG9rZSB0byB0aGUgUmVh=
bCBPbmUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzAyLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9=
ldmVyeS50by9wL3NvY3JhdGVzLWFzLWEtc2VydmljZSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo5fQ=3D=3D: the =
human skill of pulling out ideas people haven=E2=80=99t yet put into words,=
 like the anecdote that becomes a front-page story or the detail that =
crystallizes a philosophy into something a reader remembers.

That=E2=80=99s exactly the gap this experiment helped me see. The =
simulation knew about Kyle=E2=80=99s positions on developer choice and =
walled gardens because he=E2=80=99s made them clear in public for years. It=
 couldn=E2=80=99t know that he codes on Saturdays and rotates between three=
 machines to stay honest about other people=E2=80=99s tools.

In response to a post-real interview request for comment about the =
experiment, Daigle said, =E2=80=9CI thought the simulated interview was =
pretty good! Mostly, it overindexed on my written work rather than my =
spoken interviews and podcasts. Without access to everything that =
I=E2=80=99ve written internally, it went harder on topics I=E2=80=99ve =
spoken about on my blog, etc. than I normally speak about.=E2=80=9D

This is the real argument for doing an AI-simulated test run before an =
interview. It will show you where the gaps in the public record are, so you=
 can spend the interview filling in those gaps and extracting truly =
original, scarce knowledge for your readers and the world.

Daigle found a use for my answers, too. =E2=80=9CEven reading the AI =
responses, I found it clarifying my thinking and the sharpness of my =
answers, so it helped me, too.=E2=80=9D

Then he added: =E2=80=9CI actually=
 did a similar thing for Mike. What questions I expected him to ask. I =
didn=E2=80=99t save the output=E2=80=94I garbage-collect a lot of =
it=E2=80=94so it=E2=80=99s funny how we=E2=80=99re all operating.=E2=80=9D

Maybe the joke was on me all along.



