<h2 id=3D"alignment">Alignment</h2>
<p><strong>The worried well</strong>. I=E2=80=99m a Midjourney fanboy. I pa=
y for it, I defend it at dinner against people who think AI can=E2=80=99t m=
ake art, and I think it makes the most beautiful images on the internet. So=
 I am the wrong person to ask whether the AI image generator=E2=80=99s next=
 idea is a good one, but given the fever pitch discussions on X last week a=
bout its latest venture, I=E2=80=99m going to try. </p>
<p>Midjourney wants to lower you into a warm pool ringed with half a millio=
n ultrasound sensors that map your whole body in 60 seconds without using r=
adiation or the magnets commonly used in big and clunky, and sometimes scar=
y, CT and MRI scans. </p>
<p>The company=E2=80=99s aim is to change imaging from being a dramatic eve=
nt requiring a hospital visit and make it ambient and cheap, the way a bloo=
d test is. This is the kind of catch-it-early screening tool that sets the =
longevity crowd salivating=E2=80=94a path to preventing disease and, dare I=
 say, living forever.</p>
<p>Doctors, however, are right to push back on the underlying premise that =
healthy people should be getting scanned at all. The trouble with scanning =
healthy people more often is that you find weird little things that may mea=
n nothing but can=E2=80=99t be ignored. These are called incidentalomas, an=
d while they=E2=80=99re manageable when patient volume is low, they=E2=80=
=99re not when these scans are done at a mega-scale. Midjourney=E2=80=99s s=
tated ambition is to do 1 billion scans a month. If you point that many hea=
lthy, anxious people at a health system already buckling under demand, you =
don=E2=80=99t catch disease early so much as manufacture a tidal wave of fo=
llow-up scans, referrals, biopsies, and clinics clogged with people who wer=
e perfectly well until someone at a medical spa=E2=80=94where the scans are=
 launching first=E2=80=94told them otherwise. </p>
<p>But it=E2=80=99s a charged topic, and I understand why. If a scan catche=
s the lump that turns out to be cancer, that is worth every false alarm in =
the world to the person it happened to=E2=80=94and the testimonies you hear=
 are always from that person. You never hear from the thousands who got a b=
iopsy and two weeks of anxiety-inducing dread over something that turned ou=
t to be nothing. </p>
<p>None of this is meant to take away from what Midjourney has built. The s=
can gives a detailed map of fat, visceral fat, liver fat, and lean mass tha=
t can be tracked over time=E2=80=94very useful for GLP-1 users, who=E2=80=
=99re increasingly fixated on their fat mass to lean mass ratio.</p>
<p>So would I do it? For body composition, gladly. To go hunting for what m=
ight be hiding inside me, no, because I=E2=80=99ve seen where that road lea=
ds, and it is paved with prickly biopsies. The worried well don=E2=80=99t n=
eed another reason to worry; they do, however, need to know what they=E2=80=
=99re made of, and on that, the scan delivers.=E2=80=94<em><u><a href=3D"ht=
tps://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05=
