THE MEMORY PROBLEM

OpenClaw=E2=80=99s memory system is =
elaborate. It=E2=80=99s built to remember details across conversations so =
the agent doesn=E2=80=99t start from scratch every time you message it. To =
do that, it runs on:

 * Eight separate bootstrap files, the files that it =
writes when you first start it up, each storing a different kind of =
information about you, including AGENTS.md, SOUL.md, TOOLS.md, IDENTITY.md,=
 USER.md, HEARTBEAT.md, BOOTSTRAP.md, and MEMORY.md https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgSXMgdGhlIE9wZW5DbGF3IEFsdGVybmF0aXZlI=
FlvdSBBbHJlYWR5IEhhdmUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzIxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJs=
IjoiaHR0cDovL21lbW9yeS5tZCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoyMn0=3D
 * A memory =
consolidation =E2=80=9Cdreaming=E2=80=9D process, where it reviews =
everything that happened during the day and decides which scraps are worth =
keeping and which to forget
 * Multiple ways to search
 * A storage layer that organizes what it knows, with evidence attached to =
each claim, like a personal Wikipedia

As an engineer, I can appreciate why=
 they might have built it this way, but as a user, this sophistication has =
a cost.

When OpenClaw makes a mistake=E2=80=94forgets something important =
or acts on stale information=E2=80=94the answer could lie in any of a =
half-dozen places: the bootstrap files, the daily notes, the dreaming =
process, a condensed version of older notes, what=E2=80=99s saved when the =
system clears its short-term memory, or the encyclopedic storage layer. =
Often, there=E2=80=99s no way to tell which, which makes the root cause =
impossible to diagnose.

In contrast, Claude Code=E2=80=99s memory is =
stored in plain Markdown files. When the AI does something weird, you open =
the relevant CLAUDE.md or memory file and see exactly what it=E2=80=99s =
been told=E2=80=94the instructions and remembered facts it=E2=80=99s acting=
 on. Debugging is just editing a text file.

It=E2=80=99s worth asking why =
OpenClaw=E2=80=99s system got so complicated. One likely answer is that =
each layer exists to patch a weakness in the simpler one beneath it. Start =
with plain notes in a file, and soon the agent needs a way to search =
them=E2=80=94so search gets bolted on. Each functionality adds another =
layer where something can go wrong.


BUILDING CLAUDIE ON CLAUDE CODE

Every=E2=80=99s consulting team needed an AI employee to handle =
operations=E2=80=94track engagements, manage communications, update =
dashboards, and follow up on tasks with our clients. Knowing what =
we=E2=80=99d learned about both tools=E2=80=94especially OpenClaw=E2=80=99s=
 unreliability=E2=80=94we built that employee, Claudie, on Claude Code.

Our team lives in Slack, so Claudie needed to be there, too. That meant =
writing a layer of code to connect Claude Code to Slack=E2=80=94about 1,100=
 lines of Python. It listens for messages in Slack, hands them to Claude =
Code, streams the responses back, and takes care of housekeeping like =
formatting and file uploads. Everything else=E2=80=94sessions, memory, =
tools, and skills=E2=80=94Claude Code handles on its own.

