<h2 id=3D"what-people-loved-about-openclaw-and-how-claude-code-gets-you-the=
re">What people loved about OpenClaw=E2=80=94and how Claude Code gets you t=
here </h2>
<p>OpenClaw went viral because it made something click. For the first time,=
 a lot of people watched an AI go off and complete a task. The unofficial t=
agline that emerged across reviews and threads was: AI that <u><a href=3D"h=
ttps://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f0=
5e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2x=
hdWRlIENvZGUgSXMgdGhlIE9wZW5DbGF3IEFsdGVybmF0aXZlIFlvdSBBbHJlYWR5IEhhdmUiLC=
Jwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzIxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYW9sL=
mNvbS9hcnRpY2xlcy9hbmRyZWota2FycGF0aHktc2F5cy11c2VzLWFpLTA5MDkwMTA2Mi5odG1s=
IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE0fQ=3D=3D/1/0100019f0478fe27-57a47ec2-4652-4312-8baf-34a=
1777c3fc0-000000/mF6OylzuBw8pgcOYVK5xAHJYIB54MK8r-wwQzqTYD0w=3D452" rel=3D"=
noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">actually does things</a></u>. Those =
capabilities break down into five categories=E2=80=94and Claude Code has ev=
ery one. </p>
<h4 id=3D"1-it-feels-like-a-person">1. <strong>It feels like a person.</str=
ong>
</h4>
<p>OpenClaw follows you everywhere=E2=80=94it remembers what you were worki=
ng on yesterday, knows what=E2=80=99s on your calendar, and has context abo=
ut your work.</p>
<p>Why it feels that way has very little to do with which messaging app=E2=
=80=94Telegram, Slack, or iMessage=E2=80=94through which you reach it. The =
feeling comes from how much of your work and your life the agent can see. B=
ecause OpenClaw runs from your home folder, it can read every note, file, a=
nd folder in your machine, giving it the breadth of context an employee wou=
ld have.</p>
<p>Most people don=E2=80=99t realize Claude Code can do the same thing. The=
y run it inside a single project folder, so it feels like a coding tool=E2=
=80=94but that=E2=80=99s just the limited context they=E2=80=99ve given it.=
 </p>
<p>This is an easy fix. Give Claude Code access to your whole computer, and=
 now you have an =E2=80=9CAI employee.=E2=80=9D </p>
<p>By default, Claude Code asks for your approval before each action, where=
as OpenClaw doesn=E2=80=99t. That=E2=80=99s prudent when you=E2=80=99re usi=
ng it for professional work. But if you want it to have more autonomy, pass=
 the flag --dangerously-skip-permissions. </p>
<h4 id=3D"2-it-does-things-in-the-real-world"><strong>2. It does things in =
the real world.</strong></h4>
<p>People who use OpenClaw all have a story about a magical =E2=80=9Caha mo=
ment=E2=80=9D when they saw it in action. They sent it a message and woke u=
p to a working website with a payment processor wired in. They asked it to =
triage their inbox overnight and found the replies drafted by morning. </p>
<p>Similarly, Claude Code can operate your computer directly=E2=80=94create=
 and move files, run programs, and read web pages. And both tools can plug =
into outside services=E2=80=94Google Workspace, your customer relationship =
manager, Asana, a calendar, a database=E2=80=94using model context protocol=
 (MCP), an open standard Anthropic introduced.</p>
<h4 id=3D"3-it-remembers-sort-of"><strong>3. It remembers (sort of).</stron=
g></h4>
<p>People discovered OpenClaw could remember things long-term. Save a note =
today, ask about it a month later=E2=80=94=E2=80=9CWhat have I read about X=
 lately? =E2=80=94and it returns not just that note but related things you=
=E2=80=99d forgotten you wrote down.</p>
<p>Claude Code=E2=80=99s memory is more elaborate. It exists across three l=
ayers:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>A file called CLAUDE.md.</strong> It sits in any folder you run Cla=
ude Code from, and you fill it with whatever the agent should know. It read=
s it every time it starts up. It=E2=80=99s plain text, so you can edit it i=
n any editor.</li>
<li>
<strong>A built-in memory system</strong>. As the agent works, it saves use=
ful observations and pulls them back up later. It=E2=80=99s native to Claud=
e Code, with nothing to set up.</li>
<li>
<strong>A local search engine.</strong> For Claudie, I added qmd, an open-s=
ource tool that indexes every conversation log, meeting transcript, and wor=
k document on the machine, and lets her search across all of it.</li>
</ol>
<h4 id=3D"4-you-can-teach-it-new-skills"><strong>4. You can teach it new sk=
ills.</strong></h4>
<p>OpenClaw lets you teach it new tricks: Write a simple text file describi=
ng a task you want it to handle=E2=80=94a =E2=80=9Dskill=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94a=
nd the agent picks it up and follows it. At Every, we have skills for build=
ing decks with the right branding, managing client dashboards, and doing pr=
oject management in Asana. </p>
<p>Skills are an Anthropic standard, which means Claude Code has the same s=
ystem built in. A skill written for OpenClaw runs in Claude Code as is, no =
changes needed.</p>
<h4 id=3D"5-it-runs-on-its-own-and-works-while-you-sleep"><strong>5. It run=
s on its own and works while you sleep.</strong></h4>
<p>OpenClaw=E2=80=99s calling-card feature is called the heartbeat=E2=80=94=
a process that runs every minute, decides what needs doing, and does it. At=
 6 a.m., before you=E2=80=99ve looked at your phone, it has read your overn=
ight email, scanned your calendar, drafted your daily briefing, and queued =
up replies to the messages that need them. </p>
<p>Underneath, the heartbeat is a cron job. Cron is a piece of software tha=
t=E2=80=99s been built into the Mac and Linux operating systems since the 1=
970s, and it does exactly one thing: run a piece of code on a schedule. Tha=
t could be every minute, every hour, or every Monday at 6 a.m. </p>
<p>Claude Code could plug into the same system. It has a mode (called =E2=
=80=9Cheadless=E2=80=9D) where you hand it a task, and it runs on its own, =
without you sitting in front of it. Pair that with cron, and you=E2=80=99ve=
 got an AI that works while you sleep. Even <strong>Peter Steinberger</stro=
ng>, the creator of OpenClaw, <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:=
%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687=
adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgSXMgdGhlIE9wZW5Db=
GF3IEFsdGVybmF0aXZlIFlvdSBBbHJlYWR5IEhhdmUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzIxLCJwb3N0X3R5=
cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly94LmNvbS9zdGVpcGV0ZS9zdGF0dXMvMjAxNDEyMzA=
4NjgxNjE5ODcxMT9zPTIwIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE1fQ=3D=3D/1/0100019f0478fe27-57a47e=
c2-4652-4312-8baf-34a1777c3fc0-000000/ftLTBnN9XOua94oIhyLB_VUt-GBGj4PWfJksh=
C0iX-c=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">has said</a></u=
> that this Claude-Code-plus-cron-job setup is the same as OpenClaw. </p>
<h2 id=3D"where-openclaw-falls-short">Where OpenClaw falls short </h2>
<p>We=E2=80=99ve established that OpenClaw and Claude Code can do many of t=
he same things. So why do I steer people toward Claude Code? A few reasons.=
</p>
<p>Let me give credit where it=E2=80=99s due: OpenClaw showed the world som=
ething important and earned every one of its <u><a href=3D"https://icu.ever=
y.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f=
