er" target=3D"_blank">CLAUDE.md</a></u> file=E2=80=94the standing context y=
ou=E2=80=99ve written for it=E2=80=94which usually runs 2,000 to 5,000 toke=
ns, not 50,000. And when a thread does get long enough to need compaction, =
a process where the system compresses older conversation history to make ro=
om for new information, Claude trims it back automatically. </p>
<h4 id=3D"the-memory-problem">The memory problem</h4>
<p>OpenClaw=E2=80=99s memory system is elaborate. It=E2=80=99s built to rem=
ember details across conversations so the agent doesn=E2=80=99t start from =
scratch every time you message it. To do that, it runs on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Eight separate bootstrap files, the files that it writes when you first=
 start it up, each storing a different kind of information about you, inclu=
ding AGENTS.md, SOUL.md, TOOLS.md, IDENTITY.md, USER.md, HEARTBEAT.md, BOOT=
STRAP.md, and <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2=
Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18=
966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgSXMgdGhlIE9wZW5DbGF3IEFsdGVybmF0a=
XZlIFlvdSBBbHJlYWR5IEhhdmUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzIxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0Iiwi=
dXJsIjoiaHR0cDovL21lbW9yeS5tZCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoyMn0=3D/1/0100019f0478fe27-5=
7a47ec2-4652-4312-8baf-34a1777c3fc0-000000/KM5-kUsXnzGYyysDiN9R9ta7KJuqNDdt=
N4vWnZYoNWQ=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">MEMORY.md<=
/a></u>
</li>
<li>A memory consolidation =E2=80=9Cdreaming=E2=80=9D process, where it rev=
iews everything that happened during the day and decides which scraps are w=
orth keeping and which to forget</li>
<li>Multiple ways to search</li>
<li>A storage layer that organizes what it knows, with evidence attached to=
 each claim, like a personal Wikipedia</li>
</ul>
<p>As an engineer, I can appreciate why they might have built it this way, =
but as a user, this sophistication has a cost.</p>
<p>When OpenClaw makes a mistake=E2=80=94forgets something important or act=
s on stale information=E2=80=94the answer could lie in any of a half-dozen =
places: the bootstrap files, the daily notes, the dreaming process, a conde=
nsed version of older notes, what=E2=80=99s saved when the system clears it=
s short-term memory, or the encyclopedic storage layer. Often, there=E2=80=
=99s no way to tell which, which makes the root cause impossible to diagnos=
e.</p>
<p>In contrast, Claude Code=E2=80=99s memory is stored in plain Markdown fi=
les. When the AI does something weird, you open the relevant CLAUDE.md or m=
emory file and see exactly what it=E2=80=99s been told=E2=80=94the instruct=
ions and remembered facts it=E2=80=99s acting on. Debugging is just editing=
 a text file. </p>
<p>It=E2=80=99s worth asking why OpenClaw=E2=80=99s system got so complicat=
ed. One likely answer is that each layer exists to patch a weakness in the =
simpler one beneath it. Start with plain notes in a file, and soon the agen=
t needs a way to search them=E2=80=94so search gets bolted on. Each functio=
nality adds another layer where something can go wrong. </p>
<h2 id=3D"building-claudie-on-claude-code">Building Claudie on Claude Code<=
/h2>
<p>Every=E2=80=99s consulting team needed an AI employee to handle operatio=
ns=E2=80=94track engagements, manage communications, update dashboards, and=
 follow up on tasks with our clients. Knowing what we=E2=80=99d learned abo=
ut both tools=E2=80=94especially OpenClaw=E2=80=99s unreliability=E2=80=94w=
e built that employee, Claudie, on Claude Code. </p>
<p>Our team lives in Slack, so Claudie needed to be there, too. That meant =
writing a layer of code to connect Claude Code to Slack=E2=80=94about 1,100=
 lines of Python. It listens for messages in Slack, hands them to Claude Co=
de, streams the responses back, and takes care of housekeeping like formatt=
ing and file uploads. Everything else=E2=80=94sessions, memory, tools, and =
skills=E2=80=94Claude Code handles on its own.</p>
