INSIDE EVERY

DAN IS CLONING KATE, BUT NOT IN A=
 WEIRD WAY

For as long as I=E2=80=99ve been at Every, Dan has been chasing=
 the same white whale: cloning our editor in chief, Kate Lee https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2FuIEFJIExlYXJuIEdvb2QgSnVkZ21lbnQ_IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6N=
DMxNywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vb24tZXZlcnkv=
a2F0ZS1sZWUtam9pbnMtZXZlcnktYXMtZWRpdG9yLWluLWNoaWVmIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI3fQ=
=3D=3D.

Only a narrow slice of her, to be clear. He wants an AI copy =
editor that can identify the sentence-level problems Kate would catch =
before she ever sees a draft. Copy editing is indispensable, tedious work, =
and every hour Kate spends repairing links and cleaning sentences is an =
hour she can=E2=80=99t spend shaping arguments or developing writers.

Dan=E2=80=99s previous attempts relied on prompts, style guides, and skills=
. Those approaches could teach a general-purpose model the rules Kate was =
able to articulate, but they couldn=E2=80=99t reproduce the judgment she =
uses when rules collide. The same repetition can feel lazy in one paragraph=
 and essential to the rhythm of another; a hedge can weaken a claim or keep=
 the writer from saying something untrue. Adding more instructions produced=
 an ever-longer list of exceptions, but not Kate-like results.

This time, Dan is changing the model itself. Using Tinker https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2FuIEFJIExlYXJuIEdvb2QgSnVkZ21lbnQ_IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6N=
DMxNywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vdGhpbmtpbmdtYWNoaW5lcy5h=
aS90aW5rZXIvIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI4fQ=3D=3D, an API from Thinking Machines =
that lets builders and programmers train models from their own machine, he =
has fine-tuned three or four candidate models on 30,027 of Kate=E2=80=99s =
historical suggestions. The models learn Kate=E2=80=99s patterns across =
thousands of real-life examples instead of trying to reconstruct her =
judgment from prompts and rules..

Dan gives each version of a candidate =
model the sentences that Kate has already edited, but hides her changes. He=
 then compares the model=E2=80=99s suggestions with hers. Anything it =
misses=E2=80=94or makes worse=E2=80=94becomes another problem for the next =
version to solve. He=E2=80=99ll consider a model successful when it catches=
 nine out of every 10 edits Kate would make, and Kate can accept nine out =
of 10 of its suggestions as written, without the model ever introducing a =
serious error. If it works, Kate gets hours back for the editorial =
decisions that require all of her=E2=80=94not only the slice Dan is trying =
to clone.

