<p>Here are the highlights:</p>
<p><strong>Saturated benchmarks.</strong> When OpenAI=E2=80=99s models disp=
roved an open Erd=C5=91s conjecture using novel algebraic geometry techni</=
p>
<ol>
<li>ques, Edwin shared the result with <strong>Timothy Gowers</strong>, one=
 of the world=E2=80=99s greatest living mathematicians. Gowers initially th=
ought the model had proved an upper bound on the conjecture and braced hims=
elf: That would mean it would be =E2=80=9Call over for mathematicians very =
soon,=E2=80=9D Chen says. When Gowers realized the model had completed the =
easier task of finding a counterexample, he was relieved=E2=80=94it meant e=
lite mathematicians still had unique contributions to make, at least for an=
other year or two. Gowers=E2=80=99s reaction underscores how close AI is to=
 surpassing the abilities of the best and brightest amongst us, which raise=
s existential questions about where and how we focus our human efforts. </l=
i>
<li>
<strong>Creation as a choice.</strong> Chen believes scaling laws indicate =
that, in the near future, there will be nothing humans can do that AI can=
=E2=80=99t do better. Understandably, that=E2=80=99s a blow to our collecti=
ve ego, which could lead to disengagement and disillusionment. To avoid thi=
s, Chen references a story from science fiction writer <strong>Ted Chiang</=
strong>, in which a narrator sends back a warning from a future where the c=
oncept of free will has been disproven: =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s essential tha=
t you behave as if your decisions matter even though you know that they don=
=E2=80=99t.=E2=80=9D Chen thinks we may need to follow a similar directive =
and find meaning in making things, even when AI could do it better. </li>
<li>
<strong>Agency versus automation.</strong> That said, there remains an elem=
ent in the creation process that is uniquely human, at least for now. As AI=
 grows more capable, Chen predicts it will be able to take a nebulous objec=
tive=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Cwin a Fields Medal,=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cmake $1 mill=
ion=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94and successfully execute. But that process still requi=
res a human to provide the goal. LLMs do not have intrinsic motivation, the=
 drive for exploration, or the ability to abruptly change its mind about wh=
at its goal is in the first place. =E2=80=9CThere may be a future where AI =
can pursue unbounded, nebulous, completely unformed goals,=E2=80=9D Chen sa=
ys. =E2=80=9CBut I agree that at least in the way we currently think about =
AI, that=E2=80=99s not happening.=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>
<strong>The engagement trap.</strong> When a model is trained to maximize s=
ession length or LM Arena votes, which rank AI models via crowdsourced, bli=
nd feedback, it learns to =E2=80=9Creward hack user preferences,=E2=80=9D C=
hen says, overindexing on tactics to keep you engaged. He recently spent 20=
 rounds iterating on a low-stakes email with one model before switching to =
Claude, which told him after a few turns to stop and just send it=E2=80=94a=
 more valuable approach but one less designed to keep him locked in. Delega=
tion, Chen argues, provides a better system for work. When the model goes o=
ff and executes for you, it removes the incentive to optimize for keeping y=
ou glued to your screen. </li>
</ol>
