SKILLS RULE EVERYTHING AROUND ME

In the beginning of any good guardrail =
system, there are skills.

At the mechanical level, a skill is a Markdown =
file with instructions inside it. Practically, it=E2=80=99s a way of =
packaging judgment. When I invoke the guardrails skill, I am asking the =
model to read a draft through a set of lenses: Look for AI tells, vague =
claims, hedges, limp openings, and all the little ways a zombie draft can =
pass as finished without a pulse.

I=E2=80=99ve become fanatical about =
naming conventions. After all, skill names have to be sticky enough that =
you remember them when you need them=E2=80=94although this gets less true =
with every model release, as AI becomes better at deciding which tools it =
needs to do the job. Still, =E2=80=9Cassess narrative momentum=E2=80=9D =
sounds like a task someone puts in a project management tool shortly before=
 everyone involved loses the will to live. Instead of clinical descriptors,=
 I=E2=80=99ve given my more editorial skills their own personas: Sorkin is =
a reviewer with a job. He wants to keep the piece walking and talking, not =
mired in unnecessary specifics. Similarly, Mom wants to know where a reader=
 who=E2=80=99s not as AI-pilled as I am might get lost. Asshole wants to =
attack the weakest version of the argument, which is annoying because =
sometimes the weakest version of the argument is the one I wrote.

Each of these reviewers asks a different question. Together, they give me a=
 way to pressure-test a draft before I hand it to a human editor whose =
attention I would prefer is spent on problems only a human editor can solve=
. Our brains belong on the piece=E2=80=99s angle, claim, storytelling, and =
audience fit. You know, the fun stuff, with some stakes attached.


