e219c2c4c-000000/zmMlxRsVc7g2T76X--_Zikd0LPpla4PG54dFLS_tlVA=3D452" rel=3D"=
noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Sign up</a></u> to get it in your in=
box.</em></p>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>Before a recent one-on-one with <strong><u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.=
to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f45=
4c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTXkgRWRpdG9yIENhdWdo=
dCBNZSBTb3VuZGluZyBMaWtlIEFJLiBOb3cgQUkgQ2F0Y2hlcyBNZSBGaXJzdC4iLCJwb3N0X2l=
kIjo0MjcwLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9Aa2F0ZV=
8xNzY3IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjZ9/1/0100019ea7bdc19a-b6a6988e-3669-4a87-b31e-4cce2=
19c2c4c-000000/qKn_LD7vGnUy8ml1YCds1Xljg3GmIX7NiBUYE4LvUlw=3D452" rel=3D"no=
opener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Kate Lee</a></u></strong>, Every=E2=80=
=99s editor in chief, I opened our shared document and found a list of my o=
wn writing fails staring back at me. My drafts had picked up too many of th=
e AI tells that both I=E2=80=94and you=E2=80=94know how to spot from across=
 the room: the symmetrical sentence structures, the little rhetorical throa=
t-clears, the phrases that sound profound on first pass but on closer inspe=
ction contain nothing but air, and those pesky sets of three. </p>
<p>The worst part was that I should know better. I am the person at Every w=
ho writes about writing with AI while using AI to write about writing with =
AI. I have custom agents, <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%=
2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTXkgRWRpdG9yIENhdWdodCBNZSBTb3VuZGluZ=
yBMaWtlIEFJLiBOb3cgQUkgQ2F0Y2hlcyBNZSBGaXJzdC4iLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MjcwLCJwb3N0=
X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9ndWlkZXMvYWktc3R5bGUtZ3V=
pZGU_c291cmNlPXBvc3RfYnV0dG9uIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjd9/1/0100019ea7bdc19a-b6a698=
8e-3669-4a87-b31e-4cce219c2c4c-000000/87J39wDsevJ3EoRj4LTqEO8chGV0daAttL-Xb=
EhzBcQ=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">style guides</a=
></u>, editorial workflows, and an apparently bottomless appetite for turni=
ng every lesson into a system. And still, I had <u><a href=3D"https://icu.e=
very.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e6=
54f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTXkgRWRpdG9yIEN=
hdWdodCBNZSBTb3VuZGluZyBMaWtlIEFJLiBOb3cgQUkgQ2F0Y2hlcyBNZSBGaXJzdC4iLCJwb3=
N0X2lkIjo0MjcwLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by93b=
3JraW5nLW92ZXJ0aW1lL3dlLW5lZWQtdG8tdGFsay1hYm91dC1haS1hdXRvcGlsb3QiLCJwb3Np=
dGlvbiI6OH0=3D/1/0100019ea7bdc19a-b6a6988e-3669-4a87-b31e-4cce219c2c4c-0000=
00/-p3zQO7g_TrzuHQ2uELrfuofHAPnIJjHQJwywNXOP4U=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noref=
errer" target=3D"_blank">let the machine=E2=80=99s smoothness</a></u> pass =
for my own judgment enough times that my editors felt the need to intervene=
.</p>
<p>After the meeting, I did what I generally do when I learn something new,=
 embarrassing or otherwise: I baked it into documentation for my agents. I =
opened the notes, pulled out the patterns Kate had flagged, and listed them=
 in a new skill called /guardrails, which turns any agent I write with into=
 an exacting editorial specialist that keeps me honest.</p>
<p>I=E2=80=99ll never be completely done with /guardrails, or any of the re=
view skills like it that I=E2=80=99ve built, because my human tics and tend=
encies will move around like a squirmy toddler. But I=E2=80=99d rather make=
 new mistakes than keep repeating the old ones. Review skills are the mecha=
nism by which I do that. They=E2=80=99re another form of editor, one that c=
an catch a draft=E2=80=99s more annoying weak spots before they become a hu=
man editor=E2=80=99s problem. </p>
