gI=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Codex</a></u> or Cl=
aude Code. But once the reviewers are in place and working, I can spend mor=
e of my time pushing the draft from good to great. My drafts are now much c=
leaner and my own preferences are less of a mystery to myself, because I=E2=
=80=99ve had to think and talk about them enough that they=E2=80=99ve worn =
new grooves into my brain. </p>
<p>I=E2=80=99m going to show you a few of the reviewers I rely on and what =
goes into them (I=E2=80=99ll share a set on Every=E2=80=99s GitHub along wi=
th this piece). But it should serve as an example, not a blueprint; the spe=
cial sauce of this process comes from setting and enforcing your own collec=
tion of style requirements. </p>
<h2 id=3D"skills-rule-everything-around-me">Skills rule everything around m=
e</h2>
<p>In the beginning of any good guardrail system, there are <strong>skills.=
 </strong></p>
<p>At the mechanical level, a skill is a Markdown file with instructions in=
side it. Practically, it=E2=80=99s a way of packaging judgment. When I invo=
ke the guardrails skill, I am asking the model to read a draft through a se=
t of lenses: Look for AI tells, vague claims, hedges, limp openings, and al=
l the little ways a zombie draft can pass as finished without a pulse.</p>
<p>I=E2=80=99ve become fanatical about naming conventions. After all, skill=
 names have to be sticky enough that you remember them when you need them=
=E2=80=94although this gets less true with every model release, as AI becom=
es better at deciding which tools it needs to do the job. Still, =E2=80=9Ca=
ssess narrative momentum=E2=80=9D sounds like a task someone puts in a proj=
ect management tool shortly before everyone involved loses the will to live=
. Instead of clinical descriptors, I=E2=80=99ve given my more editorial ski=
lls their own personas: <strong>Sorkin</strong> is a reviewer with a job. H=
e wants to keep the piece walking and talking, not mired in unnecessary spe=
cifics. Similarly, <strong>Mom</strong> wants to know where a reader who=E2=
=80=99s not as AI-pilled as I am might get lost. <strong>Asshole</strong> w=
ants to attack the weakest version of the argument, which is annoying becau=
se sometimes the weakest version of the argument is the one I wrote.</p>
<p>Each of these reviewers asks a different question. Together, they give m=
e a way to pressure-test a draft before I hand it to a human editor whose a=
ttention I would prefer is spent on problems only a human editor can solve.=
 Our brains belong on the piece=E2=80=99s angle, claim, storytelling, and a=
udience fit. You know, the fun stuff, with some stakes attached. </p>
