<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>At the end of June, we lost access to GPT-5.6 Sol while it went through =
government review. <strong><u>Dan Shipper</u></strong> said returning to ol=
der models felt like going back to the Stone Age. <strong><u>Austin Tedesco=
</u></strong> compared GPT-5.5 to shooting a basketball twice as heavy.</p>
<p>Now Sol is back. It=E2=80=99s become our default for work we want to sha=
pe as we go: writing, coding, research, and analysis inside OpenAI=E2=80=99=
s new unified ChatGPT and Codex desktop app. Sol is fast, unusually good at=
 finding and using context, and easy to redirect. Anthropic=E2=80=99s Fable=
 still leads on broad assignments that require the model to decide what sho=
uld be built in the first place.</p>
<p>We get into all of it in our Vibe Check, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Why Sol scored 56/100 against Fable=E2=80=99s 90/100 on our Senior Engi=
neer benchmark=E2=80=94and how 12,900 lines of unnecessary code explain mos=
t of the gap</li>
<li>How <strong><u>Kieran Klaassen</u></strong> rebuilt <strong><u>Proof</u=
></strong> with Sol in about one-third the time Fable needed, only to prefe=
r Fable=E2=80=99s design</li>
<li>Why Sol finished last in our six-model writing benchmark while I (<stro=
ng><u>Katie Parrott</u></strong>) still used it to move through 24 drafts o=
f this article in six to eight hours</li>
<li>How Sol found <strong>Arielle Shipper</strong>=E2=80=99s email, inspect=
ed 46 CSV files, and returned with seven useful questions=E2=80=94then made=
 a calculation error serious enough to shake her trust</li>
</ul>


