Up until now, you could throw money at AI experiments because the cost was =
largely covered by a subscription model that subsidized heavy use under a =
set monthly price. Those days are numbered, Nityesh says. =E2=80=9CWe are =
in a compute-constrained world where we will not have enough data centers =
to run tomorrow=E2=80=99s AI.=E2=80=9D Expect usage costs to go up, =
particularly for individuals as AI companies prioritize big-budget =
enterprise customers.

At the same time, cheaper models=E2=80=94many of =
them open-sourced=E2=80=94have grown more capable, operating, by =
Nityesh=E2=80=99s estimation, roughly seven months behind the frontier.

Here=E2=80=99s how Nityesh is thinking about token efficiency:

 1. Routing. The simplest version is to bake a model choice into a =
skill=E2=80=99s instructions, so that skill always defaults to a cheaper =
model for that task. Dynamic workflows=E2=80=94Anthropic=E2=80=99s =
orchestration feature for large, multi-agent Claude Code jobs=E2=80=94do =
this natively, letting you assign a specific model to each component of a =
job. It=E2=80=99s a =E2=80=9Cfirst-class=E2=80=9D option, Nityesh says, but=
 with a major caveat: You can only choose from Anthropic=E2=80=99s own =
models, not open-source options or those from OpenAI.
 2. Evaluations. Before you route a task to a cheaper model, you need to =
evaluate whether it can execute without a drop in performance.. =
That=E2=80=99s hard to do because LLMs are =E2=80=9Cinherently bad at =
assessing their own capabilities,=E2=80=9D Nityesh says. Creating good =
evaluation sets requires manual, good-old-fashioned human judgment. The =
stakes are high: =E2=80=9CIf you have good evals, then you lower the cost. =
But if you have bad evals, you=E2=80=99re getting bad signal,=E2=80=9D he =
says.
 3. Token audits. After an agent has completed a task, Nityesh has it=
 break down how many tokens it used per step. He then reviews each one, =
identifies areas where token spend appears excessive for the complexity of =
the required work, and has the agent explain why it burned through so much =
compute. It=E2=80=99s a manual process; for all their intelligence, LLMs =
aren=E2=80=99t good at identifying their own inefficiencies. =E2=80=9CIf an=
 AI tells me that it used 20 million tokens for reviewing the outputs, =
that=E2=80=99s a red flag because I know that [outsize number] =
doesn=E2=80=99t make sense,=E2=80=9D Nityesh says. =E2=80=9CBut AI =
doesn=E2=80=99t have that intuition yet.=E2=80=9D

