=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">commoditized the mode=
l</a></u> of shared, Slack-based team agents (like the ones he built for Ev=
ery=E2=80=99s consulting and editorial teams)? </p>
<p>=E2=80=9CMaking the agent more token efficient,=E2=80=9D he responds wit=
hout hesitation. </p>
<p>Up until now, you could throw money at AI experiments because the cost w=
as largely covered by a subscription model that subsidized heavy use under =
a set monthly price. Those days are numbered, Nityesh says. =E2=80=9CWe are=
 in a compute-constrained world where we will not have enough data centers =
to run tomorrow=E2=80=99s AI.=E2=80=9D Expect usage costs to go up, particu=
larly for individuals as AI companies prioritize big-budget enterprise cust=
omers. </p>
<p>At the same time, cheaper models=E2=80=94many of them open-sourced=E2=80=
=94have grown more capable, operating, by Nityesh=E2=80=99s estimation, rou=
ghly seven months behind the frontier. </p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s how Nityesh is thinking about token efficiency: </p>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Routing. </strong>The simplest version is to bake a model choice in=
to a skill=E2=80=99s instructions, so that skill always defaults to a cheap=
er model for that task. Dynamic workflows=E2=80=94Anthropic=E2=80=99s orche=
stration feature for large, multi-agent Claude Code jobs=E2=80=94do this na=
tively, letting you assign a specific model to each component of a job. It=
=E2=80=99s a =E2=80=9Cfirst-class=E2=80=9D option, Nityesh says, but with a=
 major caveat: You can only choose from Anthropic=E2=80=99s own models, not=
 open-source options or those from OpenAI.</li>
<li>
<strong>Evaluations.</strong> Before you route a task to a cheaper model, y=
ou need to evaluate whether it can execute without a drop in performance.. =
That=E2=80=99s hard to do because LLMs are =E2=80=9Cinherently bad at asses=
sing their own capabilities,=E2=80=9D Nityesh says. Creating good evaluatio=
n sets requires manual, good-old-fashioned human judgment. The stakes are h=
igh: =E2=80=9CIf you have good evals, then you lower the cost. But if you h=
ave bad evals, you=E2=80=99re getting bad signal,=E2=80=9D he says. </li>
<li>
<strong>Token audits. </strong>After an agent has completed a task, Nityesh=
 has it break down how many tokens it used per step. He then reviews each o=
ne, identifies areas where token spend appears excessive for the complexity=
 of the required work, and has the agent explain why it burned through so m=
uch compute. It=E2=80=99s a manual process; for all their intelligence, LLM=
s aren=E2=80=99t good at identifying their own inefficiencies. =E2=80=9CIf =
an AI tells me that it used 20 million tokens for reviewing the outputs, th=
at=E2=80=99s a red flag because I know that [outsize number] doesn=E2=80=99=
t make sense,=E2=80=9D Nityesh says. =E2=80=9CBut AI doesn=E2=80=99t have t=
hat intuition yet.=E2=80=9D </li>
</ol>
<h5 id=3D"try-it-this-week-audit-one-repeated-agent-workflow"><strong>Try i=
t this week: Audit one repeated agent workflow.</strong></h5>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Run it once and collect the receipts. </strong>Ask your agent: =E2=
=80=9CAudit this run. Break down token use by stage. For each stage, report=
 the model used, approximate input and output tokens, the purpose of the st=
ep, and why that amount was necessary. Mark anything you cannot verify as a=
n estimate.=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>
<strong>Investigate any particularly token-hungry steps. </strong>Look for =
a mismatch between the difficulty of the work and the tokens consumed. The =
agent can explain its behavior, but you need to review and determine what s=
eems unreasonable.</li>
<li>
<strong>Change one variable and rerun the test. </strong>Trim the context, =
rewrite the skill instructions, split the work among subagents, or route on=
e step to a cheaper model. Compare token use and output quality with the fi=
rst run. Keep the change only if the result still passes your quality check=
.</li>
</ol>
