<h3><hr class=3D"quill-line"></h3>
<h2 id=3D"counterpoint">Counterpoint</h2>
<h4 id=3D"ai-will-outpace-human-ability-but-it-wont-be-cheap">AI will outpa=
ce human ability, but it won=E2=80=99t be cheap</h4>
<p>In <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%=
2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%=
2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFdl4oCZbGwgU3RpbGwgQmUgRW1wbG95ZWQgV2hlbiBBSSBDYW4gR=
G8gRXZlcnl0aGluZyIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyODksInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJo=
dHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3AvYWZ0ZXItYXV0b21hdGlvbiIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjozM30=3D/1/01=
00019e943c7e06-9b2e8dba-2618-4cf1-b950-fcfcd96fadaf-000000/IrPhLD-tOMX48AmD=
ZJo6BJ1hSeWK8HUpYUHGv3epZys=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_b=
lank">=E2=80=9CAfter Automation,=E2=80=9D</a></u> Dan argues that AI progre=
ss creates more work for humans, not less. Each time the models saturate a =
benchmark=E2=80=94and make yesterday=E2=80=99s human competence cheap in th=
e process=E2=80=94we reset the frame. The model then saturates that frame t=
oo, we reset the frame once more, and the cycle repeats=E2=80=94forever. Th=
e frame, Dan says, is never the framer.</p>
<p>If Every were a normal company, I=E2=80=99d hesitate to publicly disagre=
e with my CEO. It isn=E2=80=99t, so here goes: I don=E2=80=99t think the =
=E2=80=9Cforever=E2=80=9D part holds up.</p>
<p>The dynamic Dan describes matches my experience. A year ago, I wrote pro=
mpts until the model got better at generating them. Then I became the one s=
upplying context until the model bested me at that, too. Today I spend my t=
ime orchestrating agents and determining what =E2=80=9Cgood=E2=80=9D output=
s look like. Each time AI absorbs a piece of my job, the frame expands to i=
nclude more abstract, <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fev=
ery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebe=
fcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFdl4oCZbGwgU3RpbGwgQmUgRW1wbG95ZWQgV=
2hlbiBBSSBDYW4gRG8gRXZlcnl0aGluZyIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyODksInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBv=
c3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL2d1aWRlcy90aGUtZWlnaHQtbGV2ZWxzLW9mLWF=
pLWFkb3B0aW9uIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjM0fQ=3D=3D/1/0100019e943c7e06-9b2e8dba-2618-=
4cf1-b950-fcfcd96fadaf-000000/xLWBPC8IdSuLMZUUTyiJXqjPGv4byn9iCMijY9wgw2Y=
=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">higher-level</a></u> =
work.</p>
<p>But I don=E2=80=99t think this progression will last forever. My predict=
ion is that in a year or two, in a few well-run companies, AI will be able =
to execute every knowledge-worker task better than humans can=E2=80=94inclu=
ding setting the frames. In my role, I expect to be attending meetings to g=
ather context that doesn=E2=80=99t exist online. The other parts of my job=
=E2=80=93=E2=80=93defining evals, deciding goals, running experiments=E2=80=
=93=E2=80=93will be handled by the equivalent of Opus 6 or GPT-7.</p>
<p>Why am I confident AI is capable of taking this last step? Because frami=
ng isn=E2=80=99t magic. We don=E2=80=99t pull goals out of thin air; we der=
ive them from the layered experience of being a person in the world and the=
 bounds of our social and physical surroundings. Physics is the ultimate ev=
al metric, because if you get it wrong you die. Human ability feels like th=
e natural peg for meaning, but we=E2=80=99re just one form intelligence can=
 take. AI is another, and a system that learns from its environment can eve=
ntually run the same loop.</p>
<p>Intelligence costs energy, however, and I suspect evolution already made=
 all the right tradeoffs to make us as smart as possible for our environmen=
t given constrained resources. For situations where there isn=E2=80=99t eno=
ugh training data, a human runs on intuition and gut=E2=80=94words that des=
cribe a brain evolved to use thinking shortcuts, or heuristics, to survive.=
 A model doesn=E2=80=99t inherit DNA encoded with millions of years of evol=
ution, so it has to brute-force its way there through an expensive series o=
f simulations or =E2=80=9Cthinking=E2=80=9D tokens to get enough data to de=
cide. There are no free lunches in economics, and AI isn=E2=80=99t magic=E2=
=80=94it can=E2=80=99t get to super-human general intelligence without supe=
r-human energy consumption. Beating humans on more subjective tasks will re=
quire more thinking tokens than its worth. Just hire the human.</p>
<p>The question will evolve from, =E2=80=9CCan AI do this?=E2=80=9D to, =E2=
=80=9CIs it worth the compute?=E2=80=9D or, alternatively, =E2=80=9CDo I re=
ally <em>want</em> an AI doing this for me?=E2=80=9D It makes sense to dele=
gate tasks to a $20 a month model, or a $200 a month model, but as the <u><=
a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F=
7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJq=
ZWN0IjoiV2h5IFdl4oCZbGwgU3RpbGwgQmUgRW1wbG95ZWQgV2hlbiBBSSBDYW4gRG8gRXZlcnl=
0aGluZyIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyODksInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2=
