<h2 id=3D"inside-every">Inside Every</h2>
<h4 id=3D"what-86-percent-doesnt-tell-you">What 86 percent doesn=E2=80=99t =
tell you</h4>
<p>To use KateBench, an editor tags @every to invoke the Every agent, and a=
sks for a Kate copyedit pass. The agent leaves suggestions directly in the =
Google Doc, where the editor decides whether to accept or reject each one. =
</p>
<p>Across recent runs, editors have accepted about 85 to 90 percent of its =
suggestions. <strong><u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Feve=
ry.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebef=
cc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQmVuY2htYXJrcyBEb27igJl0IEtub3cgWW91ciBKb2=
IiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0NDUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVye=
S50by90ZWFtIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE1fQ=3D=3D/1/010001a03a6c3493-c00ebefb-975f-4d=
da-ae37-c5b8f6ba32b6-000000/PF53IBgWLw0m8lIlYMpiC8AMEn0sPBZsYSejcwgPG6s=3D4=
52" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Jannik Jung</a></u></stro=
ng>, the engineer who owns KateBench, showed us two reasons to distrust tha=
t number. The tool stopped after filing 40 suggestions, so on long essays i=
t could find a good edit and throw it away before the editor saw it. The ca=
p is now 80. </p>
