my workday with help from Codex already feels like I=E2=80=99ve been =
working this way for years.

I wanted to understand why some AI workflows =
become essential while others have the staying power of a celebrity romance=
. So I did a post-mortem on the chief offenders and looked at relevant =
behavioral science research. It turns out the difference comes down to what=
 a workflow asks of my time, energy, and sanity=E2=80=94and what it offers =
in return.


AN ABANDONED WORKFLOW IS NOT A CHARACTER TEST

Once I noticed that little blue dot next to Attention Desk and the fact =
that I wasn=E2=80=99t responding to it, I let it sit like a check-engine =
light I=E2=80=99d decided to live with. Over time, the notification started=
 to feel like an indictment of my character.

My first reaction was to =
label it a me problem. I simply lacked the stick-to-itiveness or =
sophistication to get these systems to work. Somewhere out there, other, =
better people were adopting these workflows and living their =
10-times-more-productive lives while the permanent underclass had a space =
with my name on it.

Maybe that=E2=80=99s just my own unique brand of =
catastrophizing, but I suspect this feeling of wanting to build all the =
workflows=E2=80=94particularly the workflows of people we perceive as =
successful=E2=80=94is common. Model capabilities and the best practices for=
 working with them are changing so fast that it=E2=80=99s understandable to=
 want to follow the lead of people who are winning at AI. When you try what=
 works for them and don=E2=80=99t get their results, it=E2=80=99s tempting =
to assume you=E2=80=99re doomed to failure. At least, that=E2=80=99s what =
happens if you=E2=80=99re me.

Rationally, I know that =E2=80=9Ceither I =
can make this workflow work or I=E2=80=99m a failure=E2=80=9D is classic =
black-and-white thinking. A workflow may go unused for a boatload of =
reasons: The problem is too infrequent, the trigger doesn=E2=80=99t go off,=
 or the output creates more work than it saves. A tool can fail because my =
habits don=E2=80=99t support it; my habits can also be rational responses =
to the work I actually do.


I BUILT ATTENTION DESK FOR A WORKDAY I =
DON=E2=80=99T HAVE

I wanted what Tend offered: to sink into deep work =
without wondering what was happening in Slack, then surface to find nothing=
 had caught fire. But Dan is a busy CEO pulled in enough directions that =
Tend=E2=80=99s ability to gather his communication gives him time and =
mental space back. I get an average of six to eight inbound Slack messages =
a day, which I can manage myself without much stress. For me, Tend proved =
to be a solution to a problem I simply didn=E2=80=99t have.

With my Tend dupe, I was also building a workflow that would compete with =
my own deeply ingrained behavior. I have an anxious attachment style when =
it comes to work, and checking Slack is my favorite sanctioned =
procrastination activity. I reliably check Slack 40 to 50 times per =
day=E2=80=94and every time, I feel relief from the discomfort of writing, =
or the motivating hit of a fresh news drop. Attention Desk=E2=80=99s =
attempt to deliver peace of mind couldn=E2=80=99t compete with those =
rewards.

