  <div class=3D"post-body">
    <p class=3D"post-preview-warning">This is a free preview of a subscribe=
rs-only post.</p>
    <p><em>Was this newsletter forwarded to you? <u><a href=3D"https://ever=
y.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18=
966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGV=
ycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodH=
RwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL2FjY291bnQiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6NX0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noref=
errer" target=3D"_blank">Sign up</a></u> to get it in your inbox.</em></p>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>I was on a video call with Every CEO <strong><u><a href=3D"https://every=
.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e189=
66e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVy=
cyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHR=
wczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL0BkYW5zaGlwcGVyIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjZ9" rel=3D"noopener nore=
ferrer" target=3D"_blank">Dan Shipper</a></u></strong>, getting a tour of t=
he increasingly elaborate ways he uses <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/email=
s/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/ey=
JzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27ig=
Jl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2=
ZXJ5LnRvL3AvaG93LXRvLXVzZS1jb2RleC1mb3Ita25vd2xlZGdlLXdvcmstYS1wb3dlci11c2V=
yLXMtZ3VpZGUiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6N30=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"=
_blank">OpenAI=E2=80=99s Codex app</a></u> to run his work life, when I saw=
 the next AI workflow that was going to change mine. At least, that=E2=80=
=99s what I told myself.</p>
<p>Dan was sharing an early version of <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/email=
s/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/ey=
JzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27ig=
Jl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2=
ZXJ5LnRvL3RlbmQiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6OH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=
=3D"_blank">Tend</a></u>, the Codex-native system he created to pull email,=
 Slack, meeting notes, and company updates into one place and help you act =
on them.</p>
<p>The moment the call ended, I gave the transcript to Codex and had it bui=
ld my own version of the tool, which, lacking Dan=E2=80=99s flair for namin=
g things, it called Attention Desk. I could picture the person who would us=
e it: me, but better. I would spend most of the day in deep work, surface a=
 few times to see who and what needed me, and disappear again=E2=80=94respo=
nsive, efficient, and 10 times more productive than I=E2=80=99d been before=
.</p>
<p>As of this writing, Attention Desk sits abandoned in my pinned chats, th=
e little blue dot beside its name the only reminder of how sure I was that =
it would transform my work.</p>
<p>This seems to be a pattern in the way I use AI: I see an impressive demo=
 or have one surprisingly good session with a model and decide to reorganiz=
e my work around it. Then the novelty wears off. The next thing I know, wee=
ks have gone by and I haven=E2=80=99t touched the workflow I was sure would=
 change everything. There=E2=80=99s a veritable Island of Misfit Workflows =
adrift on my desktop, from app ideas that collapsed under the weight of the=
ir own maintenance to AI skills I built and promptly abandoned. And yet wri=
ting with my <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87=
698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUg=
QUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCw=
icG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3dvcmtpbmctb3Zlcn=
RpbWUvd3JpdGluZy13aXRoLWFpLWlzLWhhcmRlci10aGFuLXlvdS10aGluayIsInBvc2l0aW9uI=
jo5fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">compound writing=
 plugin</a></u> or navigating my workday with help from Codex already feels=
 like I=E2=80=99ve been working this way for years. </p>
<p>I wanted to understand why some AI workflows become essential while othe=
rs have the staying power of a celebrity romance. So I did a post-mortem on=
 the chief offenders and looked at relevant behavioral science research. It=
 turns out the difference comes down to what a workflow asks of my time, en=
ergy, and sanity=E2=80=94and what it offers in return.</p>
<h2 id=3D"an-abandoned-workflow-is-not-a-character-test"><strong>An abandon=
ed workflow is not a character test</strong></h2>
<p>Once I noticed that little blue dot next to Attention Desk and the fact =
that I wasn=E2=80=99t<em> </em>responding to it, I let it sit like a check-=
engine light I=E2=80=99d decided to live with. Over time, the notification =
started to feel like an indictment of my character.</p>
<p>My first reaction was to label it a me problem. I simply lacked the stic=
k-to-itiveness or sophistication to get these systems to work. Somewhere ou=
t there, other, better people were adopting these workflows and living thei=
r 10-times-more-productive lives while the permanent underclass had a space=
 with my name on it. </p>
<p>Maybe that=E2=80=99s just my own unique brand of catastrophizing, but I =
suspect this feeling of wanting to build <em>all the workflows=E2=80=94</em=
>particularly the workflows of people we perceive as successful<em>=E2=80=
=94</em>is common. Model capabilities and the best practices for working wi=
th them are changing so fast that it=E2=80=99s understandable to want to fo=
llow the lead of people who are winning at AI. When you try what works for =
them and don=E2=80=99t get their results, it=E2=80=99s tempting to assume y=
ou=E2=80=99re doomed to failure. At least, that=E2=80=99s what happens if y=
ou=E2=80=99re me. </p>
<p>Rationally, I know that =E2=80=9Ceither I can make this workflow work or=
 I=E2=80=99m a failure=E2=80=9D is classic black-and-white thinking. A work=
flow may go unused for a boatload of reasons: The problem is too infrequent=
, the trigger doesn=E2=80=99t go off, or the output creates more work than =
it saves. A tool can fail because my habits don=E2=80=99t support it; my ha=
bits can also be rational responses to the work I actually do.</p>
<h2 id=3D"i-built-attention-desk-for-a-workday-i-dont-have"><strong>I built=
 Attention Desk for a workday I don=E2=80=99t have</strong></h2>
<p>I wanted what Tend offered: to sink into deep work without wondering wha=
t was happening in Slack, then surface to find nothing had caught fire. But=
 Dan is a busy CEO pulled in enough directions that Tend=E2=80=99s ability =
to gather his communication gives him time and mental space back. I get an =
average of six to eight inbound Slack messages a day, which I can manage my=
self without much stress. For me, Tend proved to be a solution to a problem=
 I simply didn=E2=80=99t have.</p>
<p>With my Tend dupe, I was also building a workflow that would compete wit=
h my own deeply ingrained behavior. I have an anxious attachment style when=
 it comes to work, and checking Slack is my favorite sanctioned procrastina=
tion activity. I reliably check Slack 40 to 50 times per day=E2=80=94and ev=
ery time, I feel relief from the discomfort of writing, or the motivating h=
it of a fresh news drop. Attention Desk=E2=80=99s attempt to deliver peace =
of mind couldn=E2=80=99t compete with those rewards.    </p>
<p><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f45=
4c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2=
Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90e=
XBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3d3dy5zY2llbnRpZmljYW1lcmljYW4uY29tL2Fy=
dGljbGUvaG93LWxvbmctZG9lcy1pdC1yZWFsbHktdGFrZS10by1mb3JtLWEtaGFiaXQvIiwicG9=
zaXRpb24iOjEwfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Behavi=
oral psychology lore</a></u> says I should have given Attention Desk 21 day=
s to stick as a habit, but that data point turns out to be a myth. A 2024 <=
u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c61=
9eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3=
dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlI=
joicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3BtYy5uY2JpLm5sbS5uaWguZ292L2FydGljbGVzL1BN=
QzExNjQxNjIzLyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMX0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=
=3D"_blank">literature review</a></u> found that the few relevant studies s=
howed habit adoption medians around two months and ranges from four to 335 =
days. Three days may not have been long enough to create a habit, but it wa=
s plenty of time to discover I didn=E2=80=99t want to spend weeks or months=
 forming this one.</p>
<p>The research also has ideas about how I could <em>force </em>an AI workf=
low to stick. For instance, I could make an explicit plan: If I am about to=
 check Slack, then I will open Attention Desk. A 2024 meta-analysis coverin=
g 642 tests <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f876=
98e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQ=
UkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwi=
cG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2RvaS5vcmcvMTAuMTA4MC8xMDQ2MzI=
4My4yMDI0LjIzMzQ1NjMiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTJ9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" targ=
et=3D"_blank">found that clear plans</a></u> grounded in the specific conte=
xt where the behavior happens can help, especially when motivation is alrea=
dy strong. There=E2=80=99s the catch: An if=E2=80=93then plan can help me a=
ct on a goal that feels important and compelling. It cannot make me need a =
CEO dashboard or want to stop using Slack as an emotional-support procrasti=
nation device.</p>
<p>Attention Desk wasn=E2=80=99t defeated by my failure to perform the righ=
t behavior-design incantation. In hindsight I can see that I made a rationa=
l choice to abandon the tool; it was built for someone else=E2=80=99s probl=
em and pitted against a behavior that solved my own. </p>
<h2 id=3D"i-kept-building-tools-for-a-person-i-wasnt"><strong>I kept buildi=
ng tools for a person I wasn=E2=80=99t</strong></h2>
<p>Attention Desk has neighbors on the island, each built for a version of =
me that doesn=E2=80=99t exist. I set up a Codex automation to generate dail=
y X and LinkedIn post recommendations based on my work. I pictured myself e=
ffortlessly hitting the three-times-a-week activity cadence I saw recommend=
ed for LinkedIn.</p>
<p>Reader, I have never shared one of those posts.</p>
<p>In the cold light of day, I realized I=E2=80=99m a spontaneous, shoot-fr=
om-the-hip kind of social media user. When I have something to say, I=E2=80=
=99ll say it. Suggested content gives me another queue to fall behind on, a=
nd I emphatically do not want that. For me, the annoyance outweighs the val=
ue of posting more. Becoming an X poster extraordinaire will have to wait.<=
/p>
<p>In the 1980s, psychologists <strong>Robert Wicklund</strong> and <strong=
>Peter Gollwitzer </strong>described a phenomenon uncomfortably close to my=
 pattern of optimistically building tools for an aspirational version of my=
self: <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654=
f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29=
ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF=
90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3d3dy5zb2Ntb3QudW5pLWtvbnN0YW56LmRlL=
3B1YmxpY2F0aW9ucy9zeW1ib2xpYy1zZWxmLWNvbXBsZXRpb24iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTN9" re=
l=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">=E2=80=9Csymbolic self-completi=
on.=E2=80=9D</a></u> People who fall short of an identity they care about c=
an reach for its symbols, including tools and skills. Their experiments did=
 not test AI workflows, but still=E2=80=94building the machine that would m=
ake me a disciplined public thinker felt remarkably similar, for an afterno=
on, to being one.</p>
<p>I doubt I=E2=80=99m alone in carrying around these more motivated selves=
. They=E2=80=99re aspirational, like a workout outfit you buy optimisticall=
y. One takeaway from my exploration of these failed projects could be to em=
brace the Katie I am, not the one I hope to be tomorrow.</p>
<h2 id=3D"a-workflow-can-be-worth-building-and-not-worth-keeping"><strong>A=
 workflow can be worth building and not worth keeping</strong></h2>
<p>Longtime Working Overtime readers will remember <u><a href=3D"https://ev=
ery.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e=
18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90a=
GVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJo=
dHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3dvcmtpbmctb3ZlcnRpbWUvYWktd2FzLXN1cHBvc2VkLXRvLWZyZWU=
tbXktdGltZS1pdC1jb25zdW1lZC1pdCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxNH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener no=
referrer" target=3D"_blank">Margot</a></u>, the AI assistant I built to man=
age my other workflows. I set her up after seeing what people like <strong>=
Nat Friedman</strong> and <strong>Claire Vo</strong> were doing with OpenCl=
aw on an <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e=
654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkg=
V29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9=
zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL2V2ZW50cy9vcGVuY2xhdy=
1jYW1wIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE1fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_=
blank">Every camp on the topic</a></u>. I imagined an AI companion fine-tun=
ed to my needs could supercharge my day-to-day life.</p>
<p>Life with Margot was great for the first several weeks. After the =E2=80=
=9Cit=E2=80=99s alive=E2=80=9D moment and eager exploration of her Google C=
alendar navigation, she became more trouble than she was worth. For example=
, every time I tried to change the model powering Margot, she quit working =
altogether and I had to revive her. A month in, I realized I was spending m=
ore time maintaining Margot than I got back.</p>
<p>I left Margot behind, but her legacy carries on. Architecting her memory=
 taught me how important it is for agents to find the right context. That l=
esson fed into my <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e=
60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFN=
vbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6ND=
M0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3dvcmtpbmctb=
3ZlcnRpbWUvaS1hc2tlZC1hbi1haS10by1hdWRpdC1teS1vd24tY2FyZWVyIiwicG9zaXRpb24i=
OjE2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">current desktop=
 setup</a></u>, where tailored context helps Codex or Claude find what they=
 need.</p>
<p>Living as we do in the Wild West days of AI, we=E2=80=99re all being tas=
ked with figuring out what is and is not worth the effort to set up and mai=
ntain. There=E2=80=99s value to tinkering with these temperamental, wonky w=
orkflows because it makes us savvier users of the models and apps we work w=
ith everyday.  </p>
<h2 id=3D"the-workflows-that-stick-give-something-back-quickly"><strong>The=
 workflows that stick give something back quickly</strong></h2>
<p>As I was doing my post-mortem on my various failed AI habits, I consider=
ed the new behaviors that <em>have </em>stuck. Exhibit A: my compound writi=
ng plugin. </p>
<p>It=E2=80=99s a toolbox of skills I built for writing, modeled after <str=
ong><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f4=
54c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya=
2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90=
eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2NvcmEuY29tcHV0ZXIiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTd=
9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Cora</a></u> </strong>gene=
ral manager <strong><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f0=
5e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5I=
FNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6=
NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL0BraWVyYW5=
fMTM1NSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxOH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_bla=
nk">Kieran Klaassen</a></u></strong>=E2=80=99s compound engineering plugin.=
 It follows the steps of the writing process to help me take an article fro=
m idea to polished draft. </p>
<p>Compound writing had a few points against it, if we=E2=80=99re using my =
failures as precedent. I borrowed it from someone else, like Tend. It=E2=80=
=99s effort-intensive, like Margot. And it also has to compete with my esta=
blished Slack-checking habit=E2=80=94as I=E2=80=99ve said I will do <em>any=
thing </em>to avoid working on a draft. </p>
<p>And yet, compound writing has become the core of how I do my job.  </p>
<p>The difference is that compound writing quickly started sending signals =
back that my effort would be worth it. Even before it worked well, the plug=
in made writing feel propulsive again. It gave me a paragraph to push again=
st, a question that unstuck an argument, or a way into the next stage of a =
draft. It gave me a writing partner that solved a real problem in my work=
=E2=80=94the loneliness inherent to writing for a living. </p>
<p>In a series of studies, behavior researchers <strong>Kaitlin Woolley</st=
rong> and <strong>Ayelet Fishbach</strong> found that <u><a href=3D"https:/=
/every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc=
02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2h5IFNvbWUgQUkgV29ya2Zsb3dzIFN0aWNr4oCUQW5kIE=
90aGVycyBEb27igJl0IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM0OCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicHJvbW8iLCJ1cmwiO=
iJodHRwczovL3B1Ym1lZC5uY2JpLm5sbS5uaWguZ292LzI3ODk5NDY3LyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjox=
OX0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">immediate rewards</a>=
</u> predicted persistence better than delayed rewards. The research is not=
 proof that a behavior will become automatic, but it helps explain why I ke=
pt at it. Compound writing paid me in currency that mattered to me immediat=
ely. The distant promise of becoming a calmer Slack-checker could not compe=
te.</p>
<h2 id=3D"solving-ai-with-more-ai-again"><strong>Solving AI with more AI (a=
gain)</strong></h2>
<p>Looking across my AI workflows=E2=80=94the ones that failed and the ones=
 that stuck=E2=80=94I can see why each ended up where it did. Attention Des=
k solved a problem I mostly imagined for a person I hoped to become. Compou=
nd writing met me inside work I already did and began paying me back almost=
 immediately. </p>
<p>I could judge a workflow by what happened once it made contact with my d=
ay-to-day work=E2=80=94whether I resented maintaining it, carried away some=
thing useful from its failure, or kept returning to it. But the worry that =
I=E2=80=99m missing out on something hasn=E2=80=99t gone away. I wanted a w=
ay to know for sure if an experimental system would never work, or if it co=
uld be useful with a tweak to its design or implementation. </p>
<p>So, naturally, I added a new AI system to audit my AI systems. This time=
, a reviewer called Agent Ops. </p>
<p>Agent Ops is a plugin inside my Codex that inventories the systems I bui=
lt or adopted and asks me questions about the goal, usage, and the outcome =
of each one before making a recommendation to either keep, tweak, or retire=
 it. </p>
<p>In setting up the plugin, I discovered that my failed workflows fall int=
o four buckets: I forgot it existed, it failed to run, the output required =
too much maintenance, or it didn=E2=80=99t solve a recurring problem. </p>
<p>I turned those answers into provisional rules. A new automation has to r=
un manually three times before I schedule it. Its output has to produce som=
ething I use with less than five minutes of review. Four unused runs of an =
automation trigger a decision to change or retire it. </p>
<p>The prompt below is a version of the interview that produced it, adapted=
 so you can run it on your own Island of Misfit Workflows.</p>
