----------------------------------------



When AI tools started getting =
good, my team went on a prototyping binge.

I was leading the product team =
at Whoop, where every product manager had a backlog of ideas they=E2=80=99d=
 been dying to explore but hadn=E2=80=99t gotten past a whiteboarding =
session or a passionately worded document. Then tools like Bolt =
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGE=
gQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3=
N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ib2x0Lm5ldy8iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6NX0=3D and Replit =
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGE=
gQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3=
N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9yZXBsaXQuY29tLyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo2fQ=3D=3D came =
along, and suddenly, we could spin up prototypes of our ideas in an =
afternoon.

It felt like getting keys to the castle. Ideas that had been =
trapped in documents were finally showing up on screens. Except... were =
they any good?

We felt productive, unhindered, moving faster than ever. =
But people were making prototypes left and right, with no process for what =
happened next. Occasionally one would generate enough excitement that a =
team put aside their previous plans and built it. Mostly, though, there was=
 no consistent way to decide which prototypes were good, which ones should =
die, and what we were supposed to learn from them.

We were building faster, but we weren=E2=80=99t deciding better.


CHEAP PROTOTYPES CHANGE THE EQUATION

Prototyping has always played a =
well-defined role in product development: to prove or disprove your best =
ideas. You=E2=80=99d go deep on a problem, decide where to focus your =
efforts, look for evidence that customers wanted a solution, then decide =
whether to pursue it. Only then would you prototype=E2=80=94and by that =
point you were already pretty confident, so the prototype confirmed your =
hunch (or didn=E2=80=99t).

This made sense when building was expensive. If=
 a prototype cost a week of engineering time, you could only afford to =
prototype ideas you were already fairly confident about. The expense forced=
 discipline.

The cartoonist Matthew Diffee describes his creative process =
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGE=
gQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3=
N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZm9yYmVzLmNvbS9zaXRlcy9qZWZmYmVyY292aWNpLzIwM=
TIvMDMvMTcvaHVtYW4tZGVtby1uZXcteW9ya2VyLWNhcnRvb25pc3QtbWF0dGhldy1kaWZmZWUt=
c2hvd3MtaG93LXRvLWJlLWNyZWF0aXZlLyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo3fQ=3D=3D as laying 100 =
eggs in the sand and swimming off, like a sea turtle. Most won=E2=80=99t =
hatch, which is the point. Sketching is cheap, so he can draw it, send it =
to the New Yorker, and move on to his next idea.

AI has made prototyping =
similarly cheap for product teams. When the cost of building collapsed, two=
 things happened at once: Prototyping became accessible to anyone with an =
idea and a laptop, and the original reason to prototype=E2=80=94de-risking =
an expensive build=E2=80=94stopped making sense. But nobody stopped to ask =
what prototyping was for now.

So prototypes drifted into something else =
entirely. Without a clear framework for what they were supposed to help =
decide, they became pitches. Product managers built things to show what was=
 possible and get colleagues excited. Engineers, meanwhile, built whatever =
seemed cool on hack days. We had more prototypes than ever, but no better =
way to decide which ones were any good=E2=80=94and it got noisy.

Every prototype still demanded someone=E2=80=99s attention. Going from five=
 prototypes to 30 risks diluting focus across six times as many ideas, =
without making it any easier to choose.

So if building is no longer the =
bottleneck, the prototype has to move earlier. It=E2=80=99s no longer there=
 to answer, =E2=80=9CIs this the right solution?=E2=80=9D but, =E2=80=9CIs =
this even the right problem?=E2=80=9D That=E2=80=99s a harder question, and=
 you can only answer it by putting prototypes in front of users and =
watching what the data says.

Whoop had to work through it all: which =
problems were worth solving, how to test ideas against users instead of =
internal stakeholders, and what a product manager was even for once =
building got cheap. None of it was resolved quickly.


HOW WHOOP=E2=80=99S AI PRODUCT TEAM PROTOTYPES TODAY

After I left Whoop, the team kept pushing on the problem. I recently caught=
 up with Anjali Ahuja, who now leads the AI product team, to understand =
what had changed. With AI, she told me, anyone could spin up a prototype in=
 a day=E2=80=94and suddenly everyone was. But this was the hack-day trap =
all over again: plenty of demos, no understanding of what worked.

Anjali=E2=80=99s team began asking a different question of each prototype: =
What could we learn by putting this in front of members? The product team =
had tried to work this way before and kept hitting the same walls; they =
needed a roadmap to market or had to coordinate with other teams building =
in overlapping spaces=E2=80=94or they didn=E2=80=99t want experiments to =
make the existing product feel unfinished.

What finally worked was a =
process for deciding where to explore, what to measure, and who should see =
unfinished ideas.

First, they defined what success looked like before =
anyone started building. The team spent six weeks with a cross-functional =
working group=E2=80=94product, engineering, design, analytics, and data =
science=E2=80=94to define what AI should accomplish for users. Instead of =
starting with feature ideas like =E2=80=9Cbuild a sleep coach=E2=80=9D or =
=E2=80=9Cadd an AI chat feature,=E2=80=9D they focused on outcomes: Could =
AI help members capture more context about their lives? Could it help them =
understand their data in a more actionable way?

Once those pillars were =
clear, hack days got lanes. Engineers and product managers could still =
explore freely within any one of those pillars, but their prototypes had to=
 test whether AI could move that outcome.

The final piece was the beta =
group. Instead of demoing prototypes to stakeholders and collecting =
opinions, Whoop put rough versions in front of 12,000 members who opted in =
to test them, then watched what happened. Did a strength training prototype=
 get people to log more workouts? Did it help them train more consistently?=
 Did it make the product more useful?

A prototype that gets demonstrated =
to stakeholders generates opinions. One that gets used by external people =
generates data. Whoop=E2=80=99s beta group let the team see what willing =
testers did with rough prototypes without exposing every member to =
half-finished ideas. It let the team operate more like a growth =
team=E2=80=94testing more ideas than would have been possible =
before=E2=80=94while still giving leadership and cross-functional teams =
visibility into what was coming. The point was to learn faster, so that =
Whoop shipped only what improved members=E2=80=99 health outcomes.


LANES, NOT GUARDRAILS

This approach changes the product manager=E2=80=99s =
job in a way I don=E2=80=99t think most product leaders have fully reckoned=
 with.

When the technical frontier moves weekly https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGEgQmV0dGVyIFdhe=
SB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoi=
aHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by90aGVzaXMvaG93LXRvLWJ1aWxkLWEtdHJ1bHktdXNlZnVsLWFpLXB=
yb2R1Y3QiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6OH0=3D, product managers can=E2=80=99t keep up. =
The traditional flow=E2=80=94the product manager defines the problem, =
writes the specification, and hands it to engineering to =
implement=E2=80=94assumed that the product manager could know enough up =
front to tell the team what to build. That model collapses when the only =
way to understand the technology is to experiment with it.

=E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t feel like I can go to engineers with a set of =
requirements anymore,=E2=80=9D Anjali told me, =E2=80=9Cbecause I =
don=E2=80=99t even know what=E2=80=99s possible to be solved.=E2=80=9D

Previously, product managers were often forced to commit early because =
exploration was expensive. Now that exploration is cheap, the product =
manager=E2=80=99s job is less about having the answer and more about =
creating the conditions for the team to find the answer together.

This requires a different skill from writing a good product spec. You have =
to be able to see through an exciting prototype and ask: What assumption =
does this test? What would we learn by putting it in front of users? If we =
shipped it tomorrow, how would we know it worked? A lot of prototypes =
can=E2=80=99t answer those questions. They=E2=80=99re solutions in search =
of a problem=E2=80=94built because they could be, with no hypothesis about =
what would happen.

The hardest part of her job, Anjali told me, is =
creating an environment where engineers feel empowered to =
explore=E2=80=94hack days, blank canvases, creative freedom=E2=80=94while =
also making sure that exploration is pointed at something: =E2=80=9CHere =
are the five things we believe matter for our members. Go figure out if AI =
can help with any of them.=E2=80=9D Those are lanes, not guardrails.


STOP DROWNING IN DEMOS

I think what tripped us up at Whoop early =
on=E2=80=94and what I see tripping up most teams in my work teaching =
managers and product leaders how to use AI=E2=80=94is confusing building =
with learning. Seeing something take shape on the screen is energizing, and=
 it feels like you=E2=80=99re making progress. But artifacts aren=E2=80=99t=
 decisions.

The questions I now ask about any prototype are: What decision=
 will this help us make, and what=E2=80=99s the fastest way to get the data=
 needed to decide?

When building was expensive, the expense itself forced =
you to build with a clear purpose. Now that building is cheap, the =
discipline has to come from knowing which problems matter most, agreeing =
upon what success looks like, and listening to the people using the =
prototypes=E2=80=94the fundamentals that have always mattered. As a product=
 leader, your job is to help build the culture where your team practices =
this discipline.

AI has made it possible to pursue more ideas than ever =
before without committing to them up front. The challenge is to do it in a =
way that doesn=E2=80=99t cause chaos internally or for your users.

The teams that figure this out first will build fewer products and ship =
better ones. The rest will drown in demos.



