  <div class=3D"post-body">
    <p><em>We=E2=80=99re kicking off a series on =E2=80=9Cunlearning=E2=80=
=9D in partnership with Maven, the expert-led course platform. Over the nex=
t three weeks, you=E2=80=99ll hear three different takes from Maven instruc=
tors on what we need to let go of as AI changes how we work. First up is </=
em><strong><em>Hilary Gridley</em></strong><em>, who previously led product=
 at Whoop. She explains why faster prototypes don=E2=80=99t make product de=
cisions easier=E2=80=94and how teams can decide which ideas are worth build=
ing.=E2=80=94<a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698=
e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4=
gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGEgQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0Mz=
Q5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9Aa2F0ZV8xNzY3I=
iwicG9zaXRpb24iOjR9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Kate Lee=
</a></em></p>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>When AI tools started getting good, my team went on a prototyping binge.=
</p>
<p>I was leading the product team at Whoop, where every product manager had=
 a backlog of ideas they=E2=80=99d been dying to explore but hadn=E2=80=99t=
 gotten past a whiteboarding session or a passionately worded document. The=
n tools like <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87=
698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcg=
aW4gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGEgQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo=
0MzQ5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ib2x0Lm5ldy8iLCJwb3NpdG=
lvbiI6NX0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Bolt</a></u> an=
d <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454=
c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gRGVtb3M=
_IEhlcmXigJlzIGEgQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5LCJwb3=
N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9yZXBsaXQuY29tLyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo2f=
Q=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Replit</a></u> came =
along, and suddenly, we could spin up prototypes of our ideas in an afterno=
on. </p>
<p>It felt like getting keys to the castle. Ideas that had been trapped in =
documents were finally showing up on screens. Except... were they any good?=
 </p>
<p>We felt productive, unhindered, moving faster than ever. But people were=
 making prototypes left and right, with no process for what happened next. =
Occasionally one would generate enough excitement that a team put aside the=
ir previous plans and built it. Mostly, though, there was no consistent way=
 to decide which prototypes were good, which ones should die, and what we w=
ere supposed to learn from them.</p>
<p>We were building faster, but we weren=E2=80=99t deciding better.</p>
<h2 id=3D"cheap-prototypes-change-the-equation">Cheap prototypes change the=
 equation</h2>
<p>Prototyping has always played a well-defined role in product development=
: to prove or disprove your best ideas. You=E2=80=99d go deep on a problem,=
 decide where to focus your efforts, look for evidence that customers wante=
d a solution, then decide whether to pursue it. Only then would you prototy=
pe=E2=80=94and by that point you were already pretty confident, so the prot=
otype confirmed your hunch (or didn=E2=80=99t).</p>
<p>This made sense when building was expensive. If a prototype cost a week =
of engineering time, you could only afford to prototype ideas you were alre=
ady fairly confident about. The expense forced discipline.</p>
<p>The cartoonist <strong>Matthew Diffee</strong> describes <u><a href=3D"h=
ttps://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5=
ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gRGVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGEg=
QmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N=
0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZm9yYmVzLmNvbS9zaXRlcy9qZWZmYmVyY292aWNpLzIwMT=
IvMDMvMTcvaHVtYW4tZGVtby1uZXcteW9ya2VyLWNhcnRvb25pc3QtbWF0dGhldy1kaWZmZWUtc=
2hvd3MtaG93LXRvLWJlLWNyZWF0aXZlLyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo3fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopene=
r noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">his creative process</a></u> as laying 100 =
eggs in the sand and swimming off, like a sea turtle. Most won=E2=80=99t ha=
tch, which is the point. Sketching is cheap, so he can draw it, send it to =
the <em>New Yorker</em>, and move on to his next idea.</p>
<p>AI has made prototyping similarly cheap for product teams. When the cost=
 of building collapsed, two things happened at once: Prototyping became acc=
essible to anyone with an idea and a laptop, and the original reason to pro=
totype=E2=80=94de-risking an expensive build=E2=80=94stopped making sense. =
But nobody stopped to ask what prototyping was for now.</p>
<p>So prototypes drifted into something else entirely. Without a clear fram=
ework for what they were supposed to help decide, they became pitches. Prod=
uct managers built things to show what was possible and get colleagues exci=
ted. Engineers, meanwhile, built whatever seemed cool on hack days. We had =
more prototypes than ever, but no better way to decide which ones were any =
good=E2=80=94and it got noisy.</p>
<p>Every prototype still demanded someone=E2=80=99s attention. Going from f=
ive prototypes to 30 risks diluting focus across six times as many ideas, w=
ithout making it any easier to choose. </p>
<p>So if building is no longer the bottleneck, the prototype has to move ea=
rlier. It=E2=80=99s no longer there to answer, =E2=80=9CIs this the right s=
olution?=E2=80=9D but, =E2=80=9CIs this even the right problem?=E2=80=9D Th=
at=E2=80=99s a harder question, and you can only answer it by putting proto=
types in front of users and watching what the data says.</p>
<p>Whoop had to work through it all: which problems were worth solving, how=
 to test ideas against users instead of internal stakeholders, and what a p=
roduct manager was even for once building got cheap. None of it was resolve=
d quickly.</p>
<h2 id=3D"how-whoops-ai-product-team-prototypes-today">How Whoop=E2=80=99s =
AI product team prototypes today</h2>
<p>After I left Whoop, the team kept pushing on the problem. I recently cau=
ght up with <strong>Anjali Ahuja</strong>, who now leads the AI product tea=
m, to understand what had changed. With AI, she told me, anyone could spin =
up a prototype in a day=E2=80=94and suddenly everyone was. But this was the=
 hack-day trap all over again: plenty of demos, no understanding of what wo=
rked.</p>
<p>Anjali=E2=80=99s team began asking a different question of each prototyp=
e: What could we learn by putting this in front of members? The product tea=
m had tried to work this way before and kept hitting the same walls; they n=
eeded a roadmap to market or had to coordinate with other teams building in=
 overlapping spaces=E2=80=94or they didn=E2=80=99t want experiments to make=
 the existing product feel unfinished.</p>
<p>What finally worked was a process for deciding where to explore, what to=
 measure, and who should see unfinished ideas.</p>
<p><strong>First, they defined what success looked like before anyone start=
ed building.</strong> The team spent six weeks with a cross-functional work=
ing group=E2=80=94product, engineering, design, analytics, and data science=
=E2=80=94to define what AI should accomplish for users. Instead of starting=
 with feature ideas like =E2=80=9Cbuild a sleep coach=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9C=
add an AI chat feature,=E2=80=9D they focused on outcomes: Could AI help me=
mbers capture more context about their lives? Could it help them understand=
 their data in a more actionable way?</p>
<p><strong>Once those pillars were clear, hack days got lanes.</strong> Eng=
ineers and product managers could still explore freely within any one of th=
ose pillars, but their prototypes had to test whether AI could move that ou=
tcome. </p>
<p><strong>The final piece was the beta group</strong>. Instead of demoing =
prototypes to stakeholders and collecting opinions, Whoop put rough version=
s in front of 12,000 members who opted in to test them, then watched what h=
appened. Did a strength training prototype get people to log more workouts?=
 Did it help them train more consistently? Did it make the product more use=
ful? </p>
<p>A prototype that gets demonstrated to stakeholders generates opinions. O=
ne that gets used by external people generates data. Whoop=E2=80=99s beta g=
roup let the team see what willing testers did with rough prototypes withou=
t exposing every member to half-finished ideas. It let the team operate mor=
e like a growth team=E2=80=94testing more ideas than would have been possib=
le before=E2=80=94while still giving leadership and cross-functional teams =
visibility into what was coming. The point was to learn faster, so that Who=
op shipped only what improved members=E2=80=99 health outcomes.</p>
<h2 id=3D"lanes-not-guardrails">Lanes, not guardrails</h2>
<p>This approach changes the product manager=E2=80=99s job in a way I don=
=E2=80=99t think most product leaders have fully reckoned with.</p>
<p>When <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e6=
54f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRHJvd25pbmcgaW4gR=
GVtb3M_IEhlcmXigJlzIGEgQmV0dGVyIFdheSB0byBQcm90b3R5cGUiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ5=
LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by90aGVzaXMvaG93LXR=
vLWJ1aWxkLWEtdHJ1bHktdXNlZnVsLWFpLXByb2R1Y3QiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6OH0=3D" rel=3D=
"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">the technical frontier moves weekly=
</a></u>, product managers can=E2=80=99t keep up. The traditional flow=E2=
=80=94the product manager defines the problem, writes the specification, an=
d hands it to engineering to implement=E2=80=94assumed that the product man=
ager could know enough up front to tell the team what to build. That model =
collapses when the only way to understand the technology is to experiment w=
ith it.</p>
<p>=E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t feel like I can go to engineers with a set of r=
equirements anymore,=E2=80=9D Anjali told me, =E2=80=9Cbecause I don=E2=80=
=99t even know what=E2=80=99s possible to be solved.=E2=80=9D </p>
<p>Previously, product managers were often forced to commit early because e=
xploration was expensive. Now that exploration is cheap, the product manage=
r=E2=80=99s job is less about having the answer and more about creating the=
 conditions for the team to find the answer together.</p>
<p>This requires a different skill from writing a good product spec. You ha=
ve to be able to see through an exciting prototype and ask: What assumption=
 does this test? What would we learn by putting it in front of users? If we=
 shipped it tomorrow, how would we know it worked? A lot of prototypes can=
=E2=80=99t answer those questions. They=E2=80=99re solutions in search of a=
 problem=E2=80=94built because they could be, with no hypothesis about what=
 would happen.</p>
<p>The hardest part of her job, Anjali told me, is creating an environment =
where engineers feel empowered to explore=E2=80=94hack days, blank canvases=
, creative freedom=E2=80=94while also making sure that exploration is point=
ed at something: =E2=80=9CHere are the five things we believe matter for ou=
r members. Go figure out if AI can help with any of them.=E2=80=9D Those ar=
e lanes, not guardrails. </p>
<h2 id=3D"stop-drowning-in-demos">Stop drowning in demos</h2>
<p>I think what tripped us up at Whoop early on=E2=80=94and what I see trip=
ping up most teams in my work teaching managers and product leaders how to =
use AI=E2=80=94is confusing building with learning. Seeing something take s=
hape on the screen is energizing, and it feels like you=E2=80=99re making p=
rogress. But artifacts aren=E2=80=99t decisions.</p>
<p>The questions I now ask about any prototype are: What decision will this=
 help us make, and what=E2=80=99s the fastest way to get the data needed to=
 decide?</p>
<p>When building was expensive, the expense itself forced you to build with=
 a clear purpose. Now that building is cheap, the discipline has to come fr=
om knowing which problems matter most, agreeing upon what success looks lik=
e, and listening to the people using the prototypes=E2=80=94the fundamental=
s that have always mattered. As a product leader, your job is to help build=
 the culture where your team practices this discipline.</p>
<p>AI has made it possible to pursue more ideas than ever before without co=
mmitting to them up front. The challenge is to do it in a way that doesn=E2=
=80=99t cause chaos internally or for your users.</p>
<p>The teams that figure this out first will build fewer products and ship =
better ones. The rest will drown in demos.</p>
