Skills can link to other skills. That=E2=80=99s what makes orchestration =
possible: Each skill can hand off to the next, so you chain them into a =
full workflow. Claude finishes one task and then runs the skill =
you=E2=80=99ve pointed to. Here=E2=80=99s a breakdown of what=E2=80=99s =
going on in this skill workflow:

 * Inputs: A brief (topic, audience, =
goal) and a knowledge base (internal data, documents, sources)
 * Phase 1, template preparation: Define the blueprint (slide types, =
sections, flow) and clean up/standardize layouts, styles, and branding
 * Phase 2, framing: Define audience and success criteria, then do deep =
research on key questions and hypotheses.
 * Phase 3, research and =
decision-making (sequential): A six-step chain that runs in order to =
identify themes, evaluate options, segment details, analyze implications, =
synthesize insights, and map connections
 * Phase 4, content research =
(parallel): Deep-dive research split across multiple content sections (A =
through N) at the same time
 * Phase 5 and 6 (parallel): Front-of-deck =
(title, agenda, key messages, opening) built alongside back-of-deck =
(detailed analysis, data/evidence, appendices)
 * Phase 7, narrative =
polish: Write the storyline, transitions, and takeaways to tie it together.
 * Phase 8, visual assets: Create charts, diagrams, icons, and infographics=
.
 * Phase 9, assembly: Drop content into templates and check =
flow/consistency.
 * Phase 10, review: Quality pass on logic, accuracy, =
design, plus stakeholder feedback (with a loop back to assembly)
 * Phase 11, handover: Package the final deck with notes, rationale, and =
sources.
 * Outputs: Three deliverables come out of the bottom: the =
polished slide deck, a handover document, and an asset library.

Piece by piece, we built something that could stand on its own. Every time =
something failed, we spun it out as its own skill and A/B tested it until =
the issue disappeared. When the model researched the wrong sources, we =
rewrote the skill to direct it where a human would have looked. When the =
slide titles were too generic, we provided examples that captured the style=
 the client liked. When the right font colors were getting lost in assembly=
, Claude wrote a script to make that part more deterministic. Instead of =
running the entire pipeline=E2=80=94nearly an hour each time=E2=80=94we =
could isolate the problem, iron it out, and move on.

This level of complexity and investment isn=E2=80=99t necessary for the =
vast majority of organizations, but in this case, it was. The solution =
still isn=E2=80=99t perfect, even at this level of investment, but now our =
client=E2=80=99s team members can spin up 10 decks in an afternoon.


