<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>As a consultant, I spend a lot of time in PowerPoint. Data doesn=E2=80=
=99t drive decisions, narrative does, and, love it or hate it, a slide deck=
 presented on a glowing screen is the closest thing we have to our ancestor=
s gathering around a campfire to tell stories. </p>
<p>The slides I made early in my career were ugly on purpose to show that m=
y ideas were good enough=E2=80=94I didn=E2=80=99t need fancy formatting to =
convince. But if you won=E2=80=99t last long with that attitude in finance =
or consulting. </p>
<p>Clients see any lack of attention to detail as a sign that you can=E2=80=
=99t be trusted. Analysts pull all-nighters making slides pixel-perfect bec=
ause they could get fired for using the wrong font or logo. <strong><u><a h=
ref=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3=
f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN=
0IjoiQUkgQ291bGQgRG8gQW55dGhpbmcuIFRoZW4gSXQgTWV0IFBvd2VyUG9pbnQuIiwicG9zdF=
9pZCI6NDMyMywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vQG5hd=
GFsaWFfMjk0NCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo2fQ=3D=3D/1/0100019f13ec1823-ec827cfd-269d-4c=
5d-854a-491abef87169-000000/Td8pCbK8rxKRUzj9LwtkIPKx00AH202hEUqbcvbcN8A=3D4=
52" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Natalia Quintero</a></u><=
/strong>, who leads the consulting practice at Every, learned this the hard=
 way just weeks into her first job, when a company=E2=80=99s executive reje=
cted the presentation her team was making over colors that didn=E2=80=99t m=
atch. If the colors were sloppy, he reasoned, the numbers were too.</p>
<p>We=E2=80=99re no Goldman Sachs or McKinsey, but at Every, we still have =
to communicate competency in our presentations. At the same time, we wouldn=
=E2=80=99t be a very credible AI enablement partner if we weren=E2=80=99t u=
sing AI to help us. The challenge is that AI-created decks often don=E2=80=
=99t <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2=
Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2=
FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQUkgQ291bGQgRG8gQW55dGhpbmcuIFRoZW4gSXQgTWV0IFBvd2VyUG9pbn=
QuIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDMyMywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8veC5jb=
20vYmVhcmx5YWkvc3RhdHVzLzIwNDU5MDEyODQ1NTgxNjAzNDMvcGhvdG8vMSIsInBvc2l0aW9u=
Ijo3fQ=3D=3D/1/0100019f13ec1823-ec827cfd-269d-4c5d-854a-491abef87169-000000=
/Qxeg4YdXAcSjLKcmy2muqsQMdgV7Anw_hshzajnZnEo=3D452" rel=3D"noopener norefer=
rer" target=3D"_blank">tell a strong story</a></u>=E2=80=94and that lack of=
 narrative cohesion communicates the same thing as sloppy design: You didn=
=E2=80=99t care enough to pay attention to detail. </p>
<p>What follows is the story of our attempt to create the perfect PowerPoin=
t with AI. We started with Claude=E2=80=99s and Codex=E2=80=99s PowerPoint =
skills, but neither could automate the process to the level of quality we n=
eeded. So we built our own. If you=E2=80=99re creating enough presentations=
=E2=80=94or your quality bar is similarly high=E2=80=94it may be worth foll=
owing the same path.</p>
<h2 id=3D"claude-gets-close-but-cant-close-every-deal">Claude gets close, b=
ut can=E2=80=99t close every deal</h2>
<p>When I joined Every in February, our consulting team was still making al=
l our slides manually in Figma=E2=80=94about two to three decks per week. T=
he first thing I tried was asking Claude Code to create PowerPoint slides f=
or an upcoming presentation. </p>
<p>It didn=E2=80=99t go well:</p>
