ve offsite</a></u>, I made the slides by hand.</p>
<p>Still, by the middle of April, things were starting to improve. We were =
getting to the point where our decks were on brand and took half an hour to=
 correct, instead of the three-plus hours they used to take to create from =
scratch. </p>
<h2 id=3D"build-25-decks-make-no-mistakes">Build 25 decks, make no mistakes=
</h2>
<p>By the end of April, after stress-testing the workflow internally on doz=
ens of decks, we were ready to bring on outside help. We contracted with a =
company whose team creates roughly 25 sales decks a week. At that scale, au=
tomating the deck work made sense=E2=80=94it freed the team for higher-valu=
e tasks like business development. But these decks were going to serious sa=
les prospects, and a single mistake risked damaging trust with a potential =
customer.</p>
<p>At first, I started with Nityesh=E2=80=99s existing, blueprint-first sol=
ution and tried vibe coding in Claude Code to adapt it to our client=E2=80=
=99s template. Claude copied the PowerPoint skill we already had, read the =
client discovery call notes, and rewrote the existing skill to match the cl=
ient=E2=80=99s style and requirements. I had Claude run the newly updated s=
kill on two briefs the client had shared and compare its output against the=
 decks the client had actually presented to spot any discrepancies. We repe=
ated this process eight times. Each round, Claude proposed fixes for what i=
t found until it confidently declared that its version was better than the =
client=E2=80=99s original.</p>
<p>Then I opened one of the decks. It was a mess.</p>
<p>Slides were out of place. Text overlapped. Several headshots were even l=
abeled with the wrong names. When I asked what happened, Claude admitted th=
at it hadn=E2=80=99t looked at the presentations. Instead, it had written a=
 set of <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femail=
s%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e24=
2%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQUkgQ291bGQgRG8gQW55dGhpbmcuIFRoZW4gSXQgTWV0IFBvd2VyUG9=
pbnQuIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDMyMywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZX=
ZlcnkudG8vYWxzby10cnVlLWZvci1odW1hbnMvaG93LXRvLWdyYWRlLWFpLWFuZC13aHkteW91L=
XNob3VsZC1kNDU1N2M0Yy1iNDI3LTRjZmItYTA5Ny1kOWFhYWYwOTljZmYiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6=
MjF9/1/0100019f13ec1823-ec827cfd-269d-4c5d-854a-491abef87169-000000/YU6TygO=
5m4rMf3NvyXDwjWzoPLWT8WAyoySIOnLoc84=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" tar=
get=3D"_blank">evaluation metrics</a></u>=E2=80=94code that checked whether=
 specific changes had made it into the deck. But that code only verified co=
ntent, not appearance, so it completely missed problems like broken layouts=
. </p>
<p>I locked in, cleared my schedule, and started micromanaging Claude as we=
 unpacked each problem one by one. That process took three weeks and multip=
le rounds of client review. The result was a plugin built from 24 skills, r=
un in 11 distinct phases, and supported by 18 Python scripts. All told, it =
cost 28.9 million tokens and $62 to generate a single deck. Here=E2=80=99s =
an (anonymized, generalized) image of the final plugin structure:</p>
