<p><br></p>
<p>Skills can link to other skills. That=E2=80=99s what makes orchestration=
 possible: Each skill can hand off to the next, so you chain them into a fu=
ll workflow. Claude finishes one task and then runs the skill you=E2=80=99v=
e pointed to. Here=E2=80=99s a breakdown of what=E2=80=99s going on in this=
 skill workflow:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Inputs:</strong> A brief (topic, audience, goal) and a knowledge ba=
se (internal data, documents, sources)</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 1, template preparation:</strong> Define the blueprint (slide=
 types, sections, flow) and clean up/standardize layouts, styles, and brand=
ing</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 2, framing:</strong> Define audience and success criteria, th=
en do deep research on key questions and hypotheses.</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 3, research and decision-making (sequential):</strong> A six-=
step chain that runs in order to identify themes, evaluate options, segment=
 details, analyze implications, synthesize insights, and map connections</l=
i>
<li>
<strong>Phase 4, content research (parallel):</strong> Deep-dive research s=
plit across multiple content sections (A through N) at the same time</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 5 and 6 (parallel):</strong> Front-of-deck (title, agenda, ke=
y messages, opening) built alongside back-of-deck (detailed analysis, data/=
evidence, appendices)</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 7, narrative polish:</strong> Write the storyline, transition=
s, and takeaways to tie it together.</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 8, visual assets:</strong> Create charts, diagrams, icons, an=
d infographics.</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 9, assembly:</strong> Drop content into templates and check f=
low/consistency.</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 10, review:</strong> Quality pass on logic, accuracy, design,=
 plus stakeholder feedback (with a loop back to assembly)</li>
<li>
<strong>Phase 11, handover:</strong> Package the final deck with notes, rat=
ionale, and sources.</li>
<li>
<strong>Outputs:</strong> Three deliverables come out of the bottom: the po=
lished slide deck, a handover document, and an asset library.</li>
</ul>
<p>Piece by piece, we built something that could stand on its own. Every ti=
me something failed, we spun it out as its own skill and A/B tested it unti=
l the issue disappeared. When the model researched the wrong sources, we re=
wrote the skill to direct it where a human would have looked. When the slid=
e titles were too generic, we provided examples that captured the style the=
 client liked. When the right font colors were getting lost in assembly, Cl=
aude wrote a script to make that part more deterministic. Instead of runnin=
g the entire pipeline=E2=80=94nearly an hour each time=E2=80=94we could iso=
late the problem, iron it out, and move on. </p>
<p>This level of complexity and investment isn=E2=80=99t necessary for the =
vast majority of organizations, but in this case, it was. The solution stil=
l isn=E2=80=99t perfect, even at this level of investment, but now our clie=
nt=E2=80=99s team members can spin up 10 decks in an afternoon.</p>
<h2 id=3D"dont-fire-your-analyst">Don=E2=80=99t fire your analyst</h2>
<p>As our experiment with AI-generated slide decks hopefully showed, fully =
automating deck creation with AI isn=E2=80=99t worth it for most people. Mo=
st clients aren=E2=80=99t at the scale where they can justify proper skill =
optimization and quality control. Unless you=E2=80=99re doing hundreds of s=
imilarly shaped presentations a month, it probably isn=E2=80=99t worth it. =
Automate what you can and wait for the next model release, or for someone t=
o publish a better plugin or tool. </p>
<p>Alternatively, you could move away from PowerPoint=E2=80=99s complexity =
and toward HTML, which Claude is much better at. Another colleague in the E=
very consulting team went down this path and makes interactive HTML, CSS, a=
nd JS slides. This <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery=
