  <div class=3D"post-body">
    <p><em>Join me and </em><strong><em>Trevin Chow</em></strong><em> for o=
ur third compound engineering camp for paid subscribers next Friday, </em><=
strong><em>June 5</em></strong><em>. We=E2=80=99ll show how planning and bu=
ilding are collapsing into one flow=E2=80=94where you hand your AI a goal a=
nd it runs with it. <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f0=
5e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tc=
G91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYWRlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90=
eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vZXZlbnRzL2NvbXBvdW5kLWVuZ2l=
uZWVyaW5nLWNhbXAtMyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo1fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" =
target=3D"_blank">RSVP</a></u>.</em></p>
<p><em>Was this newsletter forwarded to you? <u><a href=3D"https://every.to=
/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e=
242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tcG91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYWRlIiwicG9=
zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vYW=
Njb3VudCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_=
blank">Sign up</a></u> to get it in your inbox.</em></p>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>In its early days, <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455=
f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29=
tcG91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYWRlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF=
90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vY2hhaW4tb2YtdGhvdWdodC9jb=
21wb3VuZC1lbmdpbmVlcmluZy1ob3ctZXZlcnktY29kZXMtd2l0aC1hZ2VudHMiLCJwb3NpdGlv=
biI6N30=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">compound engineer=
ing</a></u> was mostly about the code. I wanted to see if I could get an AI=
 model to make a plan, do the work the way I wanted it done, review the res=
ults against my standards, and incorporate lessons from my feedback so it w=
ouldn=E2=80=99t make the same mistake next time. The loop looked like this:=
 </p>
<p><strong>Brainstorm =E2=86=92 work =E2=86=92 review =E2=86=92 compound =
=E2=86=92 repeat</strong></p>
<p>That loop is still the core of how I build <strong><u><a href=3D"https:/=
/every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc=
02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tcG91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYW=
RlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHA6Ly9jb3JhL=
mNvbXB1dGVyIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjh9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_bla=
nk">Cora</a></u></strong>. But almost a year after we first <u><a href=3D"h=
ttps://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5=
ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tcG91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBV=
cGdyYWRlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8=
vZXZlcnkudG8vc291cmNlLWNvZGUvbXktYWktaGFkLWFscmVhZHktZml4ZWQtdGhlLWNvZGUtYm=
Vmb3JlLWktc2F3LWl0IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjl9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=
=3D"_blank">coined the term</a></u> compound engineering, the work phase ha=
s become boring=E2=80=94in the best way. If the plan is good and the agent =
has the right context, it usually does the work right. It writes the code a=
nd runs the tests. It fixes the obvious issues. The question now is: =E2=80=
=9CWhere do I fit in?=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>The answer is at both ends of the process. An analogy my collaborator on=
 the <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f=
454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tcG91bmQgRW5naW5l=
ZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYWRlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCI=
sInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZ2l0aHViLmNvbS9ldmVyeWluYy9jb21wb3VuZC1lbmdpbmVlcmluZy=
1wbHVnaW4iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTB9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blan=
k">compound engineering plugin</a></u>, <strong><u><a href=3D"https://every=
=2Eto/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f8769=
8e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e189=
66e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tcG91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYWRlIiwi=
cG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8veC5jb20vdHJ=
ldmluIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjExfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_b=
lank">Trevin Chow</a></u></strong>, came up with is a <u><a href=3D"https:/=
/every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc=
02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ29tcG91bmQgRW5naW5lZXJpbmcgR2V0cyBhbiBVcGdyYW=
RlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDI3OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlc=
nkudG8vY29udGV4dC13aW5kb3cveW91LXJlLXRoZS1icmVhZC1pbi10aGUtYWktc2FuZHdpY2gi=
LCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTJ9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">sandwic=
h</a></u>. AI is the stuff in the middle. Humans are the bread on either en=
d, holding it together. </p>
<p>At the beginning, I need to decide what is worth building. I need to und=
erstand the user, the product, the weird edge cases, and the thing that fee=
ls exciting enough to spend time on. Then I can hand the middle to the agen=
t. At the end, I come back in. I click around and look at the design. I rea=
d the copy. I ask whether the experience <em>feels</em> right. Sometimes ev=
erything technically works, but the product is still not good. So I make it=
 better. </p>
<p>As the models have grown more capable, the original compound engineering=
 loop started to feel incomplete. Plan, work, review, and compound still de=
scribes the core engineering cycle, but it leaves out the two places where =
I now spend most of my attention: before there is a plan, and after the wor=
k technically passes review.</p>
<p>So I expanded the loop:</p>
<p><strong>Ideate =E2=86=92 brainstorm =E2=86=92 plan =E2=86=92 work =E2=86=
=92 review =E2=86=92 polish =E2=86=92 compound =E2=86=92 repeat</strong></p=
>
<p>Ideate and brainstorm are the new front of the process. Polish is the ne=
w end. Compound is still the most important step, because the whole point i=
s that every feature should make the next feature easier.</p>
<p>I updated the compound engineering guide to explain the full system. The=
 guide is about engineering, but I think the pattern applies to knowledge w=
ork much more broadly. The middle of a lot of work will get automated. But =
if you want the work to be good, and if you want it to feel like yours, you=
 still need to be there at the beginning and the end.</p>
