    </div>
<p>Almost every line depends on something AI couldn=E2=80=99t know: how oft=
en you want to hear from friends, what you consider a genuine conversation,=
 or whether you=E2=80=99d rather miss someone than feel nagged. The answers=
 depend on how you live and connect with others. Swap the example for a wor=
kflow tool for your team, a review system, or a company knowledge base, and=
 the same is true=E2=80=94the AI can=E2=80=99t guess what=E2=80=99s unique =
to you.</p>
<h4 id=3D"2-ask-the-right-questions"><strong>2. Ask the right questions</st=
rong></h4>
<p>Setting useful constraints depends on asking questions that expose flaws=
 in the work. </p>
<p>When an AI agent produces 200 files for something I=E2=80=99m building=
=E2=80=94say, a website that accepts payments=E2=80=94my old instinct is to=
 inspect every file, study the sign-in code, and follow the payment process=
 from beginning to end. But at AI speed, reviewing every line can take long=
er than producing it.</p>
<p>Instead, I ask five questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>=E2=80=9CHow are you handling auth? Walk me through it.=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>=E2=80=9CWhat happens when a token expires mid-session?=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>=E2=80=9CWhat are the different payment failure paths?=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>=E2=80=9CWhat if Stripe returns a timeout?=E2=80=9D</li>
<li>=E2=80=9CThis needs 10,000 concurrent users. Where is the rate limiting=
?=E2=80=9D</li>
</ul>
<p>I know what to ask because I=E2=80=99ve built these systems and debugged=
 the failures that come from skipping these questions. I also have the tech=
nical vocabulary to use for this context. The expertise is the same; I=E2=
=80=99m just applying it one level up, by reviewing decisions instead of li=
nes of code.</p>
<h4 id=3D"3-turn-your-taste-into-reusable-instructions"><strong>3. Turn you=
r taste into reusable instructions</strong></h4>
<p>I design courses and training programs on AI. After a decade in the fiel=
d, I can quickly spot where an AI-generated draft=E2=80=99s tone drifts or =
its structure loses the reader. Early on, I corrected each draft=E2=80=94bu=
t the same problems kept returning.</p>
<p>So I began turning that feedback into rules. In <u><a href=3D"https://ic=
u.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f8769=
8e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG8gU3RheSBB=
aGVhZCBvbiBBSSwgVGhpbmsgTGlrZSBhIERlc2lnbmVyIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM5OSwicG9zdF9=
0eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vYml0Lmx5LzRiT3B3VHQiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MT=
d9/1/0100019fcd7c25bc-4281540b-7e2f-4417-a839-7a845ed50962-000000/4zEiaWe32=
sgrSj2e0p0gdyuve4vHV2DtfmA-2noiY44=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" targe=
t=3D"_blank">my courses</a></u>, each concept should build on what came bef=
ore. When I add that instruction to the prompt, the drafts become more cohe=
rent. Instead of correcting the same mistake repeatedly, I=E2=80=99ve made =
one piece of my judgment reusable by my team and our AI agents.</p>
<h4 id=3D"4-start-with-the-problem-instead-of-the-tool"><strong>4. Start wi=
th the problem instead of the tool</strong></h4>
<p>Instead of asking, =E2=80=9CShould I learn this?=E2=80=9D ask, =E2=80=9C=
Does this solve a real problem in work I understand well?=E2=80=9D I also a=
sk <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fc=
lick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2Fe=
yJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG8gU3RheSBBaGVhZCBvbiBBSSwgVGhpbmsgTGlrZSBhIERlc2lnbmVyIiwi=
cG9zdF9pZCI6NDM5OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8=
vZ3VpZGVzL2FnZW50LW5hdGl2ZSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxOH0=3D/1/0100019fcd7c25bc-4281=
540b-7e2f-4417-a839-7a845ed50962-000000/txAS482fXzGJh1jwY7oYwpLNRFPsYbPcbO1=
_DHhrWJ4=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">whether an AI=
 agent can use the tool</a></u>. I skip tools that take people a long time =
to learn or pull me back into low-level work. Those at the design layer sti=
ll learn new tools; they just choose them based on the problem.</p>
<h4 id=3D"5-create-feedback-loops"><strong>5. Create feedback loops</strong=
></h4>
<p>Unlike traditional software, <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/http=
s:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce6=
87adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG8gU3RheSBBaGVhZCBvbiBBSSwgVGh=
pbmsgTGlrZSBhIERlc2lnbmVyIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM5OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsIn=
VybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vY29udGV4dC13aW5kb3cvdGhlLWNhc2UtYWdhaW5zdC1za=
2lsbHMiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTl9/1/0100019fcd7c25bc-4281540b-7e2f-4417-a839-7a84=
5ed50962-000000/slsj4j81s3HRP60kwW7YUleSVYvDgZVZuP5qLy25MVA=3D452" rel=3D"n=
oopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">AI output can vary as models, inputs,=
 or needs change</a></u>. People set up an AI system, get decent results on=
 day one, and move on. Three months later, the quality has declined=E2=80=
=94and nobody knows why.</p>
<p>Every AI-generated output is feedback on the constraints and context tha=
t produced it. It=E2=80=99s only through building feedback into the process=
 that you catch that AI keeps making the same tone mistake or notice that a=
 template that worked for the first 10 engagements broke on the eleventh be=
cause the context changed.</p>
<p>Treat the design layer as a <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https=
:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce68=
7adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG8gU3RheSBBaGVhZCBvbiBBSSwgVGhp=
bmsgTGlrZSBhIERlc2lnbmVyIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDM5OSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInV=
ybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vdGVuZCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoyMH0=3D/1/0100019fcd7c2=
5bc-4281540b-7e2f-4417-a839-7a845ed50962-000000/vP4s6XNOALhD7smbWnJOSn74B5D=
i8K8vn_wLnBxJioo=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">loop<=
/a></u>: Set the constraints, review the results, update the system, and re=
peat.</p>
<h2 id=3D"becoming-a-designer">Becoming a designer</h2>
<p>The design layer expands your career. But only if you stop measuring you=
r value by what you produce. I arrived there almost by accident. Running a =
startup taught me to set direction, make the important decisions, and build=
 systems other people could follow. When AI agents became useful, I realize=
d they needed much of the same guidance. </p>
<p>I cover more ground now than I ever could as a solo builder. I design cl=
ient engagements and course curricula, run community programs, and manage i=
nternal operations=E2=80=94often in the same week. I define what good looks=
 like, and I build the systems that let someone else execute with my judgme=
nt baked in.</p>
<p>The only difference is that now the =E2=80=9Csomeone else=E2=80=9D is al=
so an AI agent.</p>
