<p>Sells and Knutsen use Gas City to build Gas City: Knutsen=E2=80=99s Atla=
nta-based server runs roughly 100 agents that merge around 50 pull requests=
 per day=E2=80=94the output of a small team=E2=80=94burning through roughly=
 a billion tokens per day, or equal to roughly one-fifth of the English-lan=
guage corpus on Wikipedia. </p>
<p><strong>What works:</strong> There are three ideas from the world of sof=
tware engineering that Gas City is built on and are worth internalizing, ev=
en if you never touch the toolkit. </p>
<ul>
<li>
<em>Dark factory versus light factory:</em> Parts of your work where humans=
 and agents talk to each other (planning, design, review) stay visible can =
be thought of as light, and parts where agents grind through clearly define=
d work on their own stay in the background, in the dark. As you gain trust =
in the agents=E2=80=99 output, you can move more of your process into the d=
ark. </li>
<li>
<em>One pet, many cattle:</em> The future of multi-agent engineering is lik=
ely organized with one persistent, named supervisor you talk to directly (G=
as City calls it the =E2=80=9Cmayor=E2=80=9D), who hands tasks to anonymous=
, disposable workers (the =E2=80=9Cpolecats=E2=80=9D) that do one job and s=
hut down, so they execute their job without getting lost in context or in e=
ach other=E2=80=99s way. Instead of managing one hundred agents individuall=
y, you manage one conversation while the mayor does the coordinating. </li>
<li>
<em>Multiple opinions on every code review:</em> Give the same code to <u><=
a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619ea=
fce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiSW5zaWRlIHRoZSAxMDAtYWdlbnQgU=
29mdHdhcmUgRmFjdG9yeSIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyNjYsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwi=
OiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3ZpYmUtY2hlY2svb3B1cy00LTciLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTR9" r=
el=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Claude</a></u>, <u><a href=3D"=
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiSW5zaWRlIHRoZSAxMDAtYWdlbnQgU29mdHdhcmU=
gRmFjdG9yeSIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQyNjYsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwcz=
ovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3ZpYmUtY2hlY2svdmliZS1jaGVjay1jb2RleC1vcGVuYWktcy1uZXctY29ka=
W5nLWFnZW50IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE1fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=
=3D"_blank">Codex</a></u>, and Kimi at the same time for review from multip=
le angles. Three different models catch different bugs than one model run t=
hree times.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What could be better:</strong> In Gas City, every task spins up =
a fresh agent session that doesn=E2=80=99t remember the earlier steps, so a=
gents waste cycles re-reading context that other agents produced and miss c=
onnections a single session would have caught. Cost is also a challenge: A =
six-step job can cost six times the cost of one Claude session, which adds =
up fast. The toolkit still feels experimental=E2=80=93=E2=80=93it took a ro=
om full of experienced engineers an entire day to get it running, even with=
 support from the instructors.</p>
<p>Beads, the task tracker powering the system, is built for agents first. =
It runs on the command line rather than as a visual dashboard, which is fin=
e for agents but harder for humans, who want to see everything at a glance.=
 So teams using Gas City in production typically pair it with Jira or Linea=
r=E2=80=94placing tasks in two places instead of one. </p>
<p>Additionally, Gas City was built on the assumption that AI models need h=
and-holding to stay on track, but models have gotten good enough that parts=
 of Gas City built to keep models on track, such as review loops to catch m=
istakes and mid-task check-ins to prevent agents from drifting, are now mos=
tly unnecessary. Finally, Gas City uses deliberately unfamiliar words to re=
fer to different inputs, actors, and workflows=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Cbeads=E2=80=
=9D for tasks, =E2=80=9Cpolecats=E2=80=9D for workers, =E2=80=9Crefineries=
=E2=80=9D for processing steps=E2=80=94so it can be confusing for a team ne=
w to the tech. </p>
<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> =F0=9F=9F=A8 <strong>Mike Taylor</strong>, hea=
d of tech consulting: =E2=80=9CLearn from the ideas. Skip the toolkit for n=
ow.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>If you=E2=80=99re already running more than 10 Claude Code sessions in p=
arallel and reading source code, Gas City is worth a look because it=E2=80=
=99s one informed opinion on how to handle that level of orchestration. For=
 everyone else, take the ideas and wait. <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/ema=
ils/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/=
eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiSW5zaWRlIHRoZSAxMDAtYWdlbnQgU29mdHdhcmUgRmFjdG9yeSIsInBvc3R=
faWQiOjQyNjYsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL29wZW5haS5jb20vaW=
5kZXgvb3Blbi1zb3VyY2UtY29kZXgtb3JjaGVzdHJhdGlvbi1zeW1waG9ueS8iLCJwb3NpdGlvb=
iI6MTZ9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">OpenAI=E2=80=99s Sym=
phony</a></u>, released a few weeks ago, is a more accessible, enterprise-r=
eady version of a similar idea: a written set of rules that turns your exis=
ting Linear board into the dashboard the agents work from. This is more in =
line with the way software engineers work now and doesn=E2=80=99t require t=
he behavior change that Gas City does. </p>
