<p>There=E2=80=99s a wrinkle, though: The tools we use to share and discuss=
 documents, such as Slack and Google Docs, were all built for Markdown and =
plain text. Slack previews a Markdown file in the message, whereas HTML sho=
ws up as an attachment you have to download. Google Docs threads and GitHub=
 diffs don=E2=80=99t know what to do with a self-contained HTML document. T=
he moment agents start producing HTML by default, our tools will need to ad=
apt to keep up. </p>
<h5 id=3D"what-to-do-this-week"><strong>What to do this week: </strong></h5=
>
<p>When you=E2=80=99re deciding between Markdown and HTML, ask whether the =
document is meant for humans or agents.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Markdown if it=E2=80=99s for humans. </strong>It=E2=80=99ll be edit=
ed, refined, or read by an agent that needs to parse it. Examples: AGENTS.m=
d, system prompts, project plans, briefs.</li>
<li>
<strong>HTML if it=E2=80=99s for agents.</strong> A human will consume it o=
nce and move on, or it benefits from visual structure. Examples: research s=
ummaries, weekly recaps, spec demos, dashboards.</li>
</ul>
