One thing I=E2=80=99ve learned =
as an amateur woodworker is that when a job is difficult to do =
precisely=E2=80=94or tedious to do repeatedly=E2=80=94you make a jig. A jig=
, by its broadest definition, is a tool that makes it easier to make =
something else.

If you=E2=80=99re building a bookcase with adjustable =
shelves, you need to drill two straight, evenly spaced rows of holes. If =
you=E2=80=99re making a set of drawers, you need to cut the same =
interlocking joints again and again. Jigs make jobs like these simpler, =
more accurate, or both. They constrain the position or movement of a tool =
or workpiece, making consistent results less dependent on a steady hand. A =
shelf-pin jig guides a drill from hole to hole; a dovetail jig guides a =
router through every joint. Jigs can be as simple as a block of wood or as =
elaborate as a custom-built metal guide.

Jigs are also finding their way =
into AI-assisted interface design. I first saw the term used in this way by=
 Josh Puckett https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c6=
19eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRGVzaWduaW5nIFdpdGggQUk_I=
