<h2 id=3D"why-not-just-prompt">Why not just prompt?</h2>
<p>Graphical interfaces were built to replace cumbersome command-line synta=
x with <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails=
%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242=
%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRGVzaWduaW5nIFdpdGggQUk_IE1ha2UgYSBKaWcuIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6=
NDQwMiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3LmNzLnVtZC5lZHUvdXN=
lcnMvYmVuL3BhcGVycy9TaG5laWRlcm1hbjE5ODNEaXJlY3QucGRmIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI1fQ=
=3D=3D/1/0100019fdda504d8-10688f35-6f72-4182-ae10-58e9476e8cd2-000000/7Hvp-=
74f5IeoCModV3ITPMQ5NEw3LA106vjqrqbKd8M=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" t=
arget=3D"_blank">direct, visible, reversible manipulation</a></u> of the th=
ing at hand. Designers are relearning the lesson with chat interfaces, disc=
overing that prompting is too coarse a method of adjustment compared with w=
hat they=E2=80=99re used to.</p>
<p>But that doesn=E2=80=99t mean simply replicating, whole cloth, the inter=
faces of existing design apps like Figma. =E2=80=9CThese tools have been ar=
ound for a while and have become of a certain shape because of the technolo=
gy that we have accessible to us,=E2=80=9D says designer <strong><u><a href=
=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95=
455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0Ij=
oiRGVzaWduaW5nIFdpdGggQUk_IE1ha2UgYSBKaWcuIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDQwMiwicG9zdF90e=
XBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd2F0dGVuYmVyZ2VyLmNvbS8iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6=
MjZ9/1/0100019fdda504d8-10688f35-6f72-4182-ae10-58e9476e8cd2-000000/59wDlmZ=
PKZFO0aNUNiSe8cXeP0Ai2gwVnQuAQX9Sev0=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" tar=
get=3D"_blank">Amelia Wattenberger</a></u>, a </strong>partner at Shutter H=
ill Ventures and former principal research engineer at GitHub Next. =E2=80=
=9C[Now] we=E2=80=99re working with different material, and we don=E2=80=99=
t yet have tools that make us feel like we=E2=80=99re in control of them be=
cause we=E2=80=99re still figuring out our relationship to them.=E2=80=9D</=
p>
<p>Wattenberger <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/https:%2F%2Fevery.to=
%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e=
18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiRGVzaWduaW5nIFdpdGggQUk_IE1ha2UgYSBKaWcuIiwicG9=
zdF9pZCI6NDQwMiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd2F0dGVuYmVyZ2=
VyLmNvbS90aG91Z2h0cy9jb2RlLWlzLWEtbWVkaXVtLWZvci10aG91Z2h0LyIsInBvc2l0aW9uI=
joyN30=3D/1/0100019fdda504d8-10688f35-6f72-4182-ae10-58e9476e8cd2-000000/9h=
PoRA246SRYDH1HIObN3YGhvEIUQBE13EGMCQLSywQ=3D452" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer=
" target=3D"_blank">imagines agents creating</a></u> a custom whiteboard or=
 playground as soon as you start working, complete with panels for any attr=
ibutes you might want to adjust. Jigs like these could bring back aspects f=
rom pre-AI workflows to our current ways of designing, like the kinds of im=
mediate feedback that allow for iteration and exploration. =E2=80=9CWhen yo=
u use a pencil to sketch, it=E2=80=99s this symbiotic thing where you sketc=
h and you=E2=80=99re looking at it and you=E2=80=99re reacting to what it l=
ooks like after you=E2=80=99ve sketched it,=E2=80=9D she told me. =E2=80=9C=
If you sit there and only think about [what you=E2=80=99re making], you=E2=
=80=99re only going to get so far.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>Until these jigs start appearing automatically, though, designers everyw=
here will just have to prompt their own.</p>
<p></p>
