, operations, or clinical. The tool has run roughly 260,000 conversations =
since developers committed its first lines of code on March 2.

General-purpose AI products are increasingly capable, but they often fail =
to meet an organization=E2=80=99s specific needs=E2=80=94and if a =
company=E2=80=99s compliance rules, data boundaries, and workflows are =
unusual enough, a vendor may not accommodate them soon enough or without =
unacceptable compromises. The challenge is knowing when to stop waiting and=
 build it yourself.


BALANCING SECURITY WITH UTILITY

The task force exploring AI options for Headway didn=E2=80=99t want to pay =
to train a model or compete with the labs on capabilities. Instead, they =
proposed building a harness=E2=80=94a custom wrapper for an existing AI =
model https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce6=
87adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIEhlYWx0aGNhcmUgQ29tcGFueSBUa=
GF0IEJ1aWx0IHRoZSBBSSBUb29sIEl0IENvdWxkbuKAmXQgQnV5IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDQ0Nywi=
cG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vc291cmNlLWNvZGUvY2x=
hdWRlLWNvZGUtaXMtdGhlLW9wZW5jbGF3LWFsdGVybmF0aXZlLXlvdS1hbHJlYWR5LWhhdmUiLC=
Jwb3NpdGlvbiI6OX0=3D that controls what it can access and do=E2=80=94with =
boundaries designed to meet Headway=E2=80=99s security and compliance =
requirements for handling personally identifiable and protected health =
information.

Every Eddy conversation runs inside a fresh Docker =
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIEhlYWx0aGNhcmUgQ29tcGFueSBUaGF0IEJ=
1aWx0IHRoZSBBSSBUb29sIEl0IENvdWxkbuKAmXQgQnV5IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDQ0NywicG9zdF=
90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3LmRvY2tlci5jb20vIiwicG9zaXRpb24iO=
jEwfQ=3D=3D container, a sealed, disposable workspace isolated from the =
rest of Headway=E2=80=99s systems. When a conversation ends, the container =
is destroyed.

That container is why the compliance team could live with =
the agent taking action without asking the user to approve each step. =
=E2=80=9CClaude Code can decide to erase the entire hard drive,=E2=80=9D =
Ferreri says. =E2=80=9CIt doesn=E2=80=99t matter. This is a throwaway =
container of a conversation.=E2=80=9D When an agent pulls sensitive data =
for analysis, it works from a copy that=E2=80=99s wiped when the job ends: =
It can only read from Snowflake=E2=80=94the company=E2=80=99s data =
warehouse=E2=80=94never write; it cannot browse the open internet directly,=
 though it can use web search through a strict proxy; and it can=E2=80=99t =
send emails.

The worst-case scenario would be a prompt injection attack, =
where a bad actor hides instructions inside content the AI is asked to =
read=E2=80=94an email, for example=E2=80=94telling it to collect sensitive =
company data and send it elsewhere. Headway=E2=80=99s egress policy is =
designed to prevent even an agent with access to sensitive internal data =
from sending it out, Ferreri says.


HOW EDDY ESCAPED ENGINEERING

Ferreri originally planned to run a 10-person alpha in the first week. By =
week=E2=80=99s end, 30 or 40 people were knocking down his door to try Eddy=
. =E2=80=9CI had to hold people off,=E2=80=9D he says.

The features weren=E2=80=99t ready, and sending Eddy into the wild =
prematurely could damage trust. So Ferreri=E2=80=99s team laid the =
groundwork for Eddy to spread organically when they opened it to the =
company=E2=80=94no managers forcing employees to use it. Two key product =
decisions drove adoption.

First, Headway added connectors that use Model =
Context Protocol (MCP)=E2=80=94an open standard for linking AI applications=
 to external tools and data=E2=80=94so Eddy could work with software that =
employees already used. A connector for Figma lets an agent read and edit =
design files; one for Snowflake lets it query the data warehouse.

A product manager can ask Eddy to draft a product requirements document and=
 have it pull context from internal Slack threads and Google Drive. A data =
scientist can ask for a state-of-the-union dashboard, and Eddy can run =
Snowflake queries, generate an HTML artifact https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIEhlYWx0aGNhcmUgQ29tcGFueSBUaGF0IEJ1aWx0IHRoZSBBS=
SBUb29sIEl0IENvdWxkbuKAmXQgQnV5IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDQ0NywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9z=
dCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vY29udGV4dC13aW5kb3cvaW5zaWRlLXRoZS0xMDA=
tYWdlbnQtc29mdHdhcmUtZmFjdG9yeSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMX0=3D, and write the =
narrative around the numbers.

Figma and Snowflake MCPs exist for agents =
like Claude and Codex, but larger AI vendors can=E2=80=99t sell you the =
exact combination of custom connectors, permissions, and clinical access =
rules that a complex company like Headway needs. Because Headway knows its =
own data, departments, and risks, it can organize that context into a =
coherent system that satisfies its legal and compliance teams.

Eddy=E2=80=99s output also drove its spread. Over time, people stopped =
copying Eddy=E2=80=99s answers into Google Docs for patient billing =
investigations because its HTML artifacts were richer and easier to share. =
Then they wanted to comment on them the way they would on a document, so =
the team added inline commenting.

Those choices produced a remarkably =
sticky product for Headway=E2=80=99s built-in audience of roughly 900 =
coworkers. Headway later set an AI token usage goal to encourage adoption. =
But by then, a majority of the company was already using Eddy daily.


THE COST OF OWNERSHIP

Building Eddy gave Headway the product it =
couldn=E2=80=99t buy. It also left Headway responsible for work a vendor =
would normally handle: maintaining uptime, minimizing latency, responding =
to feature requests, and keeping pace with AI=E2=80=99s expanding =
capabilities. If the first week=E2=80=99s problem was holding users off, =
the problem three months in was keeping Eddy running.

By May, Eddy was experiencing outages about twice a week, and two engineers=
 were working full time on reliability. Ferreri says the system has since =
stabilized, with no major downtime, even as the team continues to ship five=
 to 10 features a day.

Startup time remains a problem. Because the system =
spins up a fresh container each time, a new Eddy conversation takes about =
30 seconds to begin. That=E2=80=99s manageable if the task is =E2=80=9Cgo =
build this feature=E2=80=9D and the agent sets to work for 20 minutes, but =
frustrating when someone wants a quick answer.

Keeping up with new AI =
capabilities is another challenge. Because Eddy has its own interface, =
features from outside AI tools don=E2=80=99t automatically become available=
 to Headway. If Cursor releases one, Headway has to decide whether to tell =
engineers to use Cursor or wait two weeks and build it into Eddy. So far, =
building in-house has made the most sense, but every outside innovation =
forces the team to reconsider whether its internal version is worth =
maintaining.

Another welcome problem is demand for more features. =
Engineering wants better Git integration, while product managers want =
richer product requirements document templates, and clinical teams need =
special data boundaries.

Each request makes sense alone, but Ferreri says,=
 =E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t want this to become a Christmas tree where every =
feature comes in, and then it just looks like nothing. Just because you can=
 doesn=E2=80=99t mean you should.=E2=80=9D

So far, Headway has decided to =
absorb the costs of building and maintaining Eddy. Making the same choice =
requires staffing the product, owning its reliability, choosing between =
stability and feature requests, and continually deciding whether to =
reproduce what outside vendors ship. Headway became its own AI vendor, with=
 the maintenance burden to match.


WHEN TO WAIT, BUY, OR BUILD

Not every company needs its own Eddy. Building is expensive, ongoing, and =
easy to get wrong. Most companies shouldn=E2=80=99t build internal AI tools=
 like this, says Mike Taylor https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f=
87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIEhlYW=
x0aGNhcmUgQ29tcGFueSBUaGF0IEJ1aWx0IHRoZSBBSSBUb29sIEl0IENvdWxkbuKAmXQgQnV5I=
iwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDQ0NywicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnku=
dG8vQG1pa2VfMjExNCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMn0=3D, head of technology consulting =
at Every. =E2=80=9CIf you can get away with off-the-shelf software, save =
yourself the maintenance costs and risk of guessing wrong. But if =
it=E2=80=99s core to your strategy to be ahead of the market on AI, =
sometimes you=E2=80=99ve got to roll up your sleeves and build =
what=E2=80=99s missing.=E2=80=9D The decision depends on what the =
constraint is, how quickly a vendor is likely to close the gap, what the =
company loses while it waits, and whether it is prepared to own what it =
creates. Headway=E2=80=99s experience suggests a three-part framework:

 * Wait when the missing capability is likely to arrive soon and the cost =
of delay is lower than the cost of building and maintaining a substitute
 * Buy when a vendor can meet the company=E2=80=99s security, data, and =
workflow requirements without forcing it to give up something strategically=
 important
 * Build when the constraint is durable, unusual, and important =
enough to justify owning reliability, security, training, and product =
decisions indefinitely

For companies that decide to build, here=E2=80=99s =
Ferreri=E2=80=99s advice:

 * Find the three or four engineers closest to =
the edge of AI and give them a quarter to explore rather than a fixed =
roadmap
 * Pair the group with an executive sponsor and bring legal, =
compliance, and security into the work early to establish what the product =
can access and do. But don=E2=80=99t give the group free rein in a vacuum.
 * Broaden access only after the team has built safe permission boundaries =
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIEhlYWx0aGNhcmUgQ29tcGFueSBUaGF0IEJ=
1aWx0IHRoZSBBSSBUb29sIEl0IENvdWxkbuKAmXQgQnV5IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDQ0NywicG9zdF=
90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vZ3VpZGVzL3NlY3VyaW5nLWFuL=
WFsd2F5cy1vbi1haS1lbXBsb3llZSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxM30=3D and an architecture =
it can reuse. An engineering-only pilot may be the responsible place to =
start. Eddy spread because Headway eventually made Eddy=E2=80=99s =
connectors, permissions, and artifacts useful outside =
engineering=E2=80=94not because every department received an unrestricted =
version on day one.
 * Connect the AI-native builders with everyone else. =
Management, documentation, training, examples, and internal distribution =
remain important for lasting adoption.

Headway=E2=80=99s stack =
isn=E2=80=99t more functionally sophisticated than what=E2=80=99s =
commercially available. Eddy=E2=80=99s advantage is its security setup, its=
 connections to Headway=E2=80=99s systems, and a product plan tailored to =
the company rather than a vendor=E2=80=99s other 10,000 customers. Waiting =
for a vendor to put those pieces together would have cost Headway more than=
 building Eddy. Other companies have to decide whether the tool they =
can=E2=80=99t buy is worth building=E2=80=94and maintaining=E2=80=94themsel=
ves.



