ner noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">to code with agents</a></u>. Telling peop=
le to always use the cheapest model possible means guessing whether it will=
 be good enough=E2=80=94and you can=E2=80=99t know whether a different mode=
l would have produced a better result. So I let go of tight spend controls =
and focused instead on codifying loose guidelines to help us make spending =
decisions in real time.</p>
<p>My parameters start with this aphorism: Responsible usage and cheap usag=
e are not the same thing=E2=80=94nor are high spend and waste. When I see b=
ig expenditures of credits or a newly minted token billionaire on our leade=
rboard, I try to react with curiosity rather than a hard limit. I reach out=
 on Slack to get more context. I ask three questions:</p>
<ol>
<li>What did it cost?</li>
<li>What did it buy us?</li>
<li>What did we learn?</li>
</ol>
<p>One teammate spent $480 in a single run to build the permissions structu=
re for a product we=E2=80=99re launching. That run was worth it; it stood u=
p necessary infrastructure for a revenue-generating product, taught the eng=
ineer techniques that made subsequent runs more efficient, and produced an =
insight our editorial team could publish. Another teammate spent roughly th=
e same amount in one day having <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every.to/CL0/http=
s:%2F%2Fevery.to%2Femails%2Fclick%2F7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce6=
87adce5ebefcc02e18966e242%2FeyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiT3VyIEFJIENvc3RzIEp1bXBlZCAyMzA=
gUGVyY2VudC4gSeKAmW0gTm90IFNldHRpbmcgVG9rZW4gQnVkZ2V0c-KAlFlldC4iLCJwb3N0X2=
lkIjo0NDExLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by90ZW5kI=
iwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE2fQ=3D=3D/1/010001a011576c78-ce98f7da-fc48-4399-965d-7605=
189aaf2c-000000/G7Iw1SAjQKBK5hbZf9wc7g_c-UCRsA1gtZ0OKr719uU=3D452" rel=3D"n=
oopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Tend</a></u>, Dan=E2=80=99s open-sour=
ce productivity tool, check email and Slack every 15 minutes. That one <em>=
wasn=E2=80=99t</em> worth it; we changed the cadence that day. Same spend, =
different answer. </p>
<p>My process is still case-by-case=E2=80=94and I expect it will be for a w=
hile. But after three months of navigating token spend, I=E2=80=99ve learne=
d four lessons for companies of our shape and size:</p>
<p><strong>Establish a circuit breaker. </strong>In our case, that=E2=80=99=
s a Ramp card limit and Slack notifications that keep total spending visibl=
e. When the card runs out, we choose whether to refill it and by how much. =
When we hit the limit, we pause and decide whether the work is worth what i=
t will cost to continue.</p>
<p><strong>Make usage visible. </strong>We gave everyone access to ChatGPT=
=E2=80=99s usage dashboard so they can see usage in real time, including wh=
ich team members are consuming the most credits. Visibility turns spend fro=
m a month-end surprise into a team learning loop. The engineer behind the $=
480 permissions run found ways to make future runs more efficient. The Tend=
 owner reduced how often it checked. When the cost, output, and lesson are =
visible together, expensive doesn=E2=80=99t automatically mean irresponsibl=
e. </p>
<p><strong>Earn the friction.</strong> Policies and decision gates introduc=
e friction when a team needs to move quickly. I see it as operations=E2=80=
=99 job to identify risk and demonstrate that a lighter guardrail <em>can=
=E2=80=99t</em> work before imposing heavier restrictions. We give the team=
 the tools and resources they need, and we expect them to act responsibly i=
n return. </p>
<p><strong>Make change as the evidence changes.</strong> If we ever see the=
 team slipping on their end of the bargain=E2=80=94usage nobody can explain=
, lessons nobody shares=E2=80=94we=E2=80=99ll roll back the autonomy and pu=
t a platform-level limit in place. We haven=E2=80=99t had to yet. We=E2=80=
=99re a 30-person company operating at the frontier, so we can afford to tr=
ust our team and react quickly for now. A larger or more regulated company =
may need more checks and limits sooner. The broader lesson is to introduce =
friction only after you=E2=80=99ve seen the problem it=E2=80=99s meant to p=
revent. </p>
<p>Where have these loose parameters gotten us? We spent $31,300 on OpenAI =
credits in July=E2=80=94$26,800 of that after the Sol fire drill. Our token=
 spend is high. But that cost is still lower than the cost of hiring enough=
 people to produce the same work: launching <u><a href=3D"https://icu.every=
