STEAL THIS WORKFLOW

WRITING WITH AI

For certain types of writing, like an end-of-Q2 sprint =
strategy document, head of growth Austin Tedesco is happy to have a =
well-harnessed agent generate as much of the text as possible.

For more personal projects, like his weekly Substack food newsletter, his =
process is different. He=E2=80=99s set up OpenClaw and Codex to serve as a =
thought partner and editor who helps him store, organize, and sharpen his =
thinking. Here=E2=80=99s his AI writing workflow:

 1. Create a standing =
writing file. Austin=E2=80=99s lives in Proof https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG9rZW4gVGlnaHRlbmluZyIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQzMTYsInBvc3Rfd=
HlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3Byb29mZWRpdG9yLmFpLz91dG1fc291cmNlPWV2=
ZXJ5d2Vic2l0ZSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoyNn0=3D, Every=E2=80=99s agent-native =
document editor, and has three sections: =E2=80=9CIdeas bank,=E2=80=9D for =
rough thoughts, =E2=80=9COutline,=E2=80=9D where ideas start to take a =
formalized shape, and =E2=80=9CDraft,=E2=80=9D where an outline is fleshed =
out into a draft essay.
 2. Collect half-formed thoughts. When an idea =
strikes, Austin sends it to his OpenClaw https://every.=
to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e1896=
6e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG9rZW4gVGlnaHRlbmluZyIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQzMTYsInBvc3Rfd=
HlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL2d1aWRlcy9jbGF3LXNjaG9vbCIs=
InBvc2l0aW9uIjoyN30=3D via text, even if it=E2=80=99s messy or half-baked. =
Because the agent is connected to Every=E2=80=99s AI-writing assistant =
Spiral https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce=
687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG9rZW4gVGlnaHRlbmluZyIsInBvc3Rf=
aWQiOjQzMTYsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3dyaXRld2l0aHNwaXJ=
hbC5jb20vIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI4fQ=3D=3D, which is trained on his tone and =
style, it can refine rough ideas into prose that matches his writing style.=
 He=E2=80=99s also hooked it up to his Substack archive, so it can pull =
relevant context from previous editions, like when he=E2=80=99s already =
mentioned a restaurant or if his position on a particular topic has changed=
.
 3. Knock out a draft. By the time Austin opens his laptop, his writing =
file already contains ideas he=E2=80=99s had throughout the week, organized=
 into a detailed outline. He then opens up the Proof doc in Codex=E2=80=99s=
 in-app browser with enough context to knock out a publishable post in one =
sitting.
 4. Monologue https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e=
654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVG9rZW4gVGlnaHRl=
bmluZyIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQzMTYsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3d=
3dy5tb25vbG9ndWUudG8vP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9ZXZlcnl3ZWJzaXRlIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI5fQ=
=3D=3D your way through writer=E2=80=99s block. While Codex handles the =
idea collection and helps with context and outlines, Austin mostly writes =
the prose himself. If he hits a wall, however, he=E2=80=99ll open Monologue=
, Every=E2=80=99s voice dictation app, and brain dump his way through. In =
these voice notes, he often acts as an editor, articulating where and how =
the draft doesn=E2=80=99t align with what he=E2=80=99s trying to articulate=
. From there, he has Codex take his thoughts, clean them up, add =
transitional phrasing, and incorporate them into the piece.

=E2=80=9CA lot of writers would say, =E2=80=98That=E2=80=99s AI bullshit,=
=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CBut those are my words. Monologueing =
is great for breaking writer=E2=80=99s block, and even better for crafting =
an outline.=E2=80=9D

Try it this week:

