Here are the highlights:

 * Microsoft wants to be the plumbing, not just =
the agents. Microsoft has spent 50 years building the platform layer =
underneath other people=E2=80=99s software, and Kevin wants the company to =
have that same role in the agentic web=E2=80=94helping solve the problems =
that come with connecting agents to tools and data.
 * An open agentic web doesn=E2=80=99t have to mean a less secure one. =
It=E2=80=99s often said that verticalized platforms, like Apple=E2=80=99s =
App Store model, can guarantee security because a central authority =
controls everything, while open ecosystems trade that control for =
permissionless innovation. Kevin argues this is a =E2=80=9Cfalse dichotomy.=
=E2=80=9D One of the things that excites him most is being able to build =
and ship things without needing anyone=E2=80=99s permission. He thinks =
it=E2=80=99s possible to get =E2=80=9Creal robust security=E2=80=9D in open=
 systems too=E2=80=94for example, by using AI agents that know the things =
you=E2=80=99re willing to share or not, and =E2=80=9Cthat have some kind of=
 knowledge of risk assessment,=E2=80=9D to police it.
 * Are you a =E2=80=9Creal=E2=80=9D programmer if you let an agent write =
your code? Kevin has heard versions of that question for 40 years, going =
back to woodworkers arguing over hand tools versus power tools. His answer =
now is the same as it=E2=80=99s always been: strong opinions about craft =
are great, but the discipline worth cultivating is staying curious about =
new tools rather than resisting them on principle. He still edits code in =
VI out of habit, even knowing =E2=80=9Cfor sure that is sub-optimizing part=
 of what I=E2=80=99m doing.=E2=80=9D

