<p>Here are the highlights:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Microsoft wants to be the plumbing, not just the agents. </strong>M=
icrosoft has spent 50 years building the platform layer underneath other pe=
ople=E2=80=99s software, and Kevin wants the company to have that same role=
 in the agentic web=E2=80=94helping solve the problems that come with conne=
cting agents to tools and data.</li>
<li>
<strong>An open agentic web doesn=E2=80=99t have to mean a less secure one.=
</strong> It=E2=80=99s often said that verticalized platforms, like Apple=
=E2=80=99s App Store model, can guarantee security because a central author=
ity controls everything, while open ecosystems trade that control for permi=
ssionless innovation. Kevin argues this is a =E2=80=9Cfalse dichotomy.=E2=
=80=9D One of the things that excites him most is being able to build and s=
hip things without needing anyone=E2=80=99s permission. He thinks it=E2=80=
=99s possible to get =E2=80=9Creal robust security=E2=80=9D in open systems=
 too=E2=80=94for example, by using AI agents that know the things you=E2=80=
=99re willing to share or not, and =E2=80=9Cthat have some kind of knowledg=
e of risk assessment,=E2=80=9D to police it.</li>
<li>
<strong>Are you a =E2=80=9Creal=E2=80=9D programmer if you let an agent wri=
te your code?</strong> Kevin has heard versions of that question for 40 yea=
rs, going back to woodworkers arguing over hand tools versus power tools. H=
is answer now is the same as it=E2=80=99s always been: strong opinions abou=
t craft are great, but the discipline worth cultivating is staying curious =
about new tools rather than resisting them on principle. He still edits cod=
e in VI out of habit, even knowing =E2=80=9Cfor sure that is sub-optimizing=
 part of what I=E2=80=99m doing.=E2=80=9D</li>
</ul>
<p>This is a must-watch or must-listen for anyone who wants to hear Kevin=
=E2=80=99s early case for the agentic web, and what it means now that the i=
nternet for agents he bet on is actually being built.</p>
