<h2 id=3D"signal"><strong>Signal</strong></h2>
<h4 id=3D"skills-could-be-making-your-ai-worse"><strong>Skills could be mak=
ing your AI worse </strong></h4>
<p>It might be time to examine your elaborate skill library. Skills are reu=
sable packages of instructions=E2=80=94and sometimes examples and tools=E2=
=80=94that load whenever they=E2=80=99re relevant to what you asked your AI=
 to do. They=E2=80=99re supposed to improve an agent=E2=80=99s performance.=
 The format, <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87=
698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2Ug=
QWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjo=
iaHR0cHM6Ly9hbnRocm9waWMuc2tpbGxqYXIuY29tL2ludHJvZHVjdGlvbi10by1hZ2VudC1za2=
lsbHMiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6Nn0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank"=
>popularized by Anthropic</a></u>, is all over X, where sprawling custom sk=
ill libraries are treated as <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c=
3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0=
IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJ=
wb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly94LmNvbS9oZXluYXZ0b29yL3N0YXR1cy8yMDM2ODYxMjgwOD=
U5MTI0MTAwIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjd9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blan=
k">status symbols</a></u>. </p>
<p><strong><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f8769=
8e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQW=
dhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoia=
HR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9AbWlrZV8yMTE0IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjh9" rel=3D"noopener no=
referrer" target=3D"_blank">Mike Taylor</a></u></strong>, Every=E2=80=99s h=
ead of tech consulting, thinks every skill should earn its place in your li=
brary with proof it improves outcomes. His argument: Frontier models are sm=
art enough that they=E2=80=99ve absorbed the need for most of the skills tr=
ending on social media. If a model can reason through something on its own=
=E2=80=94and <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87=
698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2Ug=
QWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjo=
iaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9zZWFyY2g_cXVlcnk9ZmFibGUiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6OX0=3D" re=
l=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Fable 5</a></u> or <u><a href=
=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687=
adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJ=
wb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by=
9jaGFpbi1vZi10aG91Z2h0L2hvdy1ncHQtNS02LWNoYW5nZXMta25vd2xlZGdlLXdvcmsiLCJwb=
3NpdGlvbiI6MTB9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">GPT-5.6</a><=
/u> likely can=E2=80=94adding extra instructions creates confusion, not cla=
rity. =E2=80=9CYou=E2=80=99re fighting the weights of the model by forcing =
it to do things your way instead of the way it was trained,=E2=80=9D Mike s=
ays. Any time you conflict with the model=E2=80=99s training, it=E2=80=99s =
more likely to make mistakes. All the additional text loaded from your skil=
l will also inflate costs, so you should make sure each skill you choose is=
 worth it.</p>
<p><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f45=
4c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdC=
BTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6L=
y9ldmVyeS50by92aWJlLWNoZWNrL3ZpYmUtY2hlY2stY2xhdWRlLXNraWxscy1uZWVkLWEtc2hh=
cmUtYnV0dG9uIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjExfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" targe=
t=3D"_blank">Skills</a></u> are still useful, Mike says, but only when you =
need the model to complete a workflow in a specific way, like producing a <=
u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c61=
9eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBTa2=
lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ld=
mVyeS50by9hbHNvLXRydWUtZm9yLWh1bWFucy9haS1jb3VsZC1kby1hbnl0aGluZy10aGVuLWl0=
LW1ldC1wb3dlcnBvaW50IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjEyfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferre=
r" target=3D"_blank">custom PowerPoint template</a></u> with instructions o=
n your brand style guide or referencing internal company data. Mike recentl=
y found when testing Fable 5 that some of the skills <u><a href=3D"https://=
every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc0=
2e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo=
0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by92aWJlLWNoZW=
NrL29wdXMtNC04LXZpYmVjaGVjayIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxM30=3D" rel=3D"noopener noref=
errer" target=3D"_blank">Opus 4.8</a></u> needed to avoid mistakes actually=
 <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c=
619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBT=
a2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9=
ldmVyeS50by92aWJlLWNoZWNrL2FudGhyb3BpYy1teXRob3Mtb3VyLWZhYmxlLXZpYmUtY2hlY2=
siLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTR9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">harme=
d the newer model=E2=80=99s performance</a></u>. It reminded Mike of his wo=
rk as a prompt engineer in 2023: =E2=80=9CWith GPT-3 we had to use all thes=
e hacks and magic words to get it to produce valid code. Then when GPT-4 ca=
me out, it followed instructions better, and our bag of tricks was no longe=
r necessary.=E2=80=9D</p>
<h5 id=3D"why-it-matters">
<strong>Why it matters:</strong> </h5>
<p>The data backs him up. <strong><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/cli=
ck/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJ=
qZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cG=
UiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmcvYWJzLzI2MDMuMTU0MDEiLCJwb3Npd=
GlvbiI6MTV9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">SWE-Skills-Bench=
</a></u></strong>, a research benchmark that tests whether agent skills mak=
e agents better at software engineering, tested 49 public software-engineer=
ing skills and found that 39 didn=E2=80=99t impact performance, while three=
 made things worse. At the same time, a large percentage of skills caused t=
he model to consume more compute without improving results. (The worst offe=
nder increased token use by 451 percent.) </p>
<p>Only seven skills improved outcomes, and according to the researchers, t=
hese successful skills all provided specialized guidance the model couldn=
=E2=80=99t otherwise supply, like financial-risk formulas or traffic-manage=
ment instructions.</p>
<h5 id=3D"what-it-means"><strong>What it means:</strong></h5>
<p>Skill utility has a shelf life. Instructions that patch a model=E2=80=99=
s blind spot can become redundant=E2=80=94or actively counterproductive=E2=
=80=94the moment a new version of the model absorbs that capability. The sk=
ills built to last are the ones that give the model information it couldn=
=E2=80=99t have known about your business or the way you work because it=E2=
=80=99s not public information: personal preferences about your writing sty=
le, a specific company template, internal company data, or an exact sequenc=
e of steps. When you do use skills made by other people, make sure they=E2=
=80=99re regularly updated and pruned by their author.</p>
<h5 id=3D"try-it-this-week-perform-a-skills-audit">
<strong>Try it this week</strong>:<strong> Perform a skills audit.</strong>=
 </h5>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Keep</strong> skills that provide private context, custom tool acce=
ss, personal taste, or a specific company workflow=E2=80=94things that peop=
le outside your company wouldn=E2=80=99t know.</li>
<li>
<strong>Retest</strong> skills that compensate for a general weakness or qu=
irk of a current model=E2=80=94these likely have a shelf life as the models=
 improve.</li>
<li>
<strong>Retire</strong> skills that don=E2=80=99t demonstrably improve resu=
lts. You can ask your favorite AI agent to run your prompt with a skill and=
 without, then compare the results.</li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"steal-this-workflow"><strong>Steal this workflow</strong></h2>
<h4 id=3D"evaluate-your-skills-to-make-sure-youre-getting-the-results-you-w=
ant"><strong>Evaluate your skills to make sure you=E2=80=99re getting the r=
esults you want</strong></h4>
<p>=E2=80=9CWriting lots of skills isn=E2=80=99t just productivity theater=
=E2=80=94you could be harming performance,=E2=80=9D Mike says. =E2=80=9CIf =
you=E2=80=99re going to create a skill, prove that it works.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s his approach for doing just that: </p>
<p><strong>Step 1. Define what your skill should accomplish by identifying =
examples of ideal outputs.</strong> When Mike built a custom <u><a href=3D"=
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIENhc2UgQWdhaW5zdCBTa2lsbHMiLCJwb3N=
0X2lkIjo0MzQ0LCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9hbH=
NvLXRydWUtZm9yLWh1bWFucy9haS1jb3VsZC1kby1hbnl0aGluZy10aGVuLWl0LW1ldC1wb3dlc=
nBvaW50IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"=
_blank">PowerPoint skill</a></u>, he started with two real-world examples o=
f strong human-made decks as a reference, a number that eventually expanded=
 to 15 or 20. =E2=80=9CThat=E2=80=99s the golden data set,=E2=80=9D he says=
. </p>
<p><strong>Step 2. Use the golden data set to inform how you create and mod=
ify your skill.</strong> Drill down on what you like about the set so you c=
an codify =E2=80=9Cgood=E2=80=9D into the skill=E2=80=99s instructions. Kee=
p running the same input to see if changes to your skill move the output cl=
oser to the golden data set. </p>
<p><strong>Step 3. Automate the evaluation process by focusing on one issue=
 at a time.</strong> Mike=E2=80=99s PowerPoint skill kept getting letter sp=
acing wrong, prompting him to create a large language model judge=E2=80=94t=
rained on examples of decks with good and bad spacing=E2=80=94that graded r=
esults on that one metric. The more you can dissect a subjective vibes-base=
d evaluation into narrow measurements, the more work you can delegate to th=
e model. </p>
<p><strong>Step 4. Run a sanity check.</strong> Give the model the same inp=
ut with and without your skill and see if using it meaningfully changes the=
 results. </p>
