<p><br></p>
<p><em>On customer calls.</em> A few days ago I recorded a 19-minute call w=
ith a user experiencing a lag in Monologue=E2=80=99s browser integration on=
 Mac. When we hung up, I opened Codex, and told the agent to pull the trans=
cript and find the root cause. It read the user=E2=80=99s description of th=
e issue, identified the bug, searched the codebase, and fixed it. I didn=E2=
=80=99t need to write a long prompt or a single line of code. Codex went st=
raight from the call transcript to the patch.</p>
<p><em>To crystallize ideas across recordings.</em> Over the past two weeks=
, I=E2=80=99ve been working through the distinction between active versus p=
assive work, which is the driving idea behind the Monologue Notes launch. I=
 captured fragments of my thinking while driving, in internal calls with my=
 team, and during conversations with early users.</p>
<p>Before Notes, writing an article pitch would have required a brain dump.=
 With Notes, I prompted Codex to =E2=80=9Cpull all my Monologue Notes where=
 I talk about active work and passive work, and put together a brief.=E2=80=
=9D It searched across about a dozen recordings, identified the through-lin=
es, and returned a compelling thesis=E2=80=94an argument I=E2=80=99d been c=
ircling for weeks, assembled from things I=E2=80=99d already said.</p>
<p>That argument is the basis for this article.</p>
