STEAL THIS WORKFLOW

TURN A BAD SUPPORT CHAT INTO BETTER AGENT INSTRUCTIONS

When Fin=E2=80=99s customer support agent handles a customer conversation =
incorrectly, CS manager Waqqas Mir doesn=E2=80=99t rewrite its entire =
support setup. Instead, he uses Codex to turn the mishandled chat into a =
targeted new instruction. Using this method, he=E2=80=99s added a rule =
specifying when the agent should escalate an angry customer to a human and =
an explicit instruction to patch a bug in which Fin would generate code for=
 a user if they asked=E2=80=94a guardrail Waqqas put in after he got Fin to=
 write him a file-conversion script during a test.

WAQQAS=E2=80=99S WORKFLOW:

 1. Connect Codex to Fin through the =
platform=E2=80=99s Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. Once authorized, =
the MCP lets Codex retrieve conversations from your Fin workspace. In a =
Codex prompt, enter the identification numbers for two or three chats where=
 Fin made the wrong call and ask Codex to pull the full conversations and =
diagnose what went wrong.
 2. Ask Codex where the instructions broke down. =
Follow up by asking, =E2=80=9CWhy did it not follow my instructions? Can =
you identify what is causing the confusion?=E2=80=9D Codex will identify =
missing, unclear, or contradictory guidance, and propose concise additions =
to fix the issue.
 3. Add the proposed rule and retest the scenario. The =
MCP connection allows Codex to analyze Fin data, but Codex cannot directly =
change Fin=E2=80=99s setup. Waqqas adds the new guidance himself, then runs=
 the same type of conversation again. If Fin repeats the mistake, he gives =
Codex that conversation=E2=80=99s chat ID and asks it to pinpoint which =
instruction is still causing the issue.

