<p></p>
<p>As the general manager of <strong><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/=
click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJz=
dWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTU=
xLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly93cml0ZXdpdGhzcGlyYWwuY29tLy=
IsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Spi=
ral</a></u></strong>, Every=E2=80=99s AI writing partner, I=E2=80=99m a <u>=
<a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619e=
afce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1=
Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR=
0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9jaGFpbi1vZi10aG91Z2h0L3RoZS10d28tc2xpY2UtdGVhbSIsInBvc2=
l0aW9uIjoxMX0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">=E2=80=9Ctw=
o-slice team.=E2=80=9D</a></u> I=E2=80=99m responsible for all aspects of a=
 product: the code, customer support, marketing, and product management. I =
could not do this job without Claude.</p>
<p>Claude Code has eliminated the drudgery of product management. The busyw=
ork that used to happen across 10 different apps now happens in a single ch=
at thread. I=E2=80=99ve come to view the work of product management through=
 the lens of this conversation=E2=80=94the conversation is the work.</p>
<p>These days, I experience what=E2=80=99s left of product management work =
in flow state=E2=80=94thinking through gnarly design problems, looking at i=
nteresting data, and talking to customers. <strong>Cat Wu</strong>, Claude =
Code=E2=80=99s head of product, recently <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/ema=
ils/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/=
eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo=
0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly95b3V0dS5iZS9QcGxtemxnRT=
BrZz9zaT15c3kwd3ZIa1RWRWt6WWllXHUwMDI2dD0xMDkyIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjEyfQ=3D=3D"=
 rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">said</a></u>, =E2=80=9CAs co=
de becomes much cheaper to write, the thing that becomes more valuable is d=
eciding what to write.=E2=80=9D </p>
<p>I wrote up the main skills that run my product management workflow <u><a=
 href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eaf=
ce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3=
QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0c=
HM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9ndWlkZXMvYWktcHJvZHVjdC1tYW5hZ2VtZW50LWd1aWRlIiwicG9zaXRp=
b24iOjEzfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">in a guide<=
/a></u>. Below, I trace how I arrived at those skills and reflect on post-A=
I product management and software.</p>
<h2 id=3D"write-the-roadmap-and-nothing-else"><strong>Write the roadmap and=
 nothing else</strong></h2>
<p>In my new role, the only product document I=E2=80=99ve written is the ro=
admap. Everything else=E2=80=94every PRD and every ticket=E2=80=94has been =
written by Claude.</p>
<p>Writing is thinking, so as a new general manager, I wanted to take my ti=
me drafting Spiral=E2=80=99s roadmap. I spent several days understanding th=
e product, usage trends, user feedback, and the market. I wrote about the p=
roblem Spiral can solve, how Spiral can solve it, and the features we=E2=80=
=99d need to build to deliver on it. I spent hours talking to several peopl=
e at the company who=E2=80=99d worked on previous versions of Spiral and we=
re current or former users of it themselves. (In the guide, I talk about th=
e new /ce:strategy skill in <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3=
f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0I=
joiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0=
X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9naXRodWIuY29tL0V2ZXJ5SW5jL2NvbXBvdW5=
kLWVuZ2luZWVyaW5nLXBsdWdpbiIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxNH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener norefe=
rrer" target=3D"_blank">compound engineering</a></u> that interviews you to=
 produce this document for your own product.)</p>
<p>After six drafts of the roadmap, I created a GitHub project and added it=
 as the project=E2=80=99s <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f9=
5455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0Ijo=
iQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3=
R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9lbi53aWtpcGVkaWEub3JnL3dpa2kvUkVBRE1FI=
iwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE1fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">=
README</a></u>. I=E2=80=99m already using GitHub to host all my code, so I =
figured I might as well use it for tickets as well, or as GitHub calls them=
, =E2=80=9Cissues.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>From there, I asked Claude to use the GitHub command line interface (CLI=
) to read the README and give feedback. We went back and forth on a few twe=
aks, and then I asked it to review the codebase and do a first pass of the =
tickets required to deliver the roadmap. Within a few minutes, Claude produ=
ced about 100 detailed tickets, each with strategic context, supporting dat=
a, acceptance criteria, and technical implementation notes.</p>
<p>To be fair, the roadmap I wrote was pretty detailed; Claude wasn=E2=80=
=99t hallucinating features. And it had access to a library of user feedbac=
k and recent usage reports (more on that below). But it was shocking to see=
 something that had previously taken me days or weeks get done by Claude in=
 minutes. It felt like the PM equivalent of vibe coding.</p>
<p>I=E2=80=99d previously prided myself on the absence of ambiguity in the =
tickets I produced for engineers, but this was next-level. Claude also prio=
ritized the work in an unbiased way. Sometimes, a product manager gets emot=
ionally attached to a certain feature idea for whatever reason. Claude, how=
ever, was ruthless in elevating the things that had the best shot at delive=
ring the vision and hitting our 2026 goals.</p>
<p>That doesn=E2=80=99t mean the tickets were all ready to be implemented. =
When I do pick up a ticket, I do a full review of the requirements before a=
sking Claude to implement it. This is a step where I still add some value. =
Claude=E2=80=99s first pass gets the feature right in broad strokes, but it=
 struggles with some aspects of data modeling, microinteractions, and edge =
cases. I often adjust specs to reflect the nuances of real usage patterns, =
while Claude seems to envision a perfectly rational user reminiscent of <u>=
<a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619e=
afce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1=
Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR=
0cHM6Ly93d3cucG5hcy5vcmcvZG9pLzEwLjEwNzMvcG5hcy4yNDA5NjQ2MTIxIiwicG9zaXRpb2=
4iOjE2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">pre-Kahnemani=
an economics</a></u>.</p>
<p>I don=E2=80=99t do sprints. I have five columns in the GitHub project: l=
ater, next, now, in progress, and done. Around once a day, I run a custom c=
ommand, /prioritize, and Claude does a sweep=E2=80=94checking for stale tic=
kets, confirming that =E2=80=9Cnow=E2=80=9D is this week=E2=80=99s work, pu=
lling anything urgent out of the backlog. </p>
<p>If I discover a bug or a user asks for a compelling feature, I tell Clau=
de to create a ticket. It gets a =E2=80=9Ctriage=E2=80=9D label and is sort=
ed in the next /prioritize run. If it=E2=80=99s a priority-zero issue, I go=
 straight to fixing it without creating an issue.</p>
<p>Over time, the GitHub project becomes the product=E2=80=99s working memo=
ry: a fluid, continuously prioritized picture of where things stand. I=E2=
=80=99ve claimed to work in an <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/=
7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZW=
N0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb=
3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9hZ2lsZW1hbmlmZXN0by5vcmcvIiwicG9z=
aXRpb24iOjE3fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Agile</=
a></u> fashion before, but in hindsight, I don=E2=80=99t think Agile was re=
ally possible until these new AI tools came out.</p>
