<h2 id=3D"the-pulse-command"><strong>The pulse command</strong></h2>
<p>The old way of understanding how customers were using your product was t=
o look at dashboards and run queries. You=E2=80=99d open Amplitude or Mixpa=
nel and get an overview: how many users, how often, how long, what features=
, what revenue. Setting these up took time; sometimes they required enginee=
ring work, competing with product updates for developer bandwidth.</p>
<p>These days, I don=E2=80=99t look at dashboards. I run a custom command, =
/pulse that delivers something closer to an analyst=E2=80=99s briefing than=
 a chart. The pulse command surfaces a range of metrics, including active u=
sers, chats/messages/drafts created, response times of key aspects of the s=
ystem, conversations graded one to five, and an anonymized sampling of use =
cases. And because Claude is a language model, it doesn=E2=80=99t just pull=
 numbers: It reads the text, grades every conversation, flags anomalies wit=
h a green or red dot, and explains what it found in plain English. </p>
<p>The command is just a Markdown file, so the format itself is easy to cha=
nge. I=E2=80=99ve adjusted it about 50 times since I built it. When a featu=
re ships, I add a line, and the next morning it shows up in the report.</p>
<p>Every pulse report lives inside a Claude thread. When a recent report su=
rfaced a bug driving down conversation scores, my next message in that same=
 thread was to fix it. I did not have to create a ticket, but was able to s=
olve it in the same conversation. Over time, Claude also learns the nuances=
 of the system and saves that to memory.</p>
<h2 id=3D"product-research"><strong>Product research</strong></h2>
<p>For all the magic of AI, there is no substitute for talking to users. Wh=
at people say about your product and how they try to use it is endlessly su=
rprising. Just when I think I=E2=80=99ve shipped the world=E2=80=99s most i=
ntuitive feature, a confused user will ask a question from an angle that wo=
uld never have occurred to me.</p>
<p>That said, there are elements of product research that Claude seriously =
elevates. Here=E2=80=99s one example: A big part of Spiral=E2=80=99s value =
proposition is reflecting the user=E2=80=99s writing style in the drafts it=
 generates. There=E2=80=99s a rich academic literature on stylometry, <u><a=
 href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eaf=
ce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3=
QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0c=
HM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9wL3RoZS1zY2llbmNlLW9mLXdoeS1haS1zdGlsbC1jYW4tdC13cml0ZS1s=
aWtlLXlvdSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoyMn0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_=
blank">the study of style</a></u>.  </p>
<p>I leaned on Claude to help me wade through the literature for findings r=
elevant to Spiral=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cstyle transfer=E2=80=9D approach. Usin=
g the <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654=
f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9=
yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidX=
JsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9naXRodWIuY29tL2JsYXppY2tqcC9hcnhpdi1tY3Atc2VydmVyIiwicG9za=
XRpb24iOjIzfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Arxiv mo=
del context protocol (MCP</a></u>), Claude was able to find a dozen recent =
papers about LLM stylometry. I read their abstracts, then read a handful in=
 full. I cited those papers in the <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/cl=
ick/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdW=
JqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxL=
CJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ldmVyeS50by9wL3RoZS1zY2llbmNl=
LW9mLXdoeS1haS1zdGlsbC1jYW4tdC13cml0ZS1saWtlLXlvdSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoyNH0=3D"=
 rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">article I wrote for Every,</=
a></u> and they=E2=80=99ve been directly informing the new style system I=
=E2=80=99m building in Spiral. It=E2=80=99s so cool to see academic citatio=
ns sprinkled across product requirements. For product work where you have a=
 real opportunity to differentiate, it=E2=80=99s worth going the extra mile=
 on research, which is now within reach.</p>
<h2 id=3D"what-saas-survives"><strong>What SaaS survives </strong></h2>
<p>AI should open up product management to more people=E2=80=94you don=E2=
=80=99t need formal PM training when the tool itself can teach you. If you =
don=E2=80=99t know what metrics to pick for your pulse equivalent, ask Clau=
de for recommendations. If you=E2=80=99ve never analyzed an A/B test, ask C=
laude how. If you=E2=80=99re not sure whether a feature will move the needl=
e, ask Claude to predict its impact. To paraphrase <u><a href=3D"https://ev=
ery.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e=
18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb=
3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ibG9ncy5udmlk=
aWEuY29tL2Jsb2cvZGF2b3Mtd2VmLWJsYWNrcm9jay1jZW8tbGFycnktZmluay1qZW5zZW4taHV=
hbmcvIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI1fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_b=
lank">Nvidia CEO </a></u><strong><u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/clic=
k/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJq=
ZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJ=
wb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly9ibG9ncy5udmlkaWEuY29tL2Jsb2cvZG=
F2b3Mtd2VmLWJsYWNrcm9jay1jZW8tbGFycnktZmluay1qZW5zZW4taHVhbmcvIiwicG9zaXRpb=
24iOjI2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Jensen Huang=
</a></u></strong>, AI is the easiest product in history to use, because if =
you don=E2=80=99t know how to use AI, just ask the AI.</p>
<p>I=E2=80=99ve cancelled several B2B subscriptions since moving my product=
 management work into Claude, which means I=E2=80=99m seeing the <u><a href=
=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687=
adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3QgTWF=
uYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cHM6Ly=
90ZWNoY3J1bmNoLmNvbS8yMDI2LzAzLzAxL3NhYXMtaW4tc2Fhcy1vdXQtaGVyZXMtd2hhdHMtZ=
HJpdmluZy10aGUtc2Fhc3BvY2FseXBzZS8iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6Mjd9" rel=3D"noopener no=
referrer" target=3D"_blank">SaaSpocalypse</a></u> play out in my own spendi=
ng decisions. Yet I=E2=80=99m building a SaaS product. How do I make sure S=
piral doesn=E2=80=99t get steamrolled by the frontier model providers?</p>
<p>I believe it=E2=80=99s possible for a SaaS product to survive if it has =
two main characteristics: </p>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Unique sources of critical data:</strong> my database, my analytics=
, my payment system=E2=80=94services that would be very difficult to rip ou=
t.</li>
<li>
<strong>Products with seamless agent integrations.</strong> Github, Stripe,=
 Posthog, and Logfire have played nicely with Claude. One service I inherit=
ed from my predecessor didn=E2=80=99t have an MCP, and it was swiftly cance=
lled.</li>
</ol>
<p>For Spiral, if we nail style transfer=E2=80=94an inherent limitation of =
heavily post-trained language models=E2=80=94Spiral becomes the unique sour=
ce of your written voice in an agentic world. That=E2=80=99s valuable and s=
ticky. Already, API chats outnumber web chats, a milestone that we reached =
three days after launching the agent that handles Spiral=E2=80=99s API requ=
ests. That means that users are not necessarily using Spiral in the Spiral =
app, but across their workflows. </p>
<p>Good product management is making something people want, to quote <u><a =
href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafc=
e687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiQ2xhdWRlIENvZGUgZm9yIFByb2R1Y3Q=
gTWFuYWdlcnMiLCJwb3N0X2lkIjo0MTUxLCJwb3N0X3R5cGUiOiJwb3N0IiwidXJsIjoiaHR0cH=
M6Ly93d3cucGF1bGdyYWhhbS5jb20vZ29vZC5odG1sIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjI4fQ=3D=3D" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Y Combinator</a></u>. Great prod=
ucts come from inspiration and ingenuity, things that tools and processes=
=E2=80=94no matter how good=E2=80=94won=E2=80=99t bring you. Perhaps the be=
st thing about this new agent toolset is that it gets rid of the busywork t=
hat saps creative energy. There=E2=80=99s more space now for daydreaming an=
d far-fetched ideas. Product management can now be fun.</p>
