<h2><hr class=3D"quill-line"></h2>
<h2>Alignment </h2>
<p><strong>Terminal pilled. </strong>Four months ago I opened the coding te=
rminal for the first time, and it felt like staring into a black box that m=
ight bite me. Now I=E2=80=99m a snob about using it instead of a desktop ap=
p.</p>
<p>I build dashboards for biotech companies in it. I pull clinical trial da=
ta and parse financial filings while asking AI to explain the business mode=
l to me like I=E2=80=99m 11, and then like I=E2=80=99m 15, and then like I=
=E2=80=99m a grownup. On top of all that, I run Ghostty as my blazingly fas=
t native terminal  so I can juggle multiple windows for different workstrea=
ms, and I feel like I=E2=80=99m in the Matrix.</p>
<p>I=E2=80=99m promiscuous about the models inside the terminal I use. It m=
ight be Claude one day, GPT the next, and whatever is new the month after t=
hat. But I will never leave the terminal. Codex and Claude Desktop and Cowo=
rk have built beautiful interfaces for exactly the work I do, and without e=
ven trying any of them, I=E2=80=99ve decided they=E2=80=99re inferior=E2=80=
=94maybe because they=E2=80=99re <em>too</em> easy to use. </p>
<p>The terminal gives me the sense that I passed through a threshold of fru=
stration most people won=E2=80=99t, and that=E2=80=99s worth the tiny slive=
r of superiority I feel when I use it. And sitting at a terminal makes me f=
eel like I belong with the people who know how to code, even though I don=
=E2=80=99t, really.</p>
<p>All it took was four months of use and a minor superiority complex, and =
I=E2=80=99ve become one of those people I used to wonder about=E2=80=94the =
ones who won=E2=80=99t try the new thing even when it might work better.=E2=
