  <div class=3D"post-body">
    <p><em>Was this newsletter forwarded to you? <u><a href=3D"https://ever=
y.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18=
966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5Miw=
icG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vYWNjb3VudCIsInBvc2=
l0aW9uIjo0fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Sign up</=
a></u> to get it in your inbox.</em></p>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">
<p></p>
<p>I=E2=80=99m a journalist and a communications expert. My job, in both ro=
les, is to find ideas that people haven=E2=80=99t yet put into words=E2=80=
=94the anecdote that could become a front-page story, the framing that coul=
d crystallize a founder=E2=80=99s philosophy into something a customer reme=
mbers. </p>
<p>In an hour interview with someone, it might not be until minute 45 that =
we start getting into the good stuff. In two hours, there may only be one t=
hing that stands out to me=E2=80=94a side story, a detail, some color. A li=
ttle piece of gold dust. An investor I=E2=80=99ve worked closely with calls=
 these =E2=80=9Cextraction sessions.=E2=80=9D I call the people who do them=
 well Socrates-as-a-service.</p>
<p>Those details and stories aren=E2=80=99t on the internet. They=E2=80=99r=
e not in any model. And the model hasn=E2=80=99t replicated yet how I pull =
them out of people. The gap between what AI can do and what a great human q=
uestioner can surface is still wide=E2=80=94and it=E2=80=99s the gap where =
the best stories live. If you don=E2=80=99t have some way to surface that i=
nformation in your organization, your brand and messaging are going to soun=
d like all the other twice-boiled content out there. </p>
<h2 id=3D"osakan-bread-and-the-wisdom-within"><strong>Osakan bread and the =
wisdom within </strong></h2>
<p>The stuff that I=E2=80=99m looking for has a name in management theory: =
=E2=80=9Ctacit knowledge.=E2=80=9D The term comes from scientist and philos=
opher <strong>Michael Polanyi</strong>, who defined it with the phrase, =E2=
=80=9CWe can know more than we can tell.=E2=80=9D It=E2=80=99s the expertis=
e and intuition that lives in our bodies and resists being turned into a do=
cument. </p>
<p>In a frequently cited <a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455=
f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29=
jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsIn=
VybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vbHVtc2EuaXQvc2l0ZXMvZGVmYXVsdC9maWxlcy9VVEVOVEkvdTk1L0xNN=
TFfSVRBX1RoZSUyMEtub3dsZWRnZS1DcmVhdGluZyUyMENvbXBhbnkucGRmIiwicG9zaXRpb24i=
OjV9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">1991 article</a>, Japan=
ese management expert <strong>Ikujiro Nonaka </strong>argued that while Wes=
tern companies excelled at =E2=80=9Cinformation processing,=E2=80=9D Japane=
se companies specialized in the =E2=80=9Ccreation of knowledge,=E2=80=9D th=
rough a feedback loop that turned tacit knowledge into a competitive advant=
age. His most memorable example: In the 1980s, the Osaka-based Matsushita E=
lectric Company was struggling to get the kneading right in a bread machine=
=2E They sent a software developer to a=
pprentice with a baker at a local hote=
l famous for its luscious loaves. The knowledge she brought back helped the=
 team perfect the dough-stretching technology inside the machine and ultima=
tely create a top-selling device. </p>
<p>I am sure that the lucky engineer asked the baker a lot of questions, bu=
t there was certainly a lot she absorbed just from watching. Indeed, Polany=
i argued that tacit knowledge exists outside of numbers or symbolic languag=
e=E2=80=94the kind of systemization that AI requires to ingest information.=
 </p>
<p>Many =E2=80=9Cbakers=E2=80=9D from whom we try to extract tacit knowledg=
e often don=E2=80=99t even know the depth of expertise they carry. And they=
 certainly couldn=E2=80=99t tell you what questions you need to ask to acce=
