<h2 id=3D"ai-as-an-imperfect-interlocutor"><strong>AI as an imperfect inter=
locutor </strong></h2>
<p>AI can do some of that questioning and, in some cases, do it well. At Ev=
ery, we have an AI agent ask us questions <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/em=
ails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242=
/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF=
90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vc291cmNlLWNvZGUvaG93LXdlL=
XJ1bi1hLTI1LXBlcnNvbi1jb21wYW55LW9uLWZvdXItYWktYWdlbnRzIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjl9=
" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">when we write OKRs</a></u>.=
 The agent has ingested Every=E2=80=99s company strategy and has context on=
 all the members of the organization. My colleague, <strong><u><a href=3D"h=
ttps://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5=
ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9p=
ZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vQGthdGl=
lLnBhcnJvdHQxMiIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=
=3D"_blank">Katie Parrott</a></u></strong>, has Claude interview her <u><a =
href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafc=
e687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiw=
icG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG=
8vd29ya2luZy1vdmVydGltZS93cml0aW5nLXdpdGgtYWktaXMtaGFyZGVyLXRoYW4teW91LXRoa=
W5rIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjExfQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_bla=
nk">before she writes an article</a></u>. Those notes become the basis of a=
n outline of the piece.</p>
<p>I would argue, however, that AI-driven extraction works well when the pa=
rameters are clear and the assignment structured, like writing an article o=
r a plan for software. If you=E2=80=99re looking to turn over a completely =
new rock, interview someone about something they haven=E2=80=99t spoken muc=
h about before, or run the kind of open-ended information gathering work th=
at happens when companies decide to rebrand. In those sessions, a chief mar=
keting officer or branding agency will spend time speaking to members of th=
e company and asking them open-ended questions about the business. The poin=
t is to keep things open, go wide, and see what comes up. </p>
<p>There=E2=80=99s a second problem: A human in the room can be surprised m=
id-conversation and abandon the plan=E2=80=94perhaps notice hesitation or d=
ig into a thread that wasn=E2=80=99t on the list. A prompt mostly can=E2=80=
=99t. When I elicit insight from someone, I am applying my judgment about w=
hat is a good story in real time=E2=80=94judgment that=E2=80=99s been honed=
 by years in news and communications. This mutual, live attention is someth=
ing AI can=E2=80=99t capture because it=E2=80=99s not in the room. </p>
<p>The obvious objection is that this is a moving target=E2=80=94context wi=
ndows and memory are improving to allow for more detailed, fluid conversati=
ons. <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f=
454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBT=
ZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8=
vZXZlcnkudG8vcC93aGF0LWlzLXRhc3RlLXJlYWxseSIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxMn0=3D" rel=3D=
"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Taste</a></u> won=E2=80=99t, howeve=
r, won=E2=80=99t. Someone still has to decide which detail out of a two-hou=
r conversation is the piece of gold dust.</p>
<p>Nonaka himself argued that the goal isn=E2=80=99t always to make tacit k=
nowledge fully explicit. Because tacit knowledge is so personal and often s=
o abstract, sometimes the right tool with which to communicate is a metapho=
r or an analogy=E2=80=94a form of language that can hold multiple ambiguous=
 meanings. Eliciting that kind of language from someone takes its own form =
of tacit knowledge: the skills of a Socrates.</p>
<h2 id=3D"steal-these-techniques"><strong>Steal these techniques </strong><=
/h2>
<p>So how can you surface those nuggets of gold? Despite the explosion of i=
nterview podcasts asking for multiple hours of your time, I find most hosts=
 are not great at asking questions. The format demands an arc=E2=80=94a jou=
rney=E2=80=94which is the opposite of what you want when you=E2=80=99re try=
ing to surface tacit knowledge. Real extraction zigs and zags, doubling bac=
k on itself and picking up something you said 20 minutes ago to pull a diff=
erent thread, following gold, not audience interest. </p>
<p>Here are the techniques that I keep coming back to: </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Warm people up. </strong>We open up more once trust is established.=
 I never skip the small talk at the beginning of a conversation, and I=E2=
=80=99ll often bring something we have in common: =E2=80=9CI saw you just s=
poke about X=E2=80=94I=E2=80=99ve been thinking about that too.=E2=80=9D NP=
R interviewer <strong>Terry Gross</strong>=E2=80=99s favorite icebreaker qu=
estion is, <a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e6=
54f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgY=
SBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBz=
Oi8vd3d3Lm55dGltZXMuY29tLzIwMTgvMTEvMTcvc3R5bGUvc2VsZi1jYXJlL3RlcnJ5LWdyb3N=
zLWNvbnZlcnNhdGlvbi1hZHZpY2UuaHRtbCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxM30=3D" rel=3D"noopene=
r noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">=E2=80=9C</a><u><a href=3D"https://every.to=
/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e=
242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9=
zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3Lm55dGltZXMuY29tLzIwMTgvMTEvMT=
cvc3R5bGUvc2VsZi1jYXJlL3RlcnJ5LWdyb3NzLWNvbnZlcnNhdGlvbi1hZHZpY2UuaHRtbCIsI=
nBvc2l0aW9uIjoxNH0=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Tell m=
e about yourself</a></u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f=
05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29j=
cmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInV=
ybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3Lm55dGltZXMuY29tLzIwMTgvMTEvMTcvc3R5bGUvc2VsZi1jYXJlL3=
RlcnJ5LWdyb3NzLWNvbnZlcnNhdGlvbi1hZHZpY2UuaHRtbCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxNX0=3D" r=
el=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">.=E2=80=9D</a> The question le=
ts the person you are speaking to take the lead and protects you as the que=
stioner from saying anything that might make them prickle while you are sti=
ll warming up. </li>
<li>
<strong>Ask a mix of general and specific questions. </strong>When <strong>=
Lenny Rachitsky</strong> <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95=
455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0Ijoi=
U29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCI=
sInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vcmV2aWV3LmZpcnN0cm91bmQuY29tL3JlbHVjdGFudGx5LWluZmx1ZW=
50aWFsLWluc2lkZS1sZW5ueS1yYWNoaXRza3lzLWRlbWFuZGluZ2x5LWNoaWxsLWxpZmUvIiwic=
G9zaXRpb24iOjE2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">reve=
aled the questions</a></u> he sends to his podcast guests in preparation fo=
r the podcast, this combination stood out. For example, he asks them, =E2=
=80=9CAnything you haven=E2=80=99t shared elsewhere that could be interesti=
ng to share in this forum?=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94a very general question, and =
=E2=80=9CWhat=E2=80=99s one pivotal moment in your career?=E2=80=9D=E2=80=
=94which asks the guest to pinpoint one turning point. To extract unverbali=
zed insights from someone, it helps to ask them to both think macro about t=
heir area of expertise as well as micro. </li>
<li>
<strong>Come back to thoughts and drill in. </strong>If a line of inquiry g=
oes nowhere, don=E2=80=99t abandon it=E2=80=94go back later and try again f=
rom a different angle. The first pass often loosens wisdom up.</li>
<li>
<strong>Repeat things back. </strong>Repeating what someone said often help=
s them process their thoughts further, and they will often add additional d=
etail they didn=E2=80=99t know they remembered. </li>
<li>
<strong>Detail, detail, detail. </strong>Specifics are where the real stuff=
 lives. How did that make you feel in that moment? Why do you think that wa=
y? </li>
<li>
<strong>Listen well. </strong>Pulitzer Prize-winning radio journalist <stro=
ng>Studs Terkel</strong> spent decades interviewing everyday people in Chic=
ago, and was <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87=
698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMg=
YXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh=
0dHBzOi8vdHJhbnNvbS5vcmcvMjAwMS9zdHVkcy10ZXJrZWwtaW4tY29udmVyc2F0aW9uLyIsIn=
Bvc2l0aW9uIjoxN30=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">describ=
ed by one subject</a></u> as offering =E2=80=9Ca state of being, it=E2=80=
=99s a way of attending to, attention-ing another person.=E2=80=9D That is =
what good listening looks like. </li>
<li>
<strong>Ask about squirrels. </strong>In his documentary about the debate s=
urrounding the death penalty, <strong>Werner Herzog</strong> <u><a href=3D"=
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiU29jcmF0ZXMgYXMgYSBTZXJ2aWNlIiwicG9zdF9=
pZCI6NDE5MiwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3LnlvdXR1YmUuY2=
9tL3dhdGNoP3Y9S1JDMWZrUG9hOG8iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTh9" rel=3D"noopener norefer=
rer" target=3D"_blank">interviews a death row chaplain</a></u> who, at the =
start of their conversation, delivers the polished answers he=E2=80=99s giv=
en 100 times about accompanying people in their final minutes. Then Herzog =
asks him about squirrels. Thrown off, he breaks down. The grief he feels ab=
out his job is laid bare. Ask people about the unscripted things.</li>
</ul>
<p>Study this. Collect great questions you like. Build prompts to borrow th=
ese techniques for structured AI-driven sessions if you want. </p>
<p>But the judgment underneath these habits remains harder to transfer. It=
=E2=80=99s its own form of tacit knowledge. And for now, it still belongs t=
o humans.</p>
