nkudG8vcC9saXZpbmctc29mdHdhcmU_bWV0ZXJlZF9wYXl3YWxsPTEiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6Mn0=
=3D">AI has made software disorienting. It doesn=E2=80=99t have to be.</a>
      </h2>
    <div class=3D"post-meta">
        <p class=3D"byline">by <a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3=
f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0I=
joiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVy=
bCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vcC9saXZpbmctc29mdHdhcmU_bWV0ZXJlZF9wYXl3YWxsPTE=
iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6M30=3D">Jack Cheng</a></p>
    </div>
      <figure>
        <img class=3D"post-cover" src=3D"https://d24ovhgu8s7341.cloudfront.=
net/uploads/post/cover/4115/full_page_cover_Friday_Piece.png" width=3D"600"=
>
          <figcaption>Midjourney/Every illustration.</figcaption>
      </figure>
  </div>

  <div class=3D"post-body">
    <p><em>Why do constant updates fill us with dread in some apps, while w=
e greet the daily evolution of an AI agent with more curiosity? </em><stron=
g><em><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f45=
4c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIi=
wicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkud=
G8vQGphY2tjaGVuZyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo0fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">Jack Cheng</a></em></strong><em>, Every=E2=80=99s senior ed=
itor, explores that tension through a clarifying distinction: =E2=80=9Ctool=
-like software,=E2=80=9D which we expect to be stable and consistent, versu=
s =E2=80=9Cliving software,=E2=80=9D which we expect to grow and adapt. Rea=
d on for his practical advice for builders of both.=E2=80=94<a href=3D"http=
s://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebe=
fcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSw=
icG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vb24tZXZlcnkva2F0ZS=
1sZWUtam9pbnMtZXZlcnktYXMtZWRpdG9yLWluLWNoaWVmIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjV9" rel=3D"=
noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Kate Lee</a></em></p>
<p><em>Was this newsletter forwarded to you? <u><a href=3D"https://every.to=
/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e=
242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXB=
lIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vYWNjb3VudCIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo2fQ=
=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Sign up</a></u> to ge=
t it in your inbox.</em></p>
<p></p>
<hr class=3D"quill-line">Lately, I=E2=80=99ve been wishing that more softwa=
re had a =E2=80=9Cfreeze=E2=80=9D button. <p></p>
<p>When pressed, the product would crystalize in its present state. The fea=
ture set would lock, and the interface would solidify, as if <u><a href=3D"=
https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce=
5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDE=
xNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vc3RhcndhcnMuZmFuZG9tLmNvbS=
93aWtpL0NhcmJvbi1mcmVlemluZyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo3fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener nor=
eferrer" target=3D"_blank">dipped in carbonite</a></u>. There would be no m=
ore new updates. No changes whatsoever.</p>
<p>I want this button because companies are loading apps with more and more=
 features, whether AI or the result of AI-accelerated development, making t=
he tools unrecognizable. The additions are even more jarring for apps that =
I only use occasionally, like Figma. There, a chat box now beckons to descr=
ibe my idea to make it come to life. A =E2=80=9CRecents=E2=80=9D toolbar ab=
ove it has buttons for Figma Sites, Figma Buzz, and Figma Make=E2=80=94all =
<u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c6=
19eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwic=
G9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3LmZpZ21h=
LmNvbS9ibG9nL2NvbmZpZy0yMDI1LXJlY2FwLyIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjo4fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"no=
opener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">launched last May</a></u>. A sidebar m=
odule encourages me to try an AI image- and video-generation product called=
 <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c=
619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwi=
cG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vd3d3LmZpZ21=
hLmNvbS9ibG9nL3dlbGNvbWUtd2VhdnktdG8tZmlnbWEvIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjl9" rel=3D"n=
oopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Figma Weave</a></u>=E2=80=94and which=
 I have to log into separately using my Figma account.</p>
<p>And here I am just trying to update the gradient on an app icon.</p>
<p>At the same time, my <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f954=
55f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiT=
Gl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6=
Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vZ3VpZGVzL2NsYXctc2Nob29sIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjEwfQ=3D=
=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Claw</a></u>, Pip, gets =
new releases almost daily. I wake up, and Pip suddenly knows kung fu=E2=80=
=94or if not kung fu, <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455=
f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl=
2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Im=
h0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vY29udGV4dC13aW5kb3cvZXZlcnktaXMtaGFsZi1hZ2VudC1ub3ciL=
CJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTF9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">how to d=
ream</a></u>. Sometimes, the same updates send me on <u><a href=3D"https://=
every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc0=
2e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9=
zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vcC9pLWhpcmVkLWFuLWFpLX=
RvLWRvLW15LWNob3Jlcy1ub3ctaS1tYWludGFpbi10aGUtYWkiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTJ9" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">daylong bug hunts</a></u>, locki=
ng me out of a product I rely on to help plan my week, coordinate my family=
 calendar, write code, and brainstorm marketing ideas for my friend=E2=80=
=99s <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f=
454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJl=
IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vdHVyby5=
jb20vdXMvZW4vY2FyLXJlbnRhbC91bml0ZWQtc3RhdGVzL21hcmluYS1kZWwtcmV5LWNhL2RlbG=
9yZWFuL2RtYy0xMi8zMzU2NjgiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTN9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"=
 target=3D"_blank">Delorean rental</a></u>. Still, I find myself wondering,=
 regularly, =E2=80=9CWhat new thing can Pip do now?=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>Why do I loathe change for the first case and forgive=E2=80=94or even em=
brace=E2=80=94it in the second? </p>
<p>It=E2=80=99s because the first case is software that I want to use for a=
 specific purpose. Half-baked AI features pumped out to appease investors m=
uddy that purpose, but so do legitimate additions, AI or not. Each new addi=
tion brings new functionality that seems neat on its own but, in aggregate,=
 transforms the overall product into something other than the tool I know i=
t to be.</p>
<p>On the other hand, software such as my Claw does not have a defined purp=
ose. I=E2=80=99m creating uses and applications as I go that might be entir=
ely different from how someone else is using the same technology, and it=E2=
=80=99s adapting to me just as much as I=E2=80=99m adapting to it. Its prop=
erties=E2=80=94and our relationship=E2=80=94are dynamic. </p>
<p>I=E2=80=99ve come to call the former group =E2=80=9Ctool-like software=
=E2=80=9D and the latter group =E2=80=9Cliving software.=E2=80=9D Living so=
ftware doesn=E2=80=99t just mean AI agents=E2=80=94though often there=E2=80=
=99s an agentic aspect to them. Both categories come with a set of expectat=
ions, and recognizing the differences in those expectations can explain my =
disorientation. For builders, it can also help us decide how and what to bu=
ild.</p>
<h2><strong>How we got here: A brief history of software development</stron=
g></h2>
<p>Software development cycles have been accelerating for decades. In the 1=
980s, nine years passed between MS-DOS and Windows 3.0, in part because sof=
tware was distributed physically, on floppy disks=E2=80=94and later, CD-ROM=
s. Customers had to <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f0=
5e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2a=
W5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0=
dHBzOi8vcXouY29tLzQ4NjM3OS9waG90b3Mtc2NlbmVzLWZyb20tdGhlLXdvcmxkd2lkZS1mcmV=
uenktb2YtbWljcm9zb2Z0cy13aW5kb3dzLTk1LXJlbGVhc2UiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTR9" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">go out of their way</a></u> to u=
pgrade, so major releases had to prove their value. The internet hastened t=
he tempo considerably. Tools like Rails and React scaffolded repetitive for=
ms and database connections, Amazon Web Services and GitHub let developers =
deploy code to millions remotely, and app stores made automatic updates the=
 default on billions of devices. But even as software went from a box on a =
shelf to something more like fluid pushed through a digital IV, it made sen=
se to bundle significant changes and release them infrequently, because the=
y took time and coordination to build.</p>
<p>Now, AI coding models have made it possible for a single developer <u><a=
 href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eaf=
ce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF=
9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vY2hha=
W4tb2YtdGhvdWdodC93aGVuLXlvdXItdmliZS1jb2RlZC1hcHAtZ29lcy12aXJhbC1hbmQtdGhl=
bi1nb2VzLWRvd24iLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTV9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D=
"_blank">to produce dramatically more code</a></u>. The review of this code=
 itself can be automated by AI, and the codebase can <u><a href=3D"https://=
every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc0=
2e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9=
zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vZXZlcnkudG8vZ3VpZGVzL2NvbXBvdW5kLW=
VuZ2luZWVyaW5nIiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" tar=
get=3D"_blank">learn from its mistakes</a></u>. Features can also be replic=
ated much more quickly=E2=80=94just point your coding agent at the thing yo=
u want to clone.</p>
<p>The result for end users is a lot of things we didn=E2=80=99t expect, an=
d in many cases didn=E2=80=99t want. The old, slower pace of development en=
sured that companies and teams thought long and hard about what features th=
ey wanted to ship and what would truly be useful to users. Today=E2=80=99s =
hyper-fast timelines=E2=80=94Anthropic and OpenAI rolled out OpenClaw-esque=
 features <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698=
e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiTGl2aW5nIFNvZnR=
3YXJlIiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDExNSwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8veC=
5jb20vZW1vbGxpY2svc3RhdHVzLzIwMzQ3ODAxMjc0MzE2ODg2ODQiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTd9"=
 rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">within weeks</a></u>=E2=80=
=94are pushing the builders of traditional software to capitulate to trends=
 or ship simply because they <em>can</em>.</p>
<h2><strong>Expectations, OpenClaw, and the undead</strong></h2>
<p>If I expect software to be a tool, I want it to do one or several things=
 and do it well. I want it to be consistent and stable. I don=E2=80=99t wan=
t my hammer to work only 92 percent of the time. Nor do I want my hammer to=
 become a chainsaw.</p>
