<h2>The new Sparkle: File organization on your terms</h2>
<p>I=E2=80=99ve been the general manager of Sparkle for a little over a yea=
r. In that time, I=E2=80=99ve tried a lot of approaches to AI file organiza=
tion that didn=E2=80=99t quite work. People wanted AI to handle the organiz=
ational heavy lifting, and they wanted to be able to change the file struct=
ure until it met their exact, often idiosyncratic specifications.</p>
<p>The old Sparkle managed clutter by creating a rigid file system for you.=
 The new Sparkle creates one <em>with</em> you. It analyzes your files and =
generates a custom system=E2=80=94but only as the starting point. From ther=
e, you can make as many changes as you want by chatting with Sparkle=E2=80=
=99s built-in agent, until the hierarchy feels right.</p>
<h2>But first: Spring cleaning</h2>
<p>Before organizing what matters, Sparkle helps you get rid of what doesn=
=E2=80=99t.</p>
<p>The median Sparkle user has around 5,000 files on their Mac. A large por=
tion of those=E2=80=94screenshots, installer DMGs, system cache, duplicates=
=E2=80=94is digital junk. So we=E2=80=99ve added a cleanup pass that runs b=
efore organization begins. From the chat window built into the new app, you=
 can ask Sparkle what=E2=80=99s in your trash, or tell it what you want gon=
e (=E2=80=9CClear my screenshots folder=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cremove anythin=
g over 1 GB I haven=E2=80=99t touched in a year=E2=80=9D). Sparkle will con=
firm you really want those files gone=E2=80=94and then move them to Trash, =
which gives you one last opportunity to rescue files before you delete ever=
ything. </p>
