</div></div>
<p><br></p>
<p>As models get better, they automatically handle smaller, concrete proble=
ms, allowing you to demand more from them.</p>
<p>Say you have a bug in your code. A lower-level frame, which requires you=
 to describe the problem in detail, would be to explain what=E2=80=99s goin=
g wrong and propose possible solutions. A higher-level frame allows you to =
get abstract: =E2=80=9CThere seems to be a problem, can you fix it?=E2=80=
=9D</p>
<p>As you climb the frame hierarchy, your role is less about communicating =
the mechanics of a problem and more about defining what the most important =
problem even is. In the coding example, the higher frame is powerful becaus=
e it allows for expansiveness. (=E2=80=9CThere seems to be a problem, can y=
ou fix it?=E2=80=9D might surface the same bug as the lower-frame prompt, o=
r it may find that bug <em>and</em> identify a far more significant archite=
ctural issue.)</p>
<p>The higher the frame, the more possible solutions unfold before you=E2=
=80=94and the more room to consider what constitutes a solution in the firs=
t place.</p>
