<h2>When trusting your tools becomes a bad thing</h2>
<p>One group that would understand this immediately: airline pilots.</p>
<p>In the 1990s, researchers <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c=
3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0=
IjoiV2UgTmVlZCB0byBUYWxrIEFib3V0IEFJIEF1dG9waWxvdCIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQxMTcsInB=
vc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL3d3dy50YW5kZm9ubGluZS5jb20vZG9pL2=
Ficy8xMC4xMjA3L3MxNTMyNzEwOGlqYXAwODAxXzMiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MTV9" rel=3D"noop=
ener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">studying automated cockpits</a></u> star=
ted noticing a strange pattern. Pilots with thousands of flight hours and l=
ives on the line sometimes followed incorrect automated recommendations, ev=
en when the instruments in front of them suggested something was wrong. The=
 automation had been right often enough that their brains stopped cross-che=
cking it with the same scrutiny.</p>
<p>A 2010 review of <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f0=
5e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2UgT=
mVlZCB0byBUYWxrIEFib3V0IEFJIEF1dG9waWxvdCIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQxMTcsInBvc3RfdHlw=
ZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2RvaS5vcmcvMTAuMTE3Ny8wMDE4NzIwODEwMzc2MDU=
1IiwicG9zaXRpb24iOjE2fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank=
">decades of automation research</a></u> described a larger pattern: The mo=
re reliable an automated system becomes, the more likely humans are to let =
it pass unchecked. When a system is usually right, your attention starts tr=
eating it as if it will keep being right.</p>
<p>AI is the most fluent automated system most of us interact with in a day=
=2E And fluency has its own trick. In=20=
1999, <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/em=
ails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242=
/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2UgTmVlZCB0byBUYWxrIEFib3V0IEFJIEF1dG9waWxvdCIsInBvc3RfaW=
QiOjQxMTcsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2RvaS5vcmcvMTAuMTAwN=
i9jY29nLjE5OTkuMDM4NiIsInBvc2l0aW9uIjoxN30=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" =
target=3D"_blank">a pair of psychologists</a></u> showed people identical s=
tatements in fonts that were either easy or hard to read. The easy-to-read =
statements were rated as more true. It was the same words and same claims, =
but the version that <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f=
05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2Ug=
TmVlZCB0byBUYWxrIEFib3V0IEFJIEF1dG9waWxvdCIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQxMTcsInBvc3RfdHl=
wZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJodHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3dvcmtpbmctb3ZlcnRpbWUvaG93LX=
RvLWtlZXAteW91ci13cml0aW5nLXdlaXJkLWluLXRoZS1hZ2Utb2YtYWkiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6M=
Th9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">went down smoother</a></=
u> was judged more accurate. Your brain takes =E2=80=9Cthat was easy to pro=
cess=E2=80=9D and misfiles it as =E2=80=9Cthat must be correct.=E2=80=9D</p=
>
<p>AI output goes down very smoothly. It=E2=80=99s grammatically polished, =
the tone is confident, and the clean formatting suggests something that has=
 already been edited. The polish lets your eyes glaze over. </p>
<p>Every model upgrade makes the illusion of right-ness worse. The outputs =
get cleaner. The formatting gets better. The reasoning looks more plausible=
=2E The tool makes fewer obvious mistak=
es, which means the mistakes that rema=
in are harder to see. You are reading something that looks finished, and yo=
ur brain=E2=80=94which has been filing =E2=80=9Clooks finished=E2=80=9D as =
=E2=80=9Cis correct=E2=80=9D since long before AI existed=E2=80=94obliges.<=
/p>
<h2>Why =E2=80=98I=E2=80=99ll review it=E2=80=99 is not a plan </h2>
<p>Before the repeat work snafu, I would have told you I was reviewing ever=
ything before sending anything. The document passed through my field of vis=
ion, I tweaked a phrase, caught one weird sentence, and felt the warm glow =
of editorial virtue. My brain filed that as reviewed.</p>
<p>The feeling of having reviewed is easy to produce. The act of reviewing =
is harder. You have to form your own view before the model gives you one, c=
heck the claims, and notice where the draft has made an assumption you do n=
ot share. You have to ask whether the sentence would still feel true if som=
eone screenshotted it and sent it back to you six months later.</p>
<p>We talk a lot about <u><a href=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f9545=
5f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiV2=
UgTmVlZCB0byBUYWxrIEFib3V0IEFJIEF1dG9waWxvdCIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQxMTcsInBvc3Rfd=
