td2l0aC1hZ2VudHMiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6Mzh9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=
=3D"_blank">compound engineering loop</a></u>=E2=80=94and use them myself u=
ntil they=E2=80=99re predictable. Only when I trust a flow do I hand it off=
 to the dispatch layer and stop watching. If you skip this step, you=E2=80=
=99ll have agents opening pull requests for work you=E2=80=99ve already fin=
ished and filing duplicate issues. The order of work is key: Build it, use =
it, trust it, and then orchestrate it.</p>
<h2><strong>Your folder is already an agent</strong></h2>
<p>I started this whole experiment trying to build a swarm. I ended up with=
 44 folders, each one with specialized context built through months of work=
, connected by a dispatch layer. </p>
<p>It=E2=80=99s not what I expected, but it works. You also have the buildi=
ng blocks to create the same thing. </p>
<p>If your project has a CLAUDE.md and some files in <span class=3D"quill-e=
xtendable-media" id=3D"undefined" data-source=3D'{"text":".claude/","type":=
"source","content":""}' data-type=3D"source" data-content=3D"" style=3D"bac=
kground-color: rgb(240, 240, 240); cursor: pointer;">=EF=BB=BF<span content=
editable=3D"false">.claude/</span>=EF=BB=BF</span>, you have an agent. You =
just haven=E2=80=99t been treating it like one.</p>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s an experiment for you: Look at your project folder. Is it=
 a generic setup or a specialist? If it=E2=80=99s generic=E2=80=94if your C=
LAUDE.md is boilerplate you copied from someone=E2=80=99s blog post=E2=80=
=94spend 30 minutes making it yours. Add your conventions, your patterns, y=
our opinions about how code should be written. Then try running two agents =
in separate git worktrees (separate copies of your codebase so they don=E2=
=80=99t interfere with each other) and notice where <em>you</em> slow thing=
s down. That=E2=80=99s where the dispatch layer needs to go.</p>
<p>I=E2=80=99m one step into that myself. I=E2=80=99ve moved from manually =
orchestrating=E2=80=94opening terminal tabs, navigating folders, and starti=
ng sessions=E2=80=94to having a dispatch layer do that routing for me. </p>
<p>The step after this is already arriving. Anthropic just launched <u><a h=
ref=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce=
687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiVGhlIEZvbGRlciBJcyB0aGUgQWdlbnQg=
IiwicG9zdF9pZCI6NDEwOCwicG9zdF90eXBlIjoicG9zdCIsInVybCI6Imh0dHBzOi8vcGxhdGZ=
vcm0uY2xhdWRlLmNvbS9kb2NzL2VuL21hbmFnZWQtYWdlbnRzL292ZXJ2aWV3IiwicG9zaXRpb2=
4iOjM5fQ=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_blank">Claude Manage=
d Agents</a></u>=E2=80=94a hosted service that handles sandboxing, state ma=
nagement, and tool execution so developers can focus on what their agents d=
o rather than how to keep them running. The folder-as-agent pattern makes t=
hat kind of managed autonomy possible: a trusted, specialized environment t=
he model can run inside without you holding its hand.</p>
<p>The industry is spending a lot of energy on autonomous swarms. I spent t=
hree months there too, and found that for now, the answer is still just a f=
older. </p>
