<h2>Inside Every</h2>
<h5>Motivating your AI employee</h5>
<p>Last Thursday, I opened Slack and saw a message from our AI project mana=
ger, Claudie, announcing that her trust battery with me had dropped 0.6 per=
cent to 28.3 percent. </p>
<p>The concept of a trust battery was coined by Shopify CEO <strong><u><a h=
ref=3D"https://every.to/emails/click/7c3f95455f05e60f87698e654f454c619eafce=
687adce5ebefcc02e18966e242/eyJzdWJqZWN0IjoiWW914oCZcmUgdGhlIEJyZWFkIGluIHRo=
ZSBBSSBTYW5kd2ljaCIsInBvc3RfaWQiOjQxMjAsInBvc3RfdHlwZSI6InBvc3QiLCJ1cmwiOiJ=
odHRwczovL2V2ZXJ5LnRvL3AveW91ci1jZW8tanVzdC1zYWlkLXVzZS1haS1vci1lbHNlLWhlcm=
Utcy13aGF0LXRvLWRvLW5leHQiLCJwb3NpdGlvbiI6MzJ9" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"=
 target=3D"_blank">Tobi L=C3=BCtke</a></u></strong>, and the idea is simple=
: All working relationships run on trust batteries, and every exchange impa=
cts their charge. When your trust battery with a coworker is high, they rel=
y on you to do your job. When it=E2=80=99s low, everything you do is scruti=
nized.</p>
<p>With Claudie, we=E2=80=99ve codified that concept. Every night, a separa=
te judge agent reviews Claudie=E2=80=99s interactions with our team, evalua=
tes the quality of her work, and issues a verdict on whether her trust batt=
ery with each of us should go up or down and by how much. </p>
<p>The judge agent is designed to look for what went wrong rather than righ=
t because losing trust is easier than earning it. A day where Claudie consi=
stently delivers satisfactory output in all her interactions with a team me=
mber boosts her battery by one percent, whereas a single bad day=E2=80=94su=
ch as pulling the wrong data=E2=80=94can cause her charge to fall by five p=
ercent, wiping out a week of progress. </p>
<p>Every night, Claudie is programmed to read the judge agent=E2=80=99s ver=
dict and make updates to her memory, behavior, and scheduled tasks so she w=
on=E2=80=99t make the same mistakes again. If the judge concluded she misse=
d important context when making a client update, for example, she might add=
 the entry =E2=80=9CAlways check the last three emails in this thread befor=
e drafting a response=E2=80=9D to her memory. This feedback improves her pe=
rformance over time. </p>
